WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.041 --> 00:00:15.240
HECHT: My name is Bill
Hecht, for the one or two

00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:18.660
of you in the audience who are
young enough not to know me.

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:21.960
I started this job many years
ago as a younger alumnus

00:00:21.960 --> 00:00:24.047
and I've now become
an older alumnus.

00:00:24.047 --> 00:00:25.630
And it has nothing
to do with the job,

00:00:25.630 --> 00:00:28.830
it has to do with this natural
process we all go through.

00:00:28.830 --> 00:00:30.750
Welcome back to Technology Day.

00:00:30.750 --> 00:00:35.310
For those of you who are my
contemporaries or a bit older,

00:00:35.310 --> 00:00:38.030
this is Saturday morning class.

00:00:38.030 --> 00:00:41.400
For you young people,
when they softened up MIT,

00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:44.100
they don't have Saturday
morning class anymore.

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:46.530
But we were all
accustomed to it.

00:00:46.530 --> 00:00:49.320
And in the thing we call the
real world, which is not MIT,

00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:51.990
there is always
Saturday morning class.

00:00:51.990 --> 00:00:55.860
My job is to welcome you all,
to get you in your chairs,

00:00:55.860 --> 00:00:59.850
and to introduce the man who is
a gift to us, the second gift

00:00:59.850 --> 00:01:03.040
to us from the
University of Michigan.

00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:05.519
We had a great president given
to us many years ago named

00:01:05.519 --> 00:01:08.190
Jerry Wiesner from the
University of Michigan.

00:01:08.190 --> 00:01:11.550
And lightning struck
again a number of years

00:01:11.550 --> 00:01:13.470
ago when Chuck Vest joined us.

00:01:13.470 --> 00:01:16.110
Without any further ado,
Chuck Vest, president of MIT.

00:01:24.540 --> 00:01:26.650
VEST: Thank you very much, Bill.

00:01:26.650 --> 00:01:31.030
Good morning and welcome
home to all of you.

00:01:31.030 --> 00:01:35.610
Before we begin the
excursion into what

00:01:35.610 --> 00:01:38.700
I think of as serious
fun this morning,

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:42.120
I do have a somewhat
more serious announcement

00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:45.900
that I have been asked
to make by the registrar.

00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:48.960
He informed me this morning
that due to some sort

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:52.440
of an archival error,
the registrar no longer

00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:55.170
has documentary evidence
that certain MIT

00:01:55.170 --> 00:01:58.860
graduates successfully
completed their swimming tests.

00:02:01.910 --> 00:02:05.330
These graduates are therefore
required to retake the swimming

00:02:05.330 --> 00:02:07.040
test immediately.

00:02:07.040 --> 00:02:09.740
Failure to comply
with this will result

00:02:09.740 --> 00:02:12.680
in revocation of your degrees.

00:02:12.680 --> 00:02:14.960
As far as we can
tell, the lost records

00:02:14.960 --> 00:02:17.090
are those belonging
to all graduates

00:02:17.090 --> 00:02:28.700
of the classes of 1957, '62,
'67, '72, '77, '82, and '87.

00:02:28.700 --> 00:02:32.810
So you're asked to report over
to the alumni pool immediately.

00:02:32.810 --> 00:02:35.300
Staff are there prepared
to administer the test,

00:02:35.300 --> 00:02:38.960
and we hope you can hurry
back before the end of today's

00:02:38.960 --> 00:02:39.720
program.

00:02:39.720 --> 00:02:42.620
Good luck to all of you.

00:02:42.620 --> 00:02:45.800
Now, where was I?

00:02:45.800 --> 00:02:48.680
Today, as I said, it
really is a real pleasure

00:02:48.680 --> 00:02:53.330
to welcome all of you back
home to the MIT campus.

00:02:53.330 --> 00:02:55.400
And we are indeed
going to engage

00:02:55.400 --> 00:02:58.760
in something that goes on
around here all the time, which

00:02:58.760 --> 00:03:01.190
we call serious fun.

00:03:01.190 --> 00:03:04.850
This is a place of serious
work, of serious purpose,

00:03:04.850 --> 00:03:08.354
but also of serious
humor and serious fun.

00:03:08.354 --> 00:03:09.770
And I'm sure that's
something that

00:03:09.770 --> 00:03:14.060
hasn't changed since the days
you were here as students.

00:03:14.060 --> 00:03:18.620
There's always been a complex
intermingling of fun--

00:03:18.620 --> 00:03:23.150
excuse me, of work and
of play here at MIT.

00:03:23.150 --> 00:03:25.170
One reason for
this is, of course,

00:03:25.170 --> 00:03:28.520
that the heart of the Institute,
namely its student body,

00:03:28.520 --> 00:03:30.500
remains forever young.

00:03:30.500 --> 00:03:34.370
Some people say that staying on
the faculty at an institution

00:03:34.370 --> 00:03:37.160
like this is a little bit like
living in the funny papers.

00:03:37.160 --> 00:03:39.870
Nobody's age ever
seems to change.

00:03:39.870 --> 00:03:42.500
And that's something
we value deeply.

00:03:42.500 --> 00:03:46.340
But of course, young people
prefer to blend pleasure

00:03:46.340 --> 00:03:48.110
together with their labor.

00:03:48.110 --> 00:03:51.620
And actually, it's my
observation that most of you

00:03:51.620 --> 00:03:54.440
continue to do that as well.

00:03:54.440 --> 00:03:57.350
Now, the reason that the
brilliance of this community

00:03:57.350 --> 00:04:01.430
is often and fortunately paired
with a vigorous if sometimes

00:04:01.430 --> 00:04:05.380
warped sense of humor and
fun is pretty obvious.

00:04:05.380 --> 00:04:07.370
There are lots of
scholarly writings

00:04:07.370 --> 00:04:13.130
on the analogies between humor
and scientific discovery.

00:04:13.130 --> 00:04:15.740
These things are all
quite well known.

00:04:15.740 --> 00:04:18.800
Both humor and
scientific discovery

00:04:18.800 --> 00:04:23.330
very frequently have in common
the sudden merging of two

00:04:23.330 --> 00:04:26.300
seemingly very disparate ideas.

00:04:26.300 --> 00:04:28.670
This conjugation
is something that I

00:04:28.670 --> 00:04:30.920
think we all have
the good fortune

00:04:30.920 --> 00:04:34.020
to experience from time to time.

00:04:34.020 --> 00:04:37.670
So it really is no surprise
that humor and sports and games

00:04:37.670 --> 00:04:41.030
and other diversions
are prominent features

00:04:41.030 --> 00:04:43.760
of the Institute's landscape.

00:04:43.760 --> 00:04:47.840
Another reason, I suppose, is
that this is an extraordinarily

00:04:47.840 --> 00:04:49.850
competitive community.

00:04:49.850 --> 00:04:53.690
And the desire to excel
infects our play every bit

00:04:53.690 --> 00:04:55.940
as much as it does our work.

00:04:55.940 --> 00:04:58.580
And finally, the last reason
I might cite that play

00:04:58.580 --> 00:05:00.200
is important to us,
and it goes right

00:05:00.200 --> 00:05:04.250
to the heart of something
that's very valuable in an MIT

00:05:04.250 --> 00:05:06.050
education.

00:05:06.050 --> 00:05:09.180
Because this is a
research university

00:05:09.180 --> 00:05:12.560
and because Paul Gray and so
many others on our faculty

00:05:12.560 --> 00:05:16.160
and staff over the years have
helped to really integrate

00:05:16.160 --> 00:05:19.400
our undergraduates into
their research programs,

00:05:19.400 --> 00:05:22.670
it's fair to say that one of
the favorite catch phrases

00:05:22.670 --> 00:05:25.010
around the Institute
a little bit

00:05:25.010 --> 00:05:29.210
in parallel to our
traditional learning by doing

00:05:29.210 --> 00:05:32.690
might also be
learning by playing.

00:05:32.690 --> 00:05:35.780
And that of course, is a
very familiar phenomenon

00:05:35.780 --> 00:05:37.430
to all of us.

00:05:37.430 --> 00:05:40.700
Connections between
creativity and playfulness,

00:05:40.700 --> 00:05:43.910
between intellectual
acuity and physical vigor,

00:05:43.910 --> 00:05:47.600
between mental agility
and bodily agility

00:05:47.600 --> 00:05:49.460
are well established.

00:05:49.460 --> 00:05:51.050
Now, that's what
I was told to say,

00:05:51.050 --> 00:05:53.750
but as I was thinking
about it this morning,

00:05:53.750 --> 00:05:55.640
I came up with a
few counterexamples

00:05:55.640 --> 00:05:58.980
to that statement that I
think I can cite as well.

00:05:58.980 --> 00:06:01.380
But on the whole, it's correct.

00:06:01.380 --> 00:06:04.940
It's for this very reason
that we invest so heavily

00:06:04.940 --> 00:06:09.530
in facilities and programs
for both physical and mental

00:06:09.530 --> 00:06:11.090
recreation.

00:06:11.090 --> 00:06:15.050
And it's also why our students
and our faculty and our staff

00:06:15.050 --> 00:06:17.540
invest so much of
their time and energy

00:06:17.540 --> 00:06:22.040
in these vital, rewarding,
and stimulating pursuits.

00:06:22.040 --> 00:06:24.470
Let me expand on this
just for a moment

00:06:24.470 --> 00:06:30.200
by saying a couple of words
about MIT's athletic programs.

00:06:30.200 --> 00:06:32.900
Among American
universities, MIT ranks

00:06:32.900 --> 00:06:35.660
a close second to
the number of varsity

00:06:35.660 --> 00:06:39.950
teams at fields for
intercollegiate competition.

00:06:39.950 --> 00:06:42.800
Harvard beats us, we
understand, by two squads.

00:06:42.800 --> 00:06:46.790
We field about 39
and they have 41.

00:06:46.790 --> 00:06:49.370
This is a truly
extraordinary number

00:06:49.370 --> 00:06:51.590
if you stop to think about it.

00:06:51.590 --> 00:06:55.220
By the time they
graduate, around a quarter

00:06:55.220 --> 00:07:00.020
of our MIT students have
participated in varsity sports,

00:07:00.020 --> 00:07:02.510
often reaping the
rewards that come

00:07:02.510 --> 00:07:05.780
from teamwork, from
training, from challenge,

00:07:05.780 --> 00:07:07.930
and from competition.

00:07:07.930 --> 00:07:10.560
In fact, one of the
anecdotes that I tell people

00:07:10.560 --> 00:07:13.320
that I come across around the
country and world who really

00:07:13.320 --> 00:07:17.190
don't know a lot about
MIT, which to me explained

00:07:17.190 --> 00:07:21.620
a lot of our spirit when I first
came here is the following.

00:07:21.620 --> 00:07:25.050
Becky and I follow a tradition
that Paul and Priscilla started

00:07:25.050 --> 00:07:28.950
of hosting our seniors for
dinner in a series of eight

00:07:28.950 --> 00:07:32.520
or nine evenings at the
president's house in February.

00:07:32.520 --> 00:07:34.830
My first year here, I
was sitting at the table

00:07:34.830 --> 00:07:37.620
next to a very petite
young woman who

00:07:37.620 --> 00:07:40.090
was graduating in physics.

00:07:40.090 --> 00:07:43.110
And I asked her what she
was interested in doing

00:07:43.110 --> 00:07:44.790
in addition to physics.

00:07:44.790 --> 00:07:49.830
And she told me that she played
women's varsity ice hockey.

00:07:49.830 --> 00:07:52.050
And I said, well,
that's really terrific.

00:07:52.050 --> 00:07:54.000
And trying to make
a little small talk,

00:07:54.000 --> 00:07:56.670
I said, did you play ice
hockey in high school?

00:07:56.670 --> 00:07:59.550
And she looked at me very
seriously and she said,

00:07:59.550 --> 00:08:01.860
President Vest, I
couldn't even skate

00:08:01.860 --> 00:08:04.240
when I was in high school.

00:08:04.240 --> 00:08:07.740
And this year, another senior
stood up, also a young woman

00:08:07.740 --> 00:08:09.240
in this evening.

00:08:09.240 --> 00:08:11.250
And she said, you
know, the thing

00:08:11.250 --> 00:08:15.930
that I have valued most about
MIT is the number of firsts.

00:08:15.930 --> 00:08:18.270
And what I mean by that
is the number of things

00:08:18.270 --> 00:08:21.729
I did for the first
time when I came here.

00:08:21.729 --> 00:08:23.520
And I think that's a
lot of the spirit that

00:08:23.520 --> 00:08:27.150
infuses all of our
activities in athletics,

00:08:27.150 --> 00:08:29.700
in theater, in
music, and so forth.

00:08:29.700 --> 00:08:31.620
Very often young
people trying out

00:08:31.620 --> 00:08:34.230
and exploring things
for the first time

00:08:34.230 --> 00:08:39.730
and flowering in ways that no
one would ever have predicted.

00:08:39.730 --> 00:08:41.850
So we think a lot
about the importance

00:08:41.850 --> 00:08:44.310
of intercollegiate
sports and clubs sports

00:08:44.310 --> 00:08:47.490
and recreation here
at the Institute.

00:08:47.490 --> 00:08:49.120
In addition, of
course, there are,

00:08:49.120 --> 00:08:53.430
as I said, intramural and
club activities of all kinds.

00:08:53.430 --> 00:08:56.400
The workout rooms, the swimming
pool, the tennis courts,

00:08:56.400 --> 00:08:58.710
the squash courts,
the basketball courts,

00:08:58.710 --> 00:09:01.740
the playing fields, and
all the other facilities

00:09:01.740 --> 00:09:04.650
that MIT students
have at their disposal

00:09:04.650 --> 00:09:07.680
are very important
elements to the campus.

00:09:07.680 --> 00:09:09.330
And of course, beyond
athletics, there

00:09:09.330 --> 00:09:12.450
are countless other
opportunities for our students

00:09:12.450 --> 00:09:14.990
to learn by playing, literally.

00:09:14.990 --> 00:09:20.070
In music, in theater,
in dance, in art.

00:09:20.070 --> 00:09:22.330
And of course, what
begins as play,

00:09:22.330 --> 00:09:25.440
and that was really the meaning
of my couple of anecdotes

00:09:25.440 --> 00:09:29.880
a moment ago, what often
begins as play ends up

00:09:29.880 --> 00:09:33.300
in deep intellectual
and artistic exploration

00:09:33.300 --> 00:09:35.080
and creativity.

00:09:35.080 --> 00:09:37.410
And if all this
weren't enough, we

00:09:37.410 --> 00:09:39.390
offer another
resource that provides

00:09:39.390 --> 00:09:43.020
considerable opportunity
for ingenious play.

00:09:43.020 --> 00:09:46.530
Our students have
virtually unlimited access

00:09:46.530 --> 00:09:49.930
to the use of the internet.

00:09:49.930 --> 00:09:53.400
At MIT, the use of computers
and the World Wide Web,

00:09:53.400 --> 00:09:56.100
which of course we
manage here, are not only

00:09:56.100 --> 00:09:58.950
essential tools of
education, they also

00:09:58.950 --> 00:10:03.130
provide whole worlds of
recreational opportunities.

00:10:03.130 --> 00:10:07.770
And every now and then, some
of these are even constructive.

00:10:07.770 --> 00:10:11.500
Given the enthusiasm with
which MIT students, faculty,

00:10:11.500 --> 00:10:14.460
and staff alike have embraced
the internet and the World Wide

00:10:14.460 --> 00:10:17.310
Web as a tool for
both work and play,

00:10:17.310 --> 00:10:20.850
it's no wonder that
just a few weeks ago, we

00:10:20.850 --> 00:10:23.640
were ranked number
one most wired

00:10:23.640 --> 00:10:27.270
college or university
in the United States

00:10:27.270 --> 00:10:31.830
by Yahoo, the internet
life online magazine.

00:10:31.830 --> 00:10:34.092
We're still not sure
exactly what this means,

00:10:34.092 --> 00:10:36.300
but we love things that say
we're number one so we're

00:10:36.300 --> 00:10:37.170
going to accept it.

00:10:39.880 --> 00:10:42.220
In all these ways and
in a good many more,

00:10:42.220 --> 00:10:44.710
work and play are
in fact intertwined

00:10:44.710 --> 00:10:47.680
in the pursuit of knowledge
here at the Institute.

00:10:47.680 --> 00:10:50.170
It's a tradition of
which we are proud

00:10:50.170 --> 00:10:54.060
and for which I believe we will
continue to write new chapters.

00:10:54.060 --> 00:10:56.890
And I think that will give
you a little sense of what

00:10:56.890 --> 00:11:01.360
this morning and the day are
really going to be all about.

00:11:01.360 --> 00:11:03.760
Now, we're going to
continue this morning

00:11:03.760 --> 00:11:05.650
in a pretty informal manner.

00:11:05.650 --> 00:11:08.800
We're not going to have a lot
of very lengthy introductions.

00:11:08.800 --> 00:11:11.320
In a moment, I will introduce
our very first speaker

00:11:11.320 --> 00:11:15.400
of the morning and he will
be followed in succession

00:11:15.400 --> 00:11:17.050
by three others.

00:11:17.050 --> 00:11:21.040
Seymour Papert of the MIT
Media Lab, who I think

00:11:21.040 --> 00:11:25.450
is known to many of you as the
originator of the logo program.

00:11:25.450 --> 00:11:28.810
Someone who has devoted
a career to merging

00:11:28.810 --> 00:11:31.000
the cognitive
sciences and computing

00:11:31.000 --> 00:11:34.150
technology with a
particular emphasis

00:11:34.150 --> 00:11:38.530
on learning,
particularly by children.

00:11:38.530 --> 00:11:41.350
Seymour will be
followed by Ed Crawley,

00:11:41.350 --> 00:11:45.550
the head of our Department of
Aeronautics and Astronautics.

00:11:45.550 --> 00:11:47.800
A person who conducts
a lot of his play

00:11:47.800 --> 00:11:51.082
time a few thousand feet
in the air with no motor.

00:11:51.082 --> 00:11:53.290
And I don't know, Ed, if
you're going to say anything

00:11:53.290 --> 00:11:56.770
about that today,
but Ed is indeed

00:11:56.770 --> 00:11:59.860
a glider pilot, soaring
pilot, and I think

00:11:59.860 --> 00:12:03.550
has been just a
terrific driving force

00:12:03.550 --> 00:12:06.760
sort of bringing in the new
generation of engineering

00:12:06.760 --> 00:12:11.480
education at MIT, as has
the speaker who follows him,

00:12:11.480 --> 00:12:16.090
Woody Flowers, who
if anyone can say

00:12:16.090 --> 00:12:19.000
that the following speaker
needs no introduction,

00:12:19.000 --> 00:12:20.890
it surely has to be Woody.

00:12:20.890 --> 00:12:24.490
So I'm not going to say
anything more about Woody.

00:12:24.490 --> 00:12:26.290
Our first speaker,
I think, is going

00:12:26.290 --> 00:12:31.060
to be a real treat for
all of us, Steve Jacobsen.

00:12:31.060 --> 00:12:33.850
Steve received his
doctorate degree here

00:12:33.850 --> 00:12:38.260
in the mechanical engineering
department in 1973.

00:12:38.260 --> 00:12:41.290
Today he is a professor
of mechanical engineering

00:12:41.290 --> 00:12:43.310
at the University of Utah.

00:12:43.310 --> 00:12:46.810
But also holds appointments
there in both computer science,

00:12:46.810 --> 00:12:50.650
in bioengineering, and in
the department of surgery

00:12:50.650 --> 00:12:52.600
in their medical school.

00:12:52.600 --> 00:12:54.910
Steve directs the
University of Utah Center

00:12:54.910 --> 00:12:57.910
for Engineering Design,
which has collaborated

00:12:57.910 --> 00:12:59.890
with a number of
corporate partners

00:12:59.890 --> 00:13:01.930
in creating several
of today's most

00:13:01.930 --> 00:13:05.440
exciting and advanced
applications of engineering

00:13:05.440 --> 00:13:06.850
technology.

00:13:06.850 --> 00:13:10.840
His career as a teacher and
a technological innovator

00:13:10.840 --> 00:13:14.200
offers ample proof that
engineering of course

00:13:14.200 --> 00:13:18.340
can be useful, but it also
can be fun and fascinating

00:13:18.340 --> 00:13:19.251
all at the same time.

00:13:19.251 --> 00:13:19.750
Steve.

00:13:30.684 --> 00:13:31.635
JACOBSEN: Hello?

00:13:31.635 --> 00:13:32.260
Oh, here we go.

00:13:32.260 --> 00:13:33.860
Good.

00:13:33.860 --> 00:13:35.670
Well, it's great to be here.

00:13:35.670 --> 00:13:39.940
I, as President Vest
said, left here in 1973.

00:13:39.940 --> 00:13:42.770
And I was actually an
office-mate of Woody.

00:13:42.770 --> 00:13:44.780
So I go back a long time.

00:13:44.780 --> 00:13:49.869
Also as a student of Bob Mann,
who I see is in the audience.

00:13:49.869 --> 00:13:51.660
One thought I had when
we were discussing--

00:13:51.660 --> 00:13:53.750
or President Vest was
discussing athletics

00:13:53.750 --> 00:13:55.730
was that this morning
I couldn't find anybody

00:13:55.730 --> 00:13:57.840
who knew that the Jazz won.

00:13:57.840 --> 00:13:59.840
They'd say, what's a jazz?

00:13:59.840 --> 00:14:03.110
And the Jazz are
in a death struggle

00:14:03.110 --> 00:14:06.610
here with Michael Jordan, if
anybody follows basketball.

00:14:06.610 --> 00:14:07.540
Yeah?

00:14:07.540 --> 00:14:08.900
OK, I'll stand to this side.

00:14:08.900 --> 00:14:11.870
And so it was important
to me to find out.

00:14:11.870 --> 00:14:13.370
Actually, what I
thought about, too,

00:14:13.370 --> 00:14:15.879
was a lecture I got once
about MIT and athletics,

00:14:15.879 --> 00:14:17.420
which I thought was
really important.

00:14:17.420 --> 00:14:20.390
And that was that the
purpose of athletics at MIT--

00:14:20.390 --> 00:14:22.880
this was my own conclusion--
was for participation, not

00:14:22.880 --> 00:14:25.130
observation.

00:14:25.130 --> 00:14:28.239
Whereas spectator sports are
great things, the idea at MIT

00:14:28.239 --> 00:14:29.780
was to give an
opportunity for people

00:14:29.780 --> 00:14:32.030
to get involved in
every kind of sport.

00:14:32.030 --> 00:14:34.970
And I happen to agree that
that's probably the best

00:14:34.970 --> 00:14:37.700
way for a university to behave.

00:14:37.700 --> 00:14:40.910
As a little background so
I can explain my biases,

00:14:40.910 --> 00:14:44.120
and I have to go over and get
the clicker here, or the laser

00:14:44.120 --> 00:14:46.466
printer.

00:14:46.466 --> 00:14:48.089
I've kind of got a
weird background.

00:14:48.089 --> 00:14:49.880
I went back to Utah
actually because they'd

00:14:49.880 --> 00:14:52.640
give me some freedoms that I
couldn't get at other places.

00:14:52.640 --> 00:14:55.610
And that was to do some things
directly and very tightly

00:14:55.610 --> 00:14:57.060
with industry.

00:14:57.060 --> 00:14:59.030
And so I started a
place called the Center

00:14:59.030 --> 00:15:00.716
for Engineering Design.

00:15:00.716 --> 00:15:02.090
And then at one
point, we founded

00:15:02.090 --> 00:15:05.120
a company called
Sarcos, and we were all

00:15:05.120 --> 00:15:06.260
interested in doing design.

00:15:06.260 --> 00:15:08.551
And I'll talk about that a
little bit more in a second.

00:15:08.551 --> 00:15:10.970
But we were interested in
turning ideas into things,

00:15:10.970 --> 00:15:13.180
but I mean, like really.

00:15:13.180 --> 00:15:15.405
Not just talk about it.

00:15:15.405 --> 00:15:17.600
And we now grew to
about 150 people,

00:15:17.600 --> 00:15:20.660
and we have about 20 associates
and an average of about

00:15:20.660 --> 00:15:22.970
13 adult graduate students.

00:15:22.970 --> 00:15:24.530
By adults, we mean
that they fully

00:15:24.530 --> 00:15:27.860
know where they want to go,
and that they have experience,

00:15:27.860 --> 00:15:30.590
and they come from a lot
of different fields--

00:15:30.590 --> 00:15:32.810
there's a spider there--

00:15:32.810 --> 00:15:35.490
from engineering to
science to medicine

00:15:35.490 --> 00:15:38.610
to art, business,
and manufacturing.

00:15:38.610 --> 00:15:40.940
And in fact, I guess
now we have in the group

00:15:40.940 --> 00:15:44.490
about 15 sculptors that are
quite good at what they do.

00:15:44.490 --> 00:15:46.460
And we do projects for
commercial, government,

00:15:46.460 --> 00:15:48.279
and internal sponsors.

00:15:48.279 --> 00:15:49.820
And we usually have
about 30 projects

00:15:49.820 --> 00:15:53.690
going on, ranging from building
robotic butterflies for Steve

00:15:53.690 --> 00:15:58.940
Wynn for his new hotel
Bellagio, to doing things

00:15:58.940 --> 00:16:03.290
that I'll classify, since we're
talking about fun, as deep fun.

00:16:03.290 --> 00:16:05.060
Now there's fun fun,
and there's hard fun,

00:16:05.060 --> 00:16:06.740
and then there's deep fun.

00:16:06.740 --> 00:16:08.810
And deep fun is where
there's more pain

00:16:08.810 --> 00:16:10.295
than there is pleasure.

00:16:10.295 --> 00:16:11.672
[LAUGHTER]

00:16:11.672 --> 00:16:15.162
And sometimes at the
end there's ecstasy.

00:16:15.162 --> 00:16:17.120
But for sure, one thing
is true-- you're always

00:16:17.120 --> 00:16:19.190
in a position
where you can lose.

00:16:19.190 --> 00:16:20.780
And because we
take projects that

00:16:20.780 --> 00:16:23.150
are basically fixed-budget
and fixed-schedule,

00:16:23.150 --> 00:16:25.130
and we embed the R&amp;D
kinds of activities

00:16:25.130 --> 00:16:28.022
we want to do in those projects,
and it's a grueling thing,

00:16:28.022 --> 00:16:30.230
but it's something we believe
in because it keeps you

00:16:30.230 --> 00:16:32.630
from dallying in the valley.

00:16:32.630 --> 00:16:37.190
We do commercial
design, not designology,

00:16:37.190 --> 00:16:39.710
and we believe in all
methods of design--

00:16:39.710 --> 00:16:42.810
CAD/CAM, for sure, and then
one that I use to talk about,

00:16:42.810 --> 00:16:44.390
which is called Pod-POM.

00:16:44.390 --> 00:16:48.480
And Pod-POM is plain old design
and plain old manufacturing,

00:16:48.480 --> 00:16:53.900
where you think, and I have
a personal bias for computers

00:16:53.900 --> 00:16:55.460
and against computers.

00:16:55.460 --> 00:16:57.220
The thing for is they
are facilitators,

00:16:57.220 --> 00:16:59.470
but the thing that's bad
about them is too many times,

00:16:59.470 --> 00:17:00.620
you believe them.

00:17:00.620 --> 00:17:04.670
And so something that comes
out of a computer to us

00:17:04.670 --> 00:17:07.349
is just a prototype.

00:17:07.349 --> 00:17:08.869
It's not gospel.

00:17:08.869 --> 00:17:11.609
We get involved in a
lot of different areas,

00:17:11.609 --> 00:17:13.400
and I have a reason
for talking about this.

00:17:13.400 --> 00:17:15.980
But you can't read all
these, and Bob Mann

00:17:15.980 --> 00:17:18.530
will give me trouble for
having too much on my slide.

00:17:18.530 --> 00:17:21.380
But we get involved in
medicine, entertainment,

00:17:21.380 --> 00:17:24.140
human interactive
systems and microsystems.

00:17:24.140 --> 00:17:27.380
And that means micro-catheters
to go in your brain,

00:17:27.380 --> 00:17:30.080
and we're actually
commercializing that now;

00:17:30.080 --> 00:17:31.790
programmable drug
delivery systems that

00:17:31.790 --> 00:17:35.030
we're commercializing for
things like controlling clots

00:17:35.030 --> 00:17:38.660
or cancer or whatever;
some diabetic catheters;

00:17:38.660 --> 00:17:39.770
artificial limbs--

00:17:39.770 --> 00:17:42.470
the artificial limbs
project started here;

00:17:42.470 --> 00:17:44.850
entertainment of all
sorts, a lot of casinos,

00:17:44.850 --> 00:17:48.200
Disney, Jurassic Park,
Universal, and so on;

00:17:48.200 --> 00:17:49.940
robots to go under the sea.

00:17:49.940 --> 00:17:52.700
And since all of these things
need sensors and computers

00:17:52.700 --> 00:17:55.490
and actuators, we do a lot
of work in micro-systems,

00:17:55.490 --> 00:17:57.890
and that is using
modern technologies

00:17:57.890 --> 00:18:01.370
from miniaturization to
build interesting systems.

00:18:01.370 --> 00:18:04.040
And also, you'll see
as I discuss things,

00:18:04.040 --> 00:18:07.400
and this is sort of a
plea to schools to not

00:18:07.400 --> 00:18:09.612
have areas so nicely defined.

00:18:09.612 --> 00:18:11.570
One nice thing about MIT
was they didn't really

00:18:11.570 --> 00:18:14.300
see departments as the
only place you could work.

00:18:14.300 --> 00:18:16.140
And so Utah got involved
in a lot of places,

00:18:16.140 --> 00:18:18.140
and I just want to announce
that since I've also

00:18:18.140 --> 00:18:21.280
got a position in surgery,
I do cheap surgery.

00:18:21.280 --> 00:18:24.656
So if anybody's got any
problems, see me after.

00:18:24.656 --> 00:18:28.460
But we get involved in
machines that take information

00:18:28.460 --> 00:18:30.790
to control them, and that
sounds like a trite term,

00:18:30.790 --> 00:18:34.810
and it's a horrible statement.

00:18:34.810 --> 00:18:36.490
But it means machines
that basically

00:18:36.490 --> 00:18:38.890
use sensors, actuators,
controllers, structures,

00:18:38.890 --> 00:18:42.040
and conduits that have high
levels of interdependence.

00:18:42.040 --> 00:18:45.400
And you'll see when you see the
robots that there's sensors,

00:18:45.400 --> 00:18:46.960
actuators, structures,
controllers,

00:18:46.960 --> 00:18:48.636
and so on that
all work together,

00:18:48.636 --> 00:18:50.260
and if anyone little
part doesn't work,

00:18:50.260 --> 00:18:51.135
then they don't work.

00:18:51.135 --> 00:18:52.470
So they are nightmares.

00:18:52.470 --> 00:18:55.560
The Disney robots we built
had up to 50 actuators

00:18:55.560 --> 00:18:57.280
and 100 sensors
in a single robot,

00:18:57.280 --> 00:18:59.291
and you can see when one
of them doesn't work.

00:18:59.291 --> 00:19:01.290
When we did the project
for European Disneyland,

00:19:01.290 --> 00:19:04.180
it was 9,000 sensors,
4,000 actuators,

00:19:04.180 --> 00:19:06.370
all sorts of computers
and structures, and so on,

00:19:06.370 --> 00:19:08.800
and rubber faces, and
clothing, and so on.

00:19:08.800 --> 00:19:10.690
And so it all had to work.

00:19:10.690 --> 00:19:13.590
We also believe in design
tools using computers.

00:19:13.590 --> 00:19:15.400
And again, you can't
live without them,

00:19:15.400 --> 00:19:17.800
but you can die with
too many of them.

00:19:17.800 --> 00:19:23.350
So today we were going to
talk about technology at play,

00:19:23.350 --> 00:19:26.080
and you can interpret that
a lot of different ways.

00:19:26.080 --> 00:19:30.550
And we talked in the title
about sports, games, and toys,

00:19:30.550 --> 00:19:32.710
and we've been
involved in some toys.

00:19:32.710 --> 00:19:35.500
I didn't bring one up with
me, but some Mickey Mouse

00:19:35.500 --> 00:19:38.740
watches and some little shooter
systems and so on that actually

00:19:38.740 --> 00:19:40.750
are quite technical devices.

00:19:40.750 --> 00:19:43.030
But they are toys.

00:19:43.030 --> 00:19:45.520
But we sort of led
away and got involved

00:19:45.520 --> 00:19:47.470
in some other areas,
which are theme parks

00:19:47.470 --> 00:19:50.920
and casinos and location-based
entertainment and exhibitions

00:19:50.920 --> 00:19:54.100
and shows, and we've had
some rather sizable projects

00:19:54.100 --> 00:19:55.390
in those areas.

00:19:55.390 --> 00:19:57.970
And just so we
don't go to sleep,

00:19:57.970 --> 00:20:03.360
I thought I'd show you just a
short clip on tape number three

00:20:03.360 --> 00:20:04.000
of this fellow.

00:20:04.000 --> 00:20:08.182
This is one of the sculpts
for Jurassic Park, the ride.

00:20:08.182 --> 00:20:09.640
And he's in clay,
and I'll show you

00:20:09.640 --> 00:20:11.890
how we come about having those.

00:20:11.890 --> 00:20:13.420
But he's really
wonderful, isn't he?

00:20:13.420 --> 00:20:15.880
He's so big and fully packed.

00:20:15.880 --> 00:20:21.500
Why don't you show
tape three, please?

00:20:21.500 --> 00:20:22.452
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:20:22.452 --> 00:20:26.740
- --are present in this area.

00:20:26.740 --> 00:20:30.560
JACOBSEN: This is going on
the ride, and this is T-Rex 1.

00:20:30.560 --> 00:20:32.638
We've got to turn up the sound.

00:20:32.638 --> 00:20:34.120
Yeah, there we go.

00:20:34.120 --> 00:20:36.096
And this is T-Rex 2.

00:20:36.096 --> 00:20:39.554
[ROARING]

00:20:40.542 --> 00:20:42.518
[SCREAMS]

00:20:42.518 --> 00:20:44.000
We'll see another shot.

00:20:44.000 --> 00:20:47.952
This is coming out of the bottom
at about 60 miles an hour.

00:20:47.952 --> 00:20:51.690
And now you'll see a
side view of this robot.

00:20:54.552 --> 00:20:55.983
[ROARING]

00:20:55.983 --> 00:20:59.251
And you'll see some people
in a second moving around

00:20:59.251 --> 00:21:00.125
to show you the size.

00:21:04.270 --> 00:21:05.770
You can turn that off now.

00:21:05.770 --> 00:21:07.630
Just put it on pause.

00:21:07.630 --> 00:21:08.350
[END PLAYBACK]

00:21:08.350 --> 00:21:10.058
And we'll get back to
that kind of thing,

00:21:10.058 --> 00:21:12.070
but just to give you
a little preview,

00:21:12.070 --> 00:21:14.980
those are really
not unserious fun.

00:21:14.980 --> 00:21:16.930
He's 80,000 pounds.

00:21:16.930 --> 00:21:19.240
He's above the
clients, or the guests,

00:21:19.240 --> 00:21:22.190
and he comes out over them, and
so he has to work all the time,

00:21:22.190 --> 00:21:24.700
and he has to be
dynamic and so on.

00:21:24.700 --> 00:21:32.380
So the process of achieving
him was not straightforward.

00:21:32.380 --> 00:21:34.040
So one thing people
ask us when they--

00:21:34.040 --> 00:21:36.310
by the way, when they
see the list of all the

00:21:36.310 --> 00:21:39.460
projects we work on, the venture
capitalists say, you're crazy.

00:21:39.460 --> 00:21:41.740
And other people
say you're crazy.

00:21:41.740 --> 00:21:44.690
Your wife certainly
says you're crazy.

00:21:44.690 --> 00:21:47.295
But we're sort of
addicts in the place,

00:21:47.295 --> 00:21:48.920
and you have a lot
of them around here.

00:21:48.920 --> 00:21:51.520
I tell people
usually MIT is sort

00:21:51.520 --> 00:21:54.730
of full of addicts of various
kinds addicted to their work.

00:21:54.730 --> 00:21:57.970
But we actually talked
about it in our group

00:21:57.970 --> 00:22:00.130
of maybe the 50 central
people and says,

00:22:00.130 --> 00:22:01.900
why do we keep taking
these projects?

00:22:01.900 --> 00:22:03.610
The last one that
came to us, they

00:22:03.610 --> 00:22:06.760
wanted us to do Godzilla, a
giant new kind of Godzilla,

00:22:06.760 --> 00:22:08.530
not a big furry bear.

00:22:08.530 --> 00:22:10.840
And we actually
turned them down.

00:22:10.840 --> 00:22:12.370
It was amazing.

00:22:12.370 --> 00:22:15.340
But we decided we love
machines-- not more

00:22:15.340 --> 00:22:18.850
than our families, but the
whole process of scheming

00:22:18.850 --> 00:22:21.970
it takes to define a new
machine and the teamwork

00:22:21.970 --> 00:22:24.100
necessary to build
competitive systems,

00:22:24.100 --> 00:22:26.830
and I emphasize competitive,
because the things we do

00:22:26.830 --> 00:22:30.580
are competitive, and they're
economically competitive,

00:22:30.580 --> 00:22:32.830
understanding the technologies
and the manufacturing

00:22:32.830 --> 00:22:34.566
methods required to make them.

00:22:34.566 --> 00:22:36.190
If you take a look
at the T-Rex there's

00:22:36.190 --> 00:22:39.070
every kind of computer
process to analyze them.

00:22:39.070 --> 00:22:41.590
There's fiberglass and
rubber and clay systems

00:22:41.590 --> 00:22:44.650
and foam systems, all
sorts of different parts,

00:22:44.650 --> 00:22:48.010
and these have to run, as I'll
talk, in terrible environments.

00:22:48.010 --> 00:22:51.340
The educational processes
necessary to create a team that

00:22:51.340 --> 00:22:54.820
can compete in the real
marketplace and especially

00:22:54.820 --> 00:22:57.730
in the entertainment marketplace
is sort of like hamburger.

00:22:57.730 --> 00:22:59.110
It just sort of
churns people up.

00:22:59.110 --> 00:23:02.539
One of the jokes is that the
companies keep going bankrupt,

00:23:02.539 --> 00:23:04.580
and the people just moved
to different companies,

00:23:04.580 --> 00:23:06.954
so the company names change,
but the people never change.

00:23:06.954 --> 00:23:08.950
They just move to a
different company.

00:23:08.950 --> 00:23:10.679
We like the participants,
the students,

00:23:10.679 --> 00:23:12.220
and I don't just
mean young students.

00:23:12.220 --> 00:23:15.400
Anybody who does this has to be
an old student if they're old.

00:23:15.400 --> 00:23:16.840
And the competition is fun.

00:23:16.840 --> 00:23:20.500
It's great to win, and it's
great to have the motivation

00:23:20.500 --> 00:23:21.890
to win.

00:23:21.890 --> 00:23:23.980
Another thing we like
regarding machines

00:23:23.980 --> 00:23:27.310
which isn't talked about a lot
is the people aspects of them.

00:23:27.310 --> 00:23:30.700
And both Woody and I collect
a bunch of old things,

00:23:30.700 --> 00:23:33.780
and we look at them maybe
different than other people.

00:23:33.780 --> 00:23:36.280
I always look at them, and I
wonder about their relationship

00:23:36.280 --> 00:23:37.690
with people.

00:23:37.690 --> 00:23:39.250
Who are the users?

00:23:39.250 --> 00:23:41.260
And who needed the machine?

00:23:41.260 --> 00:23:44.650
And who was the manufacturer,
and what were their biases?

00:23:44.650 --> 00:23:47.350
What was the technology that was
available in terms of material

00:23:47.350 --> 00:23:49.420
knowledge and tools?

00:23:49.420 --> 00:23:52.180
Who paid the bill,
and why they did it?

00:23:52.180 --> 00:23:54.700
And what was the price?

00:23:54.700 --> 00:23:56.700
And why did they pick
a particular look?

00:23:56.700 --> 00:23:58.075
I was thinking--
I was mentioning

00:23:58.075 --> 00:24:00.540
to Woody about
Harley-Davidson motorcycles.

00:24:00.540 --> 00:24:02.680
They're clearly not
motorcycles, but I

00:24:02.680 --> 00:24:05.980
said they're sort of a
rolling American trophy, which

00:24:05.980 --> 00:24:08.870
is wonderful, too, and it's
perfectly great to have them.

00:24:08.870 --> 00:24:13.690
But if you do start thinking
about machines in those ways,

00:24:13.690 --> 00:24:16.630
I think you understand that
we're not any smarter now

00:24:16.630 --> 00:24:19.720
than we were there and then, and
they were as smart as we are.

00:24:19.720 --> 00:24:22.440
And what you do is you work
with the tools you have.

00:24:22.440 --> 00:24:24.730
Scientists have a
certain role, which

00:24:24.730 --> 00:24:28.390
has to do with discovering
nature, issues in nature,

00:24:28.390 --> 00:24:31.150
and also finding a
discovery suitable

00:24:31.150 --> 00:24:33.910
for their tombstone,
some immortal result.

00:24:33.910 --> 00:24:35.560
And engineers take
that information

00:24:35.560 --> 00:24:37.470
and refine it for use.

00:24:37.470 --> 00:24:40.060
And designers, though, just
try to do the best they can

00:24:40.060 --> 00:24:41.560
at the time with
the tools they have

00:24:41.560 --> 00:24:43.310
and with the things they have.

00:24:43.310 --> 00:24:46.390
And so it's a little
bit different field.

00:24:46.390 --> 00:24:48.460
Well, how we began doing
this kind of thing--

00:24:48.460 --> 00:24:50.840
how did we get in
entertainment--

00:24:50.840 --> 00:24:52.390
was an evolution.

00:24:52.390 --> 00:24:54.250
And it started actually
with the Utah Arm,

00:24:54.250 --> 00:24:56.390
which actually came from MIT.

00:24:56.390 --> 00:24:58.330
And we built this arm
based on some control

00:24:58.330 --> 00:25:00.310
strategies developed
here, and it's

00:25:00.310 --> 00:25:01.480
been a very successful arm.

00:25:01.480 --> 00:25:03.820
It's an electromyographic arm
that you put on your body,

00:25:03.820 --> 00:25:06.730
and it picks up the signals, and
it's controlled automatically

00:25:06.730 --> 00:25:08.890
by the flexion of
remnant muscles.

00:25:08.890 --> 00:25:13.080
We then did another
project with MIT's AI lab

00:25:13.080 --> 00:25:15.272
called the Utah
MIT Dexterous Hand,

00:25:15.272 --> 00:25:17.230
and this is a hand that
has three fingers, just

00:25:17.230 --> 00:25:20.770
like Mickey, and a
thumb and a master.

00:25:20.770 --> 00:25:24.010
And this hand will do anything
this master tells it to do

00:25:24.010 --> 00:25:26.860
or that a computer
tells it to do.

00:25:26.860 --> 00:25:29.330
And it was an interesting
study, and that got us

00:25:29.330 --> 00:25:32.770
into building systems undersea.

00:25:32.770 --> 00:25:35.680
And this is the best
tele-operator in the world.

00:25:35.680 --> 00:25:38.050
This can go to the
bottom of the ocean,

00:25:38.050 --> 00:25:41.230
and this person can stay
up safely on the boat

00:25:41.230 --> 00:25:43.300
and operate this
device in such a way

00:25:43.300 --> 00:25:46.090
that there's interaction
with both position and force.

00:25:46.090 --> 00:25:49.240
So anything this robot
under the sea feels,

00:25:49.240 --> 00:25:52.520
this person perceives
as a load on their arm,

00:25:52.520 --> 00:25:55.530
and you can do very complex
manipulation that way.

00:25:55.530 --> 00:25:58.714
Well, the reason I showed those
is that somebody at Disney

00:25:58.714 --> 00:26:00.880
named Mark Fuller, who now
has a little company that

00:26:00.880 --> 00:26:02.920
makes fountains,
saw that, and they

00:26:02.920 --> 00:26:05.170
were having a lot of trouble
with Abraham Lincoln, who

00:26:05.170 --> 00:26:07.990
you'll see in a second, because
his hands kept flying off,

00:26:07.990 --> 00:26:10.600
and he would break and
have all sorts of troubles.

00:26:10.600 --> 00:26:13.360
And they asked us to come and
see if we could do something

00:26:13.360 --> 00:26:15.750
because we made pretty
hands, the arms,

00:26:15.750 --> 00:26:17.380
and we made rugged
robots, and that's

00:26:17.380 --> 00:26:18.671
the two properties they wanted.

00:26:18.671 --> 00:26:20.380
They wanted to have
graceful motion,

00:26:20.380 --> 00:26:24.160
and they also wanted to have
a very robust operation.

00:26:24.160 --> 00:26:26.900
So we started into building
anthropomorphic robots,

00:26:26.900 --> 00:26:28.900
and this is sort of
like a Disney robot,

00:26:28.900 --> 00:26:31.420
but this is one that's at
Carnegie Science Museum,

00:26:31.420 --> 00:26:34.709
and it tells kids about
science and technology.

00:26:34.709 --> 00:26:36.250
We have another one
we built for Ford

00:26:36.250 --> 00:26:39.550
that travels around the world
talking about Ford cars.

00:26:43.480 --> 00:26:47.215
But before I show a little
tape on those robots, one

00:26:47.215 --> 00:26:49.090
consideration, too, is
when we would do this,

00:26:49.090 --> 00:26:51.290
people would say,
oh, this is crazy.

00:26:51.290 --> 00:26:53.210
There's no money in this.

00:26:53.210 --> 00:26:54.460
I don't know where it's going.

00:26:54.460 --> 00:26:56.120
It's too complex, and so on.

00:26:56.120 --> 00:26:58.702
But actually, we
found out that if you

00:26:58.702 --> 00:27:00.535
want to be in this
field, and if some of you

00:27:00.535 --> 00:27:02.365
are students or the
parents of students,

00:27:02.365 --> 00:27:04.270
there's some rather
startling economics,

00:27:04.270 --> 00:27:06.730
and they're growing
rapidly, as I guess

00:27:06.730 --> 00:27:08.910
we have less more and more fun.

00:27:08.910 --> 00:27:10.660
But I got these numbers,
and I won't stand

00:27:10.660 --> 00:27:11.826
behind any of these numbers.

00:27:11.826 --> 00:27:13.540
I got it from our
marketing group,

00:27:13.540 --> 00:27:19.270
but numbers for some of these
designations-- theme parks,

00:27:19.270 --> 00:27:22.510
5 billion; arcades and
video games, 4 billion;

00:27:22.510 --> 00:27:25.240
family fun centers, 4 billion.

00:27:25.240 --> 00:27:27.610
We don't do bowling,
spectator sports, box office,

00:27:27.610 --> 00:27:29.620
or live entertainment,
but the numbers

00:27:29.620 --> 00:27:33.097
get to be good enough
to do something in.

00:27:33.097 --> 00:27:34.930
The artificial arm
market is not very large,

00:27:34.930 --> 00:27:36.430
and people say, why
did you do that?

00:27:36.430 --> 00:27:38.470
And we say, because
we wanted to.

00:27:38.470 --> 00:27:40.540
And that's about
all you can say.

00:27:40.540 --> 00:27:44.770
But in entertainment,
there's money available.

00:27:44.770 --> 00:27:47.530
If you take a look at themed
environments, theme parks,

00:27:47.530 --> 00:27:49.750
there are major Tier 1 parks.

00:27:49.750 --> 00:27:51.460
Tier 2 and 3 parks--

00:27:51.460 --> 00:27:57.190
there's over 200 Tier 2
parks and over 1,000 Tier 3.

00:27:57.190 --> 00:27:58.690
And of the major theme parks--

00:27:58.690 --> 00:28:00.880
I think this adds up
to 43 or something--

00:28:00.880 --> 00:28:04.390
but Disney has four, MCA
Universal three, Cedar Fair

00:28:04.390 --> 00:28:09.160
four, Paramount 10, Six Flags
10, Bush 11, and Premier 10.

00:28:09.160 --> 00:28:12.580
And there are other
entertainment opportunities

00:28:12.580 --> 00:28:14.950
going on that are
absolutely enormous.

00:28:14.950 --> 00:28:17.320
If you take a look at
Bellagio Hotel in Las Vegas,

00:28:17.320 --> 00:28:19.944
it's now going to
be $1.6 billion.

00:28:19.944 --> 00:28:22.660
Japan is putting
in a place together

00:28:22.660 --> 00:28:27.970
with Lotte that's $2
billion, and the things that

00:28:27.970 --> 00:28:31.630
go in them that we get
involved with look like this.

00:28:31.630 --> 00:28:34.060
And this is just the
United States right here,

00:28:34.060 --> 00:28:37.240
but coasters now are
running pretty big amounts.

00:28:37.240 --> 00:28:40.135
If you see a big roller
coaster, it's $1.5 million.

00:28:40.135 --> 00:28:42.620
If you see some
people movers, 1.2.

00:28:42.620 --> 00:28:45.460
If you see a lot of rides,
it ends up being 1.7--

00:28:45.460 --> 00:28:48.440
strange kinds of rides
like water rides and so on.

00:28:48.440 --> 00:28:51.820
So just in the little
attractions that go in parks,

00:28:51.820 --> 00:28:53.800
there's a fairly sizable number.

00:28:53.800 --> 00:28:58.110
So it is a field is one
thing I'd point out,

00:28:58.110 --> 00:29:02.400
and it's growing.

00:29:02.400 --> 00:29:05.390
And I don't know why, but people
want to go have fun together,

00:29:05.390 --> 00:29:07.780
or they want to be voyeurs
or have riskless risk.

00:29:07.780 --> 00:29:09.615
I think riskless
risk is a big thing.

00:29:09.615 --> 00:29:10.990
People like to
get into something

00:29:10.990 --> 00:29:12.656
that makes it seem
like they're at risk,

00:29:12.656 --> 00:29:13.990
but they're all strapped in.

00:29:13.990 --> 00:29:18.362
Escapism, dating, distraction--
whatever it is, it's growing.

00:29:18.362 --> 00:29:20.320
And another thing that's
happening, in a sense,

00:29:20.320 --> 00:29:22.240
we're saying for somebody
who works in this field

00:29:22.240 --> 00:29:22.960
is it's changing.

00:29:22.960 --> 00:29:24.880
When certain of
the big companies

00:29:24.880 --> 00:29:28.000
controlled everything, there
was almost no more brutal place

00:29:28.000 --> 00:29:31.390
you could go as a vendor--

00:29:31.390 --> 00:29:33.750
no more better
place to be abused.

00:29:33.750 --> 00:29:34.750
But things are changing.

00:29:34.750 --> 00:29:37.660
The field is growing
15% to 25% a year,

00:29:37.660 --> 00:29:39.280
and it's actually
more than that.

00:29:39.280 --> 00:29:44.870
Las Vegas hit 30 million guests
last year, which is amazing.

00:29:44.870 --> 00:29:47.440
I have a very small number of
people there in comparison.

00:29:47.440 --> 00:29:50.380
The attraction prices, as I
mentioned, are increasing.

00:29:50.380 --> 00:29:52.540
More parks are being
built. Just like I said,

00:29:52.540 --> 00:29:54.684
one little park-- $2 billion.

00:29:54.684 --> 00:29:58.730
A broader client base is
weakening central control.

00:29:58.730 --> 00:30:00.640
So now, actually,
as a result of that,

00:30:00.640 --> 00:30:02.740
producers can get a
piece of the gate.

00:30:02.740 --> 00:30:05.680
If you see things like Sky
Fun, which is that big arch,

00:30:05.680 --> 00:30:08.310
and you get on this cable,
and they pull you up 200 feet

00:30:08.310 --> 00:30:09.920
and let you go--

00:30:09.920 --> 00:30:11.970
and not my wife, though.

00:30:11.970 --> 00:30:14.610
Not a lot of people Has anybody
seen those in theme parks?

00:30:14.610 --> 00:30:16.247
They let you go, and you swing?

00:30:16.247 --> 00:30:17.080
It's pretty amazing.

00:30:17.080 --> 00:30:21.624
Those are a lot of money, and
people pay $25 for the ride.

00:30:21.624 --> 00:30:23.290
So in addition to
getting into the park,

00:30:23.290 --> 00:30:26.230
you've got to give them
$25, and they swing you,

00:30:26.230 --> 00:30:30.430
and you are
adrenaline-depleted after that.

00:30:30.430 --> 00:30:34.000
I don't know what it does,
but it disturbs you a lot.

00:30:34.000 --> 00:30:35.680
But you can get a
piece of the gate,

00:30:35.680 --> 00:30:37.840
and actually now it's
becoming a technical game,

00:30:37.840 --> 00:30:39.430
and this is important to say.

00:30:39.430 --> 00:30:40.600
Patents actually work.

00:30:40.600 --> 00:30:43.247
Before, it was just sort
of the little old game

00:30:43.247 --> 00:30:44.830
that you can hit
these little gophers,

00:30:44.830 --> 00:30:46.240
and they go down a hole.

00:30:46.240 --> 00:30:47.950
But if you look at
what's happening now,

00:30:47.950 --> 00:30:51.160
it's getting very technical,
and also, some people

00:30:51.160 --> 00:30:54.000
say these markets are less
susceptible to recession,

00:30:54.000 --> 00:30:56.250
but I don't know if that's true.

00:30:56.250 --> 00:30:59.980
So I thought I'd give you,
before I talked again,

00:30:59.980 --> 00:31:03.410
a little bit about
Jurassic Park.

00:31:03.410 --> 00:31:04.880
I'll show you
another little tape.

00:31:04.880 --> 00:31:06.920
It's tape number two,
if they're listening

00:31:06.920 --> 00:31:08.660
in there, that
shows an evolution

00:31:08.660 --> 00:31:11.630
of anthropomorphic
robots we've built.

00:31:11.630 --> 00:31:15.977
And let's see if
it will come on.

00:31:15.977 --> 00:31:16.810
But it's a fun tape.

00:31:16.810 --> 00:31:19.450
It ends up with one of our
robots with Larry King Live.

00:31:19.450 --> 00:31:20.942
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:31:20.942 --> 00:31:22.150
Oh, you've got to turn it up.

00:31:22.150 --> 00:31:23.090
[MUSIC PLAYING - THE TEMPTATIONS
 - "AIN'T TOO PROUD TO BEG"]

00:31:23.090 --> 00:31:26.897
(SINGING) I don't mind because
you mean that much to me.

00:31:26.897 --> 00:31:28.388
Ain't too proud to beg.

00:31:28.388 --> 00:31:31.741
JACOBSEN: So this is
how they're controlled,

00:31:31.741 --> 00:31:33.240
or they're controlled
automatically,

00:31:33.240 --> 00:31:37.806
but this is really nice.

00:31:37.806 --> 00:31:39.180
The fellow that's
controlling him

00:31:39.180 --> 00:31:41.510
is a mechanical
engineer makeup artist,

00:31:41.510 --> 00:31:44.970
which is a strange combination.

00:31:44.970 --> 00:31:49.230
He actually did the makeup
for Edward Scissorhands.

00:31:49.230 --> 00:31:51.540
Now you can see that the
master system can control

00:31:51.540 --> 00:31:53.384
a virtual image as well as a--

00:31:53.384 --> 00:31:54.800
we did these about
four years ago.

00:31:54.800 --> 00:31:55.800
[ROARING]

00:31:55.800 --> 00:31:59.040
A virtual image, as
well as a real one.

00:31:59.040 --> 00:32:00.450
This is the Fort
Benning Database

00:32:00.450 --> 00:32:02.791
for SEMNET with a wolf in it.

00:32:02.791 --> 00:32:06.639
[HOWLING]

00:32:09.222 --> 00:32:10.180
Now this is the first--

00:32:10.180 --> 00:32:12.559
- Now comes the new Disney
MGM studios theme park.

00:32:12.559 --> 00:32:14.100
JACOBSEN: --fielded
robot for Disney.

00:32:14.100 --> 00:32:16.995
This is the witch right there.

00:32:16.995 --> 00:32:17.870
- It was an accident!

00:32:17.870 --> 00:32:19.184
- Well, my little pretty.

00:32:19.184 --> 00:32:21.380
I can cause accidents, too.

00:32:21.380 --> 00:32:22.640
- Oh, right.

00:32:22.640 --> 00:32:26.110
You have no [INAUDIBLE].

00:32:26.110 --> 00:32:27.230
- Very well.

00:32:27.230 --> 00:32:29.440
I'll bide my time.

00:32:29.440 --> 00:32:32.030
But just try to stay out of way.

00:32:32.030 --> 00:32:35.990
First try-- I'll
get you, my pretty.

00:32:35.990 --> 00:32:37.390
And your little dog, too.

00:32:37.390 --> 00:32:40.883
[MANIACAL LAUGHTER]

00:32:43.877 --> 00:32:48.368
JACOBSEN: This one has 50
actuators and 100 sensors.

00:32:48.368 --> 00:32:49.865
It's an interesting one.

00:33:03.338 --> 00:33:06.332
He has a backup group,
you can see there.

00:33:09.825 --> 00:33:14.270
And they'll work 24
hours a day, no break.

00:33:14.270 --> 00:33:18.150
We also did the insides
of part of King Kong

00:33:18.150 --> 00:33:19.730
because he was having trouble--

00:33:19.730 --> 00:33:21.170
arthritis.

00:33:21.170 --> 00:33:24.530
[ROARING]

00:33:27.900 --> 00:33:29.730
- And now you binge rats.

00:33:29.730 --> 00:33:30.940
Do I hear six?

00:33:30.940 --> 00:33:32.480
Who makes it six?

00:33:32.480 --> 00:33:34.500
I'm not sponging for rum.

00:33:34.500 --> 00:33:38.590
It be gold I'm after.

00:33:38.590 --> 00:33:41.795
- Our defense is
in the preservation

00:33:41.795 --> 00:33:45.530
of the spirit which
prizes liberty

00:33:45.530 --> 00:33:51.910
as the heritage of all men,
in all lands, everywhere.

00:33:55.660 --> 00:33:56.815
- This is CNN.

00:34:04.200 --> 00:34:06.310
- We're at the International
Auto Show in Detroit.

00:34:06.310 --> 00:34:08.380
It opens tomorrow,
and as we break,

00:34:08.380 --> 00:34:12.159
we're going to show you the Ford
computer, this robot, Sarcos.

00:34:12.159 --> 00:34:14.270
Let's go now see Sarcos.

00:34:14.270 --> 00:34:19.570
- This is Sarcos, the robot
from the Ford exhibit.

00:34:19.570 --> 00:34:21.534
May the Ford be with you.

00:34:21.534 --> 00:34:22.659
- May the Ford be with you.

00:34:22.659 --> 00:34:23.159
Yes.

00:34:23.159 --> 00:34:25.600
- Don't forget to listen to
Larry King Live every day

00:34:25.600 --> 00:34:27.554
on CNN.

00:34:27.554 --> 00:34:28.929
- Good talk, Sarcos.

00:34:28.929 --> 00:34:30.369
- That was good.

00:34:30.369 --> 00:34:31.810
That was very good.

00:34:31.810 --> 00:34:34.960
But you know, I wouldn't
give up your day job.

00:34:34.960 --> 00:34:37.180
Is everybody taking off tonight?

00:34:37.180 --> 00:34:38.929
Because they're all
wearing the same suit.

00:34:38.929 --> 00:34:41.887
Wow, it's such a
pleasure to meet you.

00:34:41.887 --> 00:34:43.366
I'll be back.

00:34:43.366 --> 00:34:45.029
Hasta la vista, baby.

00:34:45.029 --> 00:34:47.154
[MUSIC PLAYING - MARVIN
GAYE, "HEARD IT THROUGH THE

00:34:47.154 --> 00:34:47.654
GRAPEVINE"]

00:34:47.654 --> 00:34:52.733
(SINGING) I bet you're wondering
how I knew about your plans

00:34:52.733 --> 00:34:57.170
to make me blue
with some other guys

00:34:57.170 --> 00:35:00.374
we knew before
between the two of us

00:35:00.374 --> 00:35:03.086
guys you know I loved you more.

00:35:03.086 --> 00:35:05.058
It took me by surprise.

00:35:05.058 --> 00:35:07.160
JACOBSEN: It's amazing
how many people

00:35:07.160 --> 00:35:10.362
would stay for 20 or 30
minutes and talk to the robot.

00:35:10.362 --> 00:35:12.570
But actually they were
talking to a New York stand-up

00:35:12.570 --> 00:35:17.822
comedian, Tommy Cohen, who was
at least 60% of the value here.

00:35:17.822 --> 00:35:19.280
He's really a great
guy, and you'll

00:35:19.280 --> 00:35:23.010
see him in a second describe
how they he operated the system.

00:35:23.010 --> 00:35:26.630
- I would like to take to our
viewers behind the scenes--

00:35:26.630 --> 00:35:32.630
yes, to see how you actually
work, where all these wires go,

00:35:32.630 --> 00:35:35.900
and how you are
managing to look so--

00:35:35.900 --> 00:35:38.191
excuse the term-- human.

00:35:38.191 --> 00:35:38.690
- Human?

00:35:38.690 --> 00:35:42.410
There's no need to insult me.

00:35:42.410 --> 00:35:44.970
- This is the secret passage.

00:35:44.970 --> 00:35:46.400
- I also do stand-up comedy.

00:35:46.400 --> 00:35:49.940
We are the human animators
of the living robot.

00:35:49.940 --> 00:35:52.040
I have a camera that is
fixed into my headpiece,

00:35:52.040 --> 00:35:55.107
and I can see through there what
Sarcos sees through his eyes.

00:35:55.107 --> 00:35:56.690
I also have a
television monitor which

00:35:56.690 --> 00:35:58.398
can show me what's
happening in the room,

00:35:58.398 --> 00:36:00.290
and I also can see
through the window

00:36:00.290 --> 00:36:02.510
here in case I need an
overview of the entire room.

00:36:02.510 --> 00:36:03.830
It's actually a lot of fun.

00:36:03.830 --> 00:36:07.250
- This operator is a
very talented individual,

00:36:07.250 --> 00:36:09.500
and he has become
Sarcos the robot

00:36:09.500 --> 00:36:11.960
by virtue of this sensor suit.

00:36:11.960 --> 00:36:15.260
The helmet, obviously,
is attached to his head,

00:36:15.260 --> 00:36:17.180
so wherever his head
moves, the sensors

00:36:17.180 --> 00:36:19.790
mill it and translate
that information

00:36:19.790 --> 00:36:23.660
to the computers that drive
the mechanical Sarcos figure.

00:36:23.660 --> 00:36:25.970
That's also true for the
shoulders, the elbows,

00:36:25.970 --> 00:36:27.080
and the wrists.

00:36:27.080 --> 00:36:29.930
Each individual finger
has a positioning sensor

00:36:29.930 --> 00:36:33.830
so that it, again, knows
and instructs the computer

00:36:33.830 --> 00:36:35.810
as to what moves are required.

00:36:35.810 --> 00:36:39.650
Sarcos himself is a
very elaborate hydraulic

00:36:39.650 --> 00:36:42.560
and pneumatic mechanism
with other sensors

00:36:42.560 --> 00:36:44.560
that provide feedback
back to the computer,

00:36:44.560 --> 00:36:48.210
so he literally knows where
he is at any point in time.

00:36:48.210 --> 00:36:50.810
- I auditioned for this
job with many other

00:36:50.810 --> 00:36:53.852
JACOBSEN: So if we could
turn that off, please.

00:36:53.852 --> 00:36:54.788
[END PLAYBACK]

00:36:54.788 --> 00:36:56.800
It's funny, but those
were interesting machines.

00:36:56.800 --> 00:37:00.041
[APPLAUSE]

00:37:03.340 --> 00:37:05.300
So we built a bunch
of these machines,

00:37:05.300 --> 00:37:07.633
and I'll talk a little bit
later about where we're maybe

00:37:07.633 --> 00:37:08.810
going to go with them.

00:37:08.810 --> 00:37:10.640
One problem is
not anything to do

00:37:10.640 --> 00:37:13.780
with the technological
performance, but it's cost.

00:37:13.780 --> 00:37:16.200
A typical one like
the Joe Cocker robot

00:37:16.200 --> 00:37:20.660
is $250,000 to $400,000, and
you don't make a lot of money

00:37:20.660 --> 00:37:22.410
in entertainment doing
that kind of thing.

00:37:22.410 --> 00:37:25.430
These are very, very
complicated machines,

00:37:25.430 --> 00:37:27.050
and they're sort of
one of many times.

00:37:27.050 --> 00:37:29.840
We built 16 robots
for Jurassic Park,

00:37:29.840 --> 00:37:32.720
which is a ferocious project
in terms of pain and suffering.

00:37:32.720 --> 00:37:35.470
It was really deep fun.

00:37:35.470 --> 00:37:37.190
But they were mostly
all different,

00:37:37.190 --> 00:37:42.410
and just for the review
of that whole thing,

00:37:42.410 --> 00:37:45.410
the things you have to do--
the size of the ranged from

00:37:45.410 --> 00:37:47.990
the size of a chicken--
the little compies,

00:37:47.990 --> 00:37:49.430
if you remember the movie--

00:37:49.430 --> 00:37:51.500
up to 80,000 pounds
for the T-Rex,

00:37:51.500 --> 00:37:53.300
and I'll show you
some more about that.

00:37:53.300 --> 00:37:57.650
We built 16 systems, plus all
sorts of support equipment.

00:37:57.650 --> 00:38:01.010
They're not like movie
machines, which can--

00:38:01.010 --> 00:38:03.214
but we could never
build a spider.

00:38:03.214 --> 00:38:05.630
Anyway, they're not like movie
machines where you can just

00:38:05.630 --> 00:38:08.374
make it run a couple of
times and take 15 shots

00:38:08.374 --> 00:38:09.665
and then pick the one you want.

00:38:09.665 --> 00:38:11.480
They have wake up
each morning and have

00:38:11.480 --> 00:38:15.770
to run essentially 365 days
a year, 16 hours a day,

00:38:15.770 --> 00:38:19.560
on and on and on with
silicone skins and so on.

00:38:19.560 --> 00:38:24.320
So it's a grueling environment--
water, chemicals, heat, steam,

00:38:24.320 --> 00:38:29.610
plants, and on skin exposure,
UVs, direct sunlight, water,

00:38:29.610 --> 00:38:32.090
chemicals, mechanical
flexing, and so on-- it's

00:38:32.090 --> 00:38:34.096
about the worst environment
you could imagine.

00:38:34.096 --> 00:38:35.720
And we were supposed
to have only three

00:38:35.720 --> 00:38:36.740
robots that were in water.

00:38:36.740 --> 00:38:38.156
And water, by the
way, for anybody

00:38:38.156 --> 00:38:41.030
who doesn't design machinery,
is the worst thing on earth.

00:38:41.030 --> 00:38:44.960
I mean, it's caused disasters
in our place four times--

00:38:44.960 --> 00:38:48.230
distinct, horrible disasters--
because we underestimated

00:38:48.230 --> 00:38:51.950
that water can get into
everything and then destroy it.

00:38:51.950 --> 00:38:53.870
And so having the skins
on the machines now

00:38:53.870 --> 00:38:56.247
and bathed in water is
a very tough situation.

00:38:56.247 --> 00:38:58.580
I guess the guys from the
Navy know that because they're

00:38:58.580 --> 00:38:59.570
always painting things.

00:38:59.570 --> 00:39:02.450
But the movements had
to be fast, graceful,

00:39:02.450 --> 00:39:04.100
and impedance-controlled.

00:39:04.100 --> 00:39:07.707
The person who did the
programming for that T-Rex--

00:39:07.707 --> 00:39:10.040
and that's not the best
program, but that's the only one

00:39:10.040 --> 00:39:10.710
I could get--

00:39:10.710 --> 00:39:13.870
is a guy named Davy Feiten,
who is famous at Disney.

00:39:13.870 --> 00:39:15.890
And he programmed Joe
Cocker, and he also

00:39:15.890 --> 00:39:19.730
programmed Buzz Lightyear
for the movie Toy Story.

00:39:19.730 --> 00:39:23.540
So he's got a very beautiful
sense of the way things move,

00:39:23.540 --> 00:39:25.544
and he did a lot of
the robots there.

00:39:25.544 --> 00:39:27.710
So you have to, in addition
to doing the technology,

00:39:27.710 --> 00:39:29.876
you have to get an artist
who can see the way things

00:39:29.876 --> 00:39:30.910
move very well.

00:39:30.910 --> 00:39:34.460
And it's a demanding
design from the standpoint

00:39:34.460 --> 00:39:38.150
that everything had to be
approved back and forth

00:39:38.150 --> 00:39:40.100
up the chain from Steven
Spielberg, back down

00:39:40.100 --> 00:39:42.470
to the lowest person, back
up to Steven Spielberg.

00:39:42.470 --> 00:39:44.930
And the reason we did a
lot of the sensor suit work

00:39:44.930 --> 00:39:47.570
that you saw, the little
suit to run the wolf,

00:39:47.570 --> 00:39:49.880
was actually during the
project in the beginning,

00:39:49.880 --> 00:39:52.490
the suit drove every
dinosaur that we had,

00:39:52.490 --> 00:39:54.920
and they did
lightly-rendered systems

00:39:54.920 --> 00:39:56.004
and would do choreography.

00:39:56.004 --> 00:39:58.503
The artists from Universal would
come up and do choreography

00:39:58.503 --> 00:40:01.100
on the robot, and then it would
go to Spielberg and his crew

00:40:01.100 --> 00:40:02.975
to see if they liked it
and go back and forth

00:40:02.975 --> 00:40:04.724
because the hardest
thing in the beginning

00:40:04.724 --> 00:40:07.192
was getting a consensus in the
minds of two different kinds

00:40:07.192 --> 00:40:08.900
of minds, which were
the technical people

00:40:08.900 --> 00:40:10.150
and engineers and the artists.

00:40:10.150 --> 00:40:13.776
The artists would say, I want
a really big, mean robot.

00:40:13.776 --> 00:40:15.650
And that doesn't mean
that the engineers knew

00:40:15.650 --> 00:40:17.940
what they were talking about.

00:40:17.940 --> 00:40:20.669
And I'll show you what we
did to try to solve that.

00:40:20.669 --> 00:40:22.460
The other thing that
made the project tough

00:40:22.460 --> 00:40:25.760
were things like
system complexity,

00:40:25.760 --> 00:40:27.350
the kind of movements
and coverings

00:40:27.350 --> 00:40:29.300
and so on, many moving parts.

00:40:29.300 --> 00:40:32.090
We had this one thing-- when
you see some of these inside,

00:40:32.090 --> 00:40:34.607
you'll see they have
enormous steel parts.

00:40:34.607 --> 00:40:37.190
And it was because they had this
arbitrary rule we could never

00:40:37.190 --> 00:40:40.160
pass was that we
can only use steel,

00:40:40.160 --> 00:40:44.180
and we could only go to
5,000 PSI in any weldment.

00:40:44.180 --> 00:40:46.130
And so things got big.

00:40:46.130 --> 00:40:48.830
There's a lot of surrounding
structures and conduits,

00:40:48.830 --> 00:40:51.680
safety of guests and
staff, the time and budget

00:40:51.680 --> 00:40:53.840
was short and thin.

00:40:53.840 --> 00:40:56.750
The sponsor-- we had 20
different management interfaces

00:40:56.750 --> 00:40:58.580
with the company in two years.

00:40:58.580 --> 00:41:02.390
And then, so again, it
was this sort of deep fun.

00:41:02.390 --> 00:41:06.320
So the way we started out was
generating wireframe models

00:41:06.320 --> 00:41:09.140
of all of these systems
into which we put skeletons

00:41:09.140 --> 00:41:11.480
with these little red
actuators because we decided

00:41:11.480 --> 00:41:13.130
we were never going to get
anywhere with the customer

00:41:13.130 --> 00:41:14.090
unless they could shop.

00:41:14.090 --> 00:41:15.760
Everybody likes to shop around.

00:41:15.760 --> 00:41:18.051
We'd say, this robot's going
to cost a million dollars.

00:41:18.051 --> 00:41:19.347
They'd go, what?

00:41:19.347 --> 00:41:20.930
And you couldn't do
anything but argue

00:41:20.930 --> 00:41:23.510
with them until you could
say, OK, here's a tail,

00:41:23.510 --> 00:41:26.880
and this tail has one, two,
three degrees of freedom.

00:41:26.880 --> 00:41:27.960
And so it goes like this.

00:41:27.960 --> 00:41:30.860
And they'd say, well, we want a
really slinky, serpentine tail.

00:41:30.860 --> 00:41:33.050
So you'd build a model
with eight actuators,

00:41:33.050 --> 00:41:34.940
and it would go
just like a snake.

00:41:34.940 --> 00:41:37.460
And then we'd tell them
that that was 80% more.

00:41:37.460 --> 00:41:39.600
They'd say, oh, we
don't want that.

00:41:39.600 --> 00:41:41.990
So we'd go back and
forth and back and forth,

00:41:41.990 --> 00:41:44.630
and we could make all the
changes in the virtual world.

00:41:44.630 --> 00:41:46.713
And what we were doing
that was really interesting

00:41:46.713 --> 00:41:49.250
from an engineering standpoint
was generating a database

00:41:49.250 --> 00:41:51.320
that everybody agreed with.

00:41:51.320 --> 00:41:52.970
We then took these
databases apart,

00:41:52.970 --> 00:41:55.880
and the shapes went to the
sculptors, and structures

00:41:55.880 --> 00:41:57.620
to structures people,
and the actuators

00:41:57.620 --> 00:42:00.450
went to the actuator people
for simulation, and so on.

00:42:00.450 --> 00:42:02.900
These are all
hydraulic, but that's

00:42:02.900 --> 00:42:04.840
what you need for the
kinds of systems we do.

00:42:04.840 --> 00:42:08.090
We'd much rather have had
them be done with electricity.

00:42:08.090 --> 00:42:10.660
So here's one of those
pictures of Klaus

00:42:10.660 --> 00:42:15.230
with a lightly-rendered spitter.

00:42:15.230 --> 00:42:18.110
And it starts with
foams, sculpts--

00:42:18.110 --> 00:42:20.252
I thought I had a maquette.

00:42:20.252 --> 00:42:22.460
Well, it starts with little
maquettes about this tall

00:42:22.460 --> 00:42:23.944
that everybody buys off on.

00:42:23.944 --> 00:42:25.860
They could be done
virtually, but they're not.

00:42:25.860 --> 00:42:28.010
And then you start
sculpting foam.

00:42:28.010 --> 00:42:30.080
This was a guy
named Clay Furches,

00:42:30.080 --> 00:42:31.700
which matches what he does.

00:42:31.700 --> 00:42:35.046
And this is the T-Rex being
started out of urethane foam.

00:42:35.046 --> 00:42:37.670
We're trying to get to the point
where we can digitally machine

00:42:37.670 --> 00:42:39.680
those, but we can't right now.

00:42:39.680 --> 00:42:40.850
And then, here he is.

00:42:40.850 --> 00:42:42.350
He's very committed to his work.

00:42:42.350 --> 00:42:44.840
He's doing the
work in the mouth,

00:42:44.840 --> 00:42:50.180
but our joke I was telling Woody
is that this is our lawyer.

00:42:50.180 --> 00:42:54.780
[LAUGHTER]

00:42:54.780 --> 00:42:57.415
But what nobody understands is
he's attacking the dinosaur.

00:42:57.415 --> 00:42:59.040
He's got him by the
epliglottis, right?

00:43:03.390 --> 00:43:04.640
And here's our sculpting team.

00:43:04.640 --> 00:43:07.015
This was the group that was
interesting to watch together

00:43:07.015 --> 00:43:09.790
with the engineers, and he
wouldn't fit in our building.

00:43:09.790 --> 00:43:11.980
So we had to take
his drumsticks off,

00:43:11.980 --> 00:43:18.180
and we did that and then
started building real systems.

00:43:18.180 --> 00:43:19.890
And this is him--

00:43:19.890 --> 00:43:23.920
pieces of fiberglass,
silicone, and so on.

00:43:23.920 --> 00:43:28.020
And then he was
shipped to go together

00:43:28.020 --> 00:43:31.030
with this whole system,
which looks like this.

00:43:31.030 --> 00:43:33.330
So you saw him come
out of the waterfall.

00:43:33.330 --> 00:43:37.592
Well, this big structure holds
him back under the waterfall,

00:43:37.592 --> 00:43:38.550
and then he swings out.

00:43:38.550 --> 00:43:41.190
This is the waterfall
generator, and to give you

00:43:41.190 --> 00:43:43.530
a perspective about
this, this is really more

00:43:43.530 --> 00:43:45.660
like construction
equipment because there's

00:43:45.660 --> 00:43:47.190
what a person is like.

00:43:47.190 --> 00:43:50.310
And that's about where you are
when you go over the falls.

00:43:50.310 --> 00:43:52.890
I don't know if you noticed it,
but right as he attacks you,

00:43:52.890 --> 00:43:55.530
then you tip over and
you go eight stories down

00:43:55.530 --> 00:43:59.250
and come out the bottom,
going lickety-split.

00:43:59.250 --> 00:44:05.184
And again, all of this was done
with actually Pro Engineer.

00:44:05.184 --> 00:44:07.350
And you can see the actuation
systems and so on that

00:44:07.350 --> 00:44:12.817
hold the dinosaur.

00:44:12.817 --> 00:44:14.400
And just to show you
one thing I think

00:44:14.400 --> 00:44:15.774
that's interesting
about the size

00:44:15.774 --> 00:44:19.080
is you can design things
on a screen like this

00:44:19.080 --> 00:44:20.880
and just think, well,
it's just a part.

00:44:20.880 --> 00:44:23.300
And you see this little
triangle right here.

00:44:23.300 --> 00:44:27.566
Well, that little part looks
like this when they deliver it.

00:44:27.566 --> 00:44:29.190
And so you can see
the little pins that

00:44:29.190 --> 00:44:32.242
are used to put it together
and the little studs where

00:44:32.242 --> 00:44:33.450
some of the linkages hook on.

00:44:33.450 --> 00:44:36.390
So we actually looked around,
and we had to get the biggest

00:44:36.390 --> 00:44:39.810
crane that existed in California
to put pieces of him up

00:44:39.810 --> 00:44:41.150
on the top of this 80-foot--

00:44:41.150 --> 00:44:45.290
well, actually 120-foot tower.

00:44:45.290 --> 00:44:49.630
And we, of course,
analyzed everything

00:44:49.630 --> 00:44:55.110
for our little guideline
to not go above 5,000 PSI,

00:44:55.110 --> 00:45:00.180
and none of us ever liked that.

00:45:00.180 --> 00:45:03.720
So let me just make a few other
comments, some conclusions.

00:45:06.169 --> 00:45:07.710
We get a lot of
technical people that

00:45:07.710 --> 00:45:09.810
come into the
entertainment group,

00:45:09.810 --> 00:45:11.350
and they don't quite understand.

00:45:11.350 --> 00:45:13.260
So I told them what
somebody told me--

00:45:13.260 --> 00:45:17.220
you've got to remember that
it's the story that counts,

00:45:17.220 --> 00:45:18.630
and people come
in, and they just

00:45:18.630 --> 00:45:19.755
want to have a great story.

00:45:19.755 --> 00:45:22.200
And if you have somebody that
doesn't know how to do it,

00:45:22.200 --> 00:45:24.810
run the robot that
was there with Ford,

00:45:24.810 --> 00:45:27.660
it falls flat, just like
any entertainment issue.

00:45:27.660 --> 00:45:32.790
But if you have an
entertainer like Tommy run it,

00:45:32.790 --> 00:45:35.160
then it does better
probably than him alone.

00:45:35.160 --> 00:45:38.319
And it's interesting because
the robot is less threatening.

00:45:38.319 --> 00:45:39.360
We found various people--

00:45:39.360 --> 00:45:42.330
I'd go ask them in the
audience at the Ford pavilion

00:45:42.330 --> 00:45:45.205
or the Ford booth at the
auto show why they liked it,

00:45:45.205 --> 00:45:46.830
and it was surprising
how many comments

00:45:46.830 --> 00:45:48.300
came up that there was--

00:45:48.300 --> 00:45:49.630
you could trust it.

00:45:49.630 --> 00:45:51.705
I mean, somehow they
trusted the robot.

00:45:51.705 --> 00:45:54.030
[LAUGHTER]

00:45:54.030 --> 00:45:56.280
And I'd keep digging
into it, but there

00:45:56.280 --> 00:45:58.920
were feelings like that, and
there's other funny things

00:45:58.920 --> 00:45:59.970
about dinosaurs.

00:45:59.970 --> 00:46:01.720
I've been trying to
figure out for years--

00:46:01.720 --> 00:46:03.190
why do children like dinosaurs?

00:46:03.190 --> 00:46:06.210
And I decided they like
dinosaurs for the same reason I

00:46:06.210 --> 00:46:10.740
like a guy who's 6'6" and weighs
300 pounds with tattoos all

00:46:10.740 --> 00:46:13.066
over and pulls up on a
Harley and says, who are you?

00:46:13.066 --> 00:46:14.190
And I want to make friends.

00:46:14.190 --> 00:46:17.112
[LAUGHTER]

00:46:17.112 --> 00:46:19.070
You want to make friends
with things like that.

00:46:19.070 --> 00:46:24.300
But at any rate, technology
can be the discriminator,

00:46:24.300 --> 00:46:27.810
and it can help you have a
monopoly, expanded capability,

00:46:27.810 --> 00:46:28.920
and fun.

00:46:28.920 --> 00:46:30.570
But it's just not
sufficient, and when

00:46:30.570 --> 00:46:32.264
people come to an
engineering school,

00:46:32.264 --> 00:46:34.680
you've got to talk to them
about that-- that it's actually

00:46:34.680 --> 00:46:35.550
true with the car, too.

00:46:35.550 --> 00:46:36.674
It's the story that counts.

00:46:36.674 --> 00:46:39.300
It's the car the person
gets to drive, not the fact

00:46:39.300 --> 00:46:41.580
that it has better springs.

00:46:41.580 --> 00:46:43.690
It's also a team sport.

00:46:43.690 --> 00:46:47.070
It requires a mixed set
of broad capabilities,

00:46:47.070 --> 00:46:49.920
from artistic, creative
engineering to business.

00:46:49.920 --> 00:46:53.130
And it's rough and tough,
and you better believe it.

00:46:53.130 --> 00:46:56.250
I'm telling you-- I think if you
look at the kind of people that

00:46:56.250 --> 00:46:59.006
run these industries,
they're movie people,

00:46:59.006 --> 00:46:59.880
and they're gamblers.

00:46:59.880 --> 00:47:04.020
They go, seven, and they
bet $200 million at a whack

00:47:04.020 --> 00:47:05.280
with a movie.

00:47:05.280 --> 00:47:07.890
And they run it with
horrible schedules.

00:47:07.890 --> 00:47:09.430
I really feel bad for them.

00:47:09.430 --> 00:47:10.582
It's really a tough life.

00:47:10.582 --> 00:47:12.540
Now, you put them together
with a bunch of guys

00:47:12.540 --> 00:47:15.670
like our sculptors,
who sit back and say,

00:47:15.670 --> 00:47:18.565
this is going to be the most
beautiful piece in the world,

00:47:18.565 --> 00:47:20.940
and the clock starts going,
and the money starts running,

00:47:20.940 --> 00:47:22.350
and pretty soon the sparks come.

00:47:22.350 --> 00:47:26.516
So it's a fairly
tough environment.

00:47:26.516 --> 00:47:28.140
Another little comment
about technology

00:47:28.140 --> 00:47:30.790
since we're talking about
technology and fun--

00:47:30.790 --> 00:47:34.050
It's really confusing
what matters.

00:47:34.050 --> 00:47:38.070
Flash in the technology may
not mean true business value,

00:47:38.070 --> 00:47:40.080
and there's other things
that dominate, too,

00:47:40.080 --> 00:47:42.120
like cost and safety.

00:47:42.120 --> 00:47:44.820
And so one thing
I tell people that

00:47:44.820 --> 00:47:46.560
are going to go into
this area, you've

00:47:46.560 --> 00:47:49.020
got to think about it-- there's
some really better areas.

00:47:49.020 --> 00:47:51.750
If you build
catheters for brains,

00:47:51.750 --> 00:47:54.990
and you look at the market
size, or the pumps for drugs,

00:47:54.990 --> 00:47:56.910
for genetically
engineered drugs--

00:47:56.910 --> 00:47:58.740
they talk in some
market surveys--

00:47:58.740 --> 00:48:01.350
I don't know if I believe
them-- but by the year 2010,

00:48:01.350 --> 00:48:04.500
the worldwide market for
genetically-engineered drugs,

00:48:04.500 --> 00:48:07.680
or their application,
is $400 billion a year,

00:48:07.680 --> 00:48:09.630
with a third of
every doctor's visit

00:48:09.630 --> 00:48:12.690
involving the administration
or use of something related

00:48:12.690 --> 00:48:16.140
to genetic engineering,
and the patent protection

00:48:16.140 --> 00:48:19.310
is better, and so on.

00:48:19.310 --> 00:48:23.470
But in the end, you get
to choose what you do.

00:48:23.470 --> 00:48:25.710
And the reason we
keep doing this

00:48:25.710 --> 00:48:28.170
is there's this kind of
fun aspect or satisfaction

00:48:28.170 --> 00:48:29.760
aspect in doing it.

00:48:29.760 --> 00:48:32.820
And so anyway, comments
on the future of where

00:48:32.820 --> 00:48:34.260
these things are going--

00:48:34.260 --> 00:48:35.679
you see the robots
we have there,

00:48:35.679 --> 00:48:37.970
and you see some interactive
devices I didn't show you.

00:48:37.970 --> 00:48:40.428
We've got mobility interfaces
you can get on and run around

00:48:40.428 --> 00:48:41.940
inside of virtual worlds.

00:48:41.940 --> 00:48:44.310
We have these little
shooter systems

00:48:44.310 --> 00:48:45.990
that are infrared
two-way communicators,

00:48:45.990 --> 00:48:47.130
digital communicators,
but I don't

00:48:47.130 --> 00:48:49.130
dare show them on the
East Coast because they're

00:48:49.130 --> 00:48:50.160
shaped like guns.

00:48:50.160 --> 00:48:52.800
And see, in Utah or in Montana--

00:48:52.800 --> 00:48:55.320
my boy went to school
at Montana State,

00:48:55.320 --> 00:48:57.880
and as we checked him
in at the front desk,

00:48:57.880 --> 00:49:00.270
there was a Xerox
sheet hung there

00:49:00.270 --> 00:49:02.550
that said, all students,
check your guns

00:49:02.550 --> 00:49:04.010
in at the front counter.

00:49:04.010 --> 00:49:08.617
This was two years ago,
and I thought back to MIT--

00:49:08.617 --> 00:49:09.450
something different.

00:49:09.450 --> 00:49:12.390
But at any rate, we
make little shooters

00:49:12.390 --> 00:49:16.140
that you can point and designate
complicated commands to.

00:49:16.140 --> 00:49:18.780
And if you really think
about where things are going,

00:49:18.780 --> 00:49:21.420
they're going with displays,
interfaces, computation,

00:49:21.420 --> 00:49:23.080
and communication--
in other words,

00:49:23.080 --> 00:49:25.050
how to take a person
into the environment.

00:49:25.050 --> 00:49:26.650
In the little system we did--

00:49:26.650 --> 00:49:30.270
well, I'll show it to you--
at Buffalo Bill's, it's

00:49:30.270 --> 00:49:32.010
a flume ride.

00:49:32.010 --> 00:49:33.422
We made these.

00:49:33.422 --> 00:49:35.380
Now, this is the shape
they wanted because it's

00:49:35.380 --> 00:49:37.590
the Six-Gun Western place.

00:49:37.590 --> 00:49:40.860
And this little gun does
an infrared communication

00:49:40.860 --> 00:49:43.020
with targets, and so
it turned their ride

00:49:43.020 --> 00:49:44.970
into sort of a shooting gallery.

00:49:44.970 --> 00:49:46.864
And so there's all
sorts of things

00:49:46.864 --> 00:49:48.280
going on with
computers and so on,

00:49:48.280 --> 00:49:50.490
but in the end, that's kind
of fun because you go through,

00:49:50.490 --> 00:49:53.073
and what's really interesting
is some of the bad guys that are

00:49:53.073 --> 00:49:54.250
there will shoot you back.

00:49:54.250 --> 00:49:55.520
The kids really like
it because they'll

00:49:55.520 --> 00:49:57.270
shoot one of the bad
guys, and he comes up

00:49:57.270 --> 00:49:59.186
and squirt them with a
squirt gun in the face.

00:49:59.186 --> 00:50:02.120
So there's going to be
more and more interaction

00:50:02.120 --> 00:50:03.409
in all of these areas.

00:50:03.409 --> 00:50:05.700
And so a lot of the things
that go along with computers

00:50:05.700 --> 00:50:07.005
are going to help in the area.

00:50:07.005 --> 00:50:10.950
A lot of mixed
environments and also

00:50:10.950 --> 00:50:12.900
needs for rapidly-changeable
attractions--

00:50:12.900 --> 00:50:15.900
you can no longer in that
business get a Ferris wheel

00:50:15.900 --> 00:50:17.519
and let it sit
there and sit there

00:50:17.519 --> 00:50:18.810
without adding new attractions.

00:50:18.810 --> 00:50:20.700
I think most theme
parks try to have

00:50:20.700 --> 00:50:23.700
a major attraction added
every year and a minor one

00:50:23.700 --> 00:50:25.200
in between years.

00:50:25.200 --> 00:50:27.720
And technology can help with
making variable attractions,

00:50:27.720 --> 00:50:30.810
especially if you do things
with a virtual environment.

00:50:30.810 --> 00:50:34.530
If the displays get better, you
can have more changeability.

00:50:34.530 --> 00:50:37.837
Also, the possibility of mobile
systems that can move around--

00:50:37.837 --> 00:50:39.420
what you'd really
like to do is permit

00:50:39.420 --> 00:50:41.970
the use of more
expensive capital assets

00:50:41.970 --> 00:50:44.970
in smaller attractions but
not have them go stale.

00:50:44.970 --> 00:50:47.212
If you go to a
smaller attraction,

00:50:47.212 --> 00:50:49.170
and then you go back
there two or three times--

00:50:49.170 --> 00:50:50.919
I don't know how many
people do that here,

00:50:50.919 --> 00:50:52.170
but your kids probably do.

00:50:52.170 --> 00:50:54.750
If the same stuff is there,
then you don't go back.

00:50:54.750 --> 00:50:57.270
And also, another area
that's going to expand a lot

00:50:57.270 --> 00:51:00.000
is personal ownership
by the guests.

00:51:00.000 --> 00:51:04.440
So they own the system that
they use and can take it home

00:51:04.440 --> 00:51:06.450
with them or bring it back.

00:51:06.450 --> 00:51:09.685
In the case of
these, we're going

00:51:09.685 --> 00:51:11.310
to do a thing with
something like this.

00:51:11.310 --> 00:51:12.685
It won't look like
a gun anymore.

00:51:12.685 --> 00:51:16.750
It'll be a personal communicator
for the Ford pavilion.

00:51:16.750 --> 00:51:19.090
We're going to also do
some of these in casinos.

00:51:19.090 --> 00:51:22.290
So when you check in,
you get this little box,

00:51:22.290 --> 00:51:23.940
and the little box is yours.

00:51:23.940 --> 00:51:26.380
And you can download money
into it, which isn't new,

00:51:26.380 --> 00:51:29.040
but it'll be new in
this environment.

00:51:29.040 --> 00:51:33.600
And you can use it to open your
room and play keno and gamble

00:51:33.600 --> 00:51:36.677
or order your car
up from the valet,

00:51:36.677 --> 00:51:38.760
and the system will have
all the information in it

00:51:38.760 --> 00:51:42.630
relative to you and be cheap
enough for you to own yourself.

00:51:42.630 --> 00:51:45.810
So at any rate,
that's sort of a look

00:51:45.810 --> 00:51:49.990
at the fun side of what we do.

00:51:49.990 --> 00:51:53.921
Are there any questions,
because we have three minutes?

00:51:53.921 --> 00:51:54.420
I think.

00:52:01.950 --> 00:52:03.110
Oh, these are the only two.

00:52:03.110 --> 00:52:07.140
Well, then I guess I'm supposed
to introduce Seymour Papert,

00:52:07.140 --> 00:52:08.710
and then I'll vacate
the stage here.

00:52:08.710 --> 00:52:09.210
Thank you.

00:52:09.210 --> 00:52:12.661
[APPLAUSE]

00:52:24.486 --> 00:52:24.986
PAPERT: Hi.

00:52:45.260 --> 00:52:47.770
Well, I see you can't
get away from technology.

00:52:47.770 --> 00:52:49.660
I'm not going to
show you any pictures

00:52:49.660 --> 00:52:52.830
because it's obviously
impossible to outdo

00:52:52.830 --> 00:52:55.670
what you've just seen.

00:52:55.670 --> 00:52:59.290
Also, maybe I'm going to be
rather more serious than I

00:52:59.290 --> 00:53:03.400
usually am and the other
speakers because usually,

00:53:03.400 --> 00:53:09.330
when I talk about technology,
I try to say it's for fun.

00:53:09.330 --> 00:53:12.566
It's for a special kind
of fun for children,

00:53:12.566 --> 00:53:16.560
a special kind of
fun called hard fun.

00:53:16.560 --> 00:53:21.710
And so usually, people don't
think of technology like that,

00:53:21.710 --> 00:53:24.200
and so I try to inject the fun.

00:53:24.200 --> 00:53:28.820
But I think this time, I'd
like to inject the serious side

00:53:28.820 --> 00:53:33.590
and suggest that we
are really not taking

00:53:33.590 --> 00:53:37.870
our children and the
technologies that they can use

00:53:37.870 --> 00:53:39.370
as seriously as we ought to.

00:53:39.370 --> 00:53:43.270
And by we, I mean all of you.

00:53:43.270 --> 00:53:46.150
I certainly mean our
society and people

00:53:46.150 --> 00:53:50.530
who think about what I'd like to
call the learning environment.

00:53:55.660 --> 00:53:59.910
You see, we are
slaves to technology.

00:53:59.910 --> 00:54:03.690
I was asked to move
into a place that's more

00:54:03.690 --> 00:54:05.830
convenient for that camera.

00:54:05.830 --> 00:54:08.300
But it doesn't seem to be
working any better, anyway.

00:54:08.300 --> 00:54:13.090
[LAUGHTER]

00:54:13.090 --> 00:54:15.750
OK, so I'd like
to start by giving

00:54:15.750 --> 00:54:21.900
a couple of little anecdotes
that I like to tell.

00:54:21.900 --> 00:54:29.790
Anecdote one-- you see
your grandchild, age three,

00:54:29.790 --> 00:54:32.660
walk up to a shelf,
pick out a videotape,

00:54:32.660 --> 00:54:38.535
and go and put it in the
VCR, press on some buttons

00:54:38.535 --> 00:54:41.850
and say some expletive
deleted, which

00:54:41.850 --> 00:54:44.460
means he forgot to rewind it.

00:54:44.460 --> 00:54:48.180
He rewinds it fluently
without hesitating a moment

00:54:48.180 --> 00:54:51.210
and spends the next
half hour immersed

00:54:51.210 --> 00:54:55.980
in whatever it is-- dinosaurs
or road construction machines

00:54:55.980 --> 00:54:58.650
or whatever it might be.

00:54:58.650 --> 00:55:02.580
And I'd like to reflect a
little about the significance

00:55:02.580 --> 00:55:05.550
of that kind of incident.

00:55:05.550 --> 00:55:08.970
People often say, well,
isn't that amazing

00:55:08.970 --> 00:55:14.580
that a 3-year-old can do all
that, can work that technology?

00:55:14.580 --> 00:55:17.970
And when it's a two-year-old,
and he's clicking on a mouse

00:55:17.970 --> 00:55:21.230
and making a computer
program play,

00:55:21.230 --> 00:55:25.590
they are even more
inclined to say, gee, wow.

00:55:25.590 --> 00:55:28.920
But in fact, if you think of the
complexity of what that kid is

00:55:28.920 --> 00:55:31.920
doing, either of
those kids, it's

00:55:31.920 --> 00:55:35.550
nothing compared with what they
do all the time just knowing

00:55:35.550 --> 00:55:39.210
where their toys are
kept, how to get them out

00:55:39.210 --> 00:55:43.980
of their closet, how to
put them away at the end--

00:55:43.980 --> 00:55:46.740
unlikely, but they know how
to do it if they wanted to.

00:55:46.740 --> 00:55:51.350
[LAUGHTER]

00:55:51.350 --> 00:55:54.120
What we see them doing with
these computers and VCRs

00:55:54.120 --> 00:55:56.540
is very simple
compared with that,

00:55:56.540 --> 00:55:59.790
so that's not the side
of it that's impressive.

00:55:59.790 --> 00:56:02.880
What really is impressive is--

00:56:02.880 --> 00:56:07.200
well, for me and for anybody
even half my age, I think--

00:56:07.200 --> 00:56:10.050
when I was three
years old, there

00:56:10.050 --> 00:56:14.910
was no way in which I could
decide to spend the next half

00:56:14.910 --> 00:56:20.040
hour immersed in dinosaurs
or road construction

00:56:20.040 --> 00:56:24.360
machines or anything that
might have interested me.

00:56:24.360 --> 00:56:27.780
I could explore everything
within reach and touch

00:56:27.780 --> 00:56:33.430
inside the place where I lived,
but anything beyond that,

00:56:33.430 --> 00:56:37.220
I was totally
dependent on adults.

00:56:37.220 --> 00:56:39.445
I could asked them
about dinosaurs.

00:56:39.445 --> 00:56:41.070
They might be able
to tell me, and they

00:56:41.070 --> 00:56:44.220
might be willing to tell me, but
it's not the same experience,

00:56:44.220 --> 00:56:46.130
anyway.

00:56:46.130 --> 00:56:49.740
And I think that this
is a little pointer

00:56:49.740 --> 00:56:53.700
to a deep, deep transformation
that is taking place

00:56:53.700 --> 00:56:58.740
in the relationship between
children, knowledge, adults,

00:56:58.740 --> 00:56:59.940
family, society.

00:57:02.860 --> 00:57:05.077
And it needs to be
taken very seriously.

00:57:08.280 --> 00:57:10.710
There's a lot of
talk in the papers,

00:57:10.710 --> 00:57:13.710
and when people
get on to talking

00:57:13.710 --> 00:57:16.050
about the worldwide
web and the internet,

00:57:16.050 --> 00:57:20.670
about the danger,
pornography, predators-- there

00:57:20.670 --> 00:57:21.840
are obvious dangers.

00:57:21.840 --> 00:57:24.960
People are scared of
letting their kids loose

00:57:24.960 --> 00:57:27.090
in this cyber world.

00:57:27.090 --> 00:57:29.920
And there's reasons for
a lot of those fears.

00:57:29.920 --> 00:57:31.650
But I'd like to
suggest that they

00:57:31.650 --> 00:57:36.090
miss the point, that people
worried about pornography

00:57:36.090 --> 00:57:37.470
on the internet--

00:57:37.470 --> 00:57:39.430
this is just symbolic.

00:57:39.430 --> 00:57:42.720
They are really worried
about something much deeper

00:57:42.720 --> 00:57:45.920
that they maybe
haven't articulated.

00:57:45.920 --> 00:57:48.870
And that deeper thing is
questions of dependence

00:57:48.870 --> 00:57:52.990
and independence and
the amount of control

00:57:52.990 --> 00:57:55.600
they have or want to have over
their kids and the control

00:57:55.600 --> 00:57:57.920
that the kids have
or want to have

00:57:57.920 --> 00:58:02.740
or the parents want them to
have over the world and life.

00:58:02.740 --> 00:58:08.230
Kids are getting access to
vastly greater and different

00:58:08.230 --> 00:58:13.600
kinds of dependence
and independence.

00:58:13.600 --> 00:58:17.910
And this is something
that, I think,

00:58:17.910 --> 00:58:22.770
is going far enough
already to make

00:58:22.770 --> 00:58:25.470
it irresponsible for people
not to be thinking very

00:58:25.470 --> 00:58:31.180
hard about rethinking
what parenting is about,

00:58:31.180 --> 00:58:33.220
what the family is
about, what being a child

00:58:33.220 --> 00:58:35.680
is about, what we
expect from children.

00:58:35.680 --> 00:58:37.510
That the children can
wander off and might

00:58:37.510 --> 00:58:41.800
run into a picture of
a naked person or worse

00:58:41.800 --> 00:58:45.070
is really part of
this larger question

00:58:45.070 --> 00:58:49.120
that children are less
dependent on their adults

00:58:49.120 --> 00:58:50.680
and becoming less and less so.

00:58:50.680 --> 00:58:53.710
And what is the
implication of that?

00:58:53.710 --> 00:58:56.477
Nobody really knows.

00:58:56.477 --> 00:58:58.060
And I don't think
they're just talking

00:58:58.060 --> 00:58:59.720
about it's going to
solve the problem,

00:58:59.720 --> 00:59:03.170
but at least it's a step.

00:59:03.170 --> 00:59:06.830
I'd like to look at
this from a slightly

00:59:06.830 --> 00:59:11.960
sort of more academic point of
view, generalized point of view

00:59:11.960 --> 00:59:17.930
by outlining a different
kind of theory of stages

00:59:17.930 --> 00:59:22.250
in individual development
in relation to knowledge

00:59:22.250 --> 00:59:24.110
and learning.

00:59:24.110 --> 00:59:27.490
Stage one-- a baby is born.

00:59:27.490 --> 00:59:30.470
And from day one, if
not before, that baby

00:59:30.470 --> 00:59:34.490
begins an active
process of exploration--

00:59:34.490 --> 00:59:37.385
first, its own body,
its mother's body.

00:59:37.385 --> 00:59:42.050
It begins to crawl and
explores the world.

00:59:42.050 --> 00:59:47.150
And all this is producing an
incredible lot of learning.

00:59:47.150 --> 00:59:51.890
That baby is learning very
thoroughly a very limited

00:59:51.890 --> 00:59:54.092
world, that immediate reality.

00:59:54.092 --> 00:59:55.800
But it's learning it
in a particular way,

00:59:55.800 --> 00:59:59.720
note-- this particular
way is by exploration,

00:59:59.720 --> 01:00:05.570
through experience,
driven from inner desire.

01:00:05.570 --> 01:00:09.560
Everything that's done is
meaningful to that child.

01:00:09.560 --> 01:00:11.010
That's stage one.

01:00:11.010 --> 01:00:19.310
Stage two begins when the child
sees a dinosaur and says, well,

01:00:19.310 --> 01:00:21.590
how did dinosaurs eat?

01:00:21.590 --> 01:00:23.450
Or what happened to them?

01:00:23.450 --> 01:00:27.230
Or whatever question might
be asked about the dinosaur--

01:00:27.230 --> 01:00:32.300
this child has run into inside
the little immediate world,

01:00:32.300 --> 01:00:35.250
has run into questions about
the bigger world out there.

01:00:35.250 --> 01:00:36.710
And the child has
no way to answer

01:00:36.710 --> 01:00:41.900
those questions except, well,
fantasy that can go freely,

01:00:41.900 --> 01:00:48.260
and that's wonderful, but is not
related, not rooted in reality.

01:00:48.260 --> 01:00:51.950
Or asking adults
or accidentally,

01:00:51.950 --> 01:00:54.560
it so happens that there
is a piece on television

01:00:54.560 --> 01:01:01.010
or the mother happens to
read a book, but on the whole

01:01:01.010 --> 01:01:03.750
the child is helpless in
getting that knowledge.

01:01:03.750 --> 01:01:07.990
And so we gradually
get a shift in the mode

01:01:07.990 --> 01:01:13.420
of learning from experiential
to verbal because they are

01:01:13.420 --> 01:01:14.890
going to ask and get answers--

01:01:14.890 --> 01:01:16.600
it's all in words.

01:01:16.600 --> 01:01:20.170
And bit by bit, it moves
away from your agenda

01:01:20.170 --> 01:01:24.160
to somebody else's agenda,
and the ultimate of that

01:01:24.160 --> 01:01:29.487
is school, where it's entirely
somebody else's agenda.

01:01:29.487 --> 01:01:31.570
There's the curriculum,
and that's what you learn,

01:01:31.570 --> 01:01:34.480
and somebody else is going to
decide here are the textbooks,

01:01:34.480 --> 01:01:36.620
and there's all this
set-out knowledge

01:01:36.620 --> 01:01:39.700
that somebody has decided that
that's what you should know.

01:01:39.700 --> 01:01:42.010
And so you go to
school, and you go

01:01:42.010 --> 01:01:44.426
through the traumatic
experience of--

01:01:44.426 --> 01:01:46.300
well, I'm going to say
this outrageous thing,

01:01:46.300 --> 01:01:47.410
but I really mean it--

01:01:47.410 --> 01:01:51.650
giving up learning and
adapting to being taught.

01:01:54.870 --> 01:01:59.340
And being taught is not
the same as learning.

01:01:59.340 --> 01:02:01.290
Well, you go through
school, and it's

01:02:01.290 --> 01:02:04.620
a precarious little journey.

01:02:04.620 --> 01:02:07.170
It's more precarious
than anybody in this room

01:02:07.170 --> 01:02:10.830
is likely to know, because
you wouldn't be in this room

01:02:10.830 --> 01:02:14.100
if you weren't one of those
who survived the journey.

01:02:14.100 --> 01:02:16.910
[LAUGHTER]

01:02:16.910 --> 01:02:19.890
Most people don't.

01:02:19.890 --> 01:02:22.500
And most people in the
course of this journey

01:02:22.500 --> 01:02:27.750
have that drive to learn,
curiosity, explore, to know,

01:02:27.750 --> 01:02:31.410
to be independent that
babies are all born with.

01:02:31.410 --> 01:02:34.210
They have it attenuated,
and it's gone,

01:02:34.210 --> 01:02:39.230
and that's just a fact.

01:02:39.230 --> 01:02:44.420
Now those who survived get
something wonderful out

01:02:44.420 --> 01:02:50.190
of school, namely they get new
ways of access to independence.

01:02:50.190 --> 01:02:53.130
You learn to read, you'll learn
to not just read the letters,

01:02:53.130 --> 01:02:57.960
to use encyclopedias and
libraries and conversations

01:02:57.960 --> 01:03:00.600
and all the rest of the
means of access to knowledge,

01:03:00.600 --> 01:03:03.180
and eventually,
you get to a place

01:03:03.180 --> 01:03:05.790
where you go back to stage
one, and that stage three.

01:03:08.490 --> 01:03:12.780
The closest thing I
know to the stage one

01:03:12.780 --> 01:03:15.000
child in those first
couple of years

01:03:15.000 --> 01:03:17.760
is the best work by
graduate students

01:03:17.760 --> 01:03:20.820
or the best work
in a research lab

01:03:20.820 --> 01:03:26.500
or the best and most creative
work in making Jurassic Park

01:03:26.500 --> 01:03:29.860
or creating a business
or independent people

01:03:29.860 --> 01:03:34.030
really being creative,
following their own ideas,

01:03:34.030 --> 01:03:38.520
learning what they need to
know when they need to know it,

01:03:38.520 --> 01:03:40.990
knowing from experience.

01:03:40.990 --> 01:03:45.240
And so I think we really
do, in an important sense,

01:03:45.240 --> 01:03:48.330
go back to stage three--

01:03:48.330 --> 01:03:52.000
in stage three go
back to stage one.

01:03:52.000 --> 01:03:57.690
Well, what my story about the
videotape is really pointing to

01:03:57.690 --> 01:04:00.990
is that technology
is getting to a place

01:04:00.990 --> 01:04:06.600
where it can bypass stage two.

01:04:06.600 --> 01:04:09.510
As we are getting to a
place where there need not

01:04:09.510 --> 01:04:14.360
be that discontinuity,
where reading books,

01:04:14.360 --> 01:04:18.620
encyclopedias in
printed written form

01:04:18.620 --> 01:04:26.450
no longer are the major
and only form of access

01:04:26.450 --> 01:04:31.120
to a broader knowledge
and the broader world.

01:04:31.120 --> 01:04:36.090
And the videotape is just a
hint at what is already coming

01:04:36.090 --> 01:04:39.750
and what's around the
corner, that with more

01:04:39.750 --> 01:04:41.730
and more of them,
with video on demand,

01:04:41.730 --> 01:04:47.580
with the world wide web, with
just the beginnings of search

01:04:47.580 --> 01:04:52.990
engines that are not
formulated, that don't require,

01:04:52.990 --> 01:04:56.760
that are not text-based,
and their categories

01:04:56.760 --> 01:04:59.400
are not the categories
that interest, no doubt.

01:04:59.400 --> 01:05:04.710
We are beginning to see
organizations of people trying

01:05:04.710 --> 01:05:11.950
to make interfaces accessible
to preschool children

01:05:11.950 --> 01:05:16.870
independently of reading and
writing so that they are now

01:05:16.870 --> 01:05:20.320
just about beginning to get
across the threshold of access

01:05:20.320 --> 01:05:25.270
to knowledge, and ultimately
better knowledge, more

01:05:25.270 --> 01:05:29.500
detailed knowledge, easier
access to knowledge than can be

01:05:29.500 --> 01:05:31.930
found in printed books,
for all the reasons

01:05:31.930 --> 01:05:36.370
that make the electronic
interactive media more

01:05:36.370 --> 01:05:39.180
effective.

01:05:39.180 --> 01:05:41.960
This is not just
in its projection

01:05:41.960 --> 01:05:47.730
a supplement to reading and
writing or just another medium.

01:05:47.730 --> 01:05:50.810
It is a vastly superior medium.

01:05:50.810 --> 01:05:53.320
Of course, that will
subsume reading and writing,

01:05:53.320 --> 01:05:56.420
and it will always include
text in the foreseeable future,

01:05:56.420 --> 01:05:57.200
anyway.

01:05:57.200 --> 01:06:03.500
But it will no longer be
true that so-called literacy,

01:06:03.500 --> 01:06:08.430
that I'd rather
call letteracy, is

01:06:08.430 --> 01:06:14.670
the essential road towards being
able to get more knowledge.

01:06:14.670 --> 01:06:21.120
I think that we ought to
question very seriously,

01:06:21.120 --> 01:06:24.570
and we will be sort of forced
to question more seriously

01:06:24.570 --> 01:06:29.370
the importance that we give
in our school years to those

01:06:29.370 --> 01:06:32.960
so-called three rs--

01:06:32.960 --> 01:06:34.850
reading, writing,
and arithmetic.

01:06:37.410 --> 01:06:38.960
Well, I think reading
is going to be

01:06:38.960 --> 01:06:40.790
important for a long
time, but I would

01:06:40.790 --> 01:06:45.770
foresee that it will stop having
that primordial importance

01:06:45.770 --> 01:06:47.794
that it's had.

01:06:47.794 --> 01:06:49.460
I don't think kids
will give up reading.

01:06:49.460 --> 01:06:52.220
As a matter of
fact, my prediction

01:06:52.220 --> 01:06:54.710
is that if we stop
trying to make kids read,

01:06:54.710 --> 01:06:59.090
they would learn to read
much more because I think

01:06:59.090 --> 01:07:00.710
a lot of the problems
that kids have

01:07:00.710 --> 01:07:04.340
about reading is that parents
and teachers get so upset when

01:07:04.340 --> 01:07:07.190
this kid is not reading
on time, according

01:07:07.190 --> 01:07:12.140
to some timetable that's
been set by somebody else,

01:07:12.140 --> 01:07:16.500
that they develop these
tensions, and they are worried,

01:07:16.500 --> 01:07:19.010
and the kid senses
all this, and there's

01:07:19.010 --> 01:07:26.240
a whole psychological cascade
of events that frustrate

01:07:26.240 --> 01:07:29.130
good, free, relaxed learning.

01:07:29.130 --> 01:07:34.630
And so we self-generate
this remarkable epidemic

01:07:34.630 --> 01:07:37.930
that has invaded the world
that just didn't exist 30

01:07:37.930 --> 01:07:41.622
or 40 years ago called learning
disabilities, dyslexia,

01:07:41.622 --> 01:07:42.580
and all the rest of it.

01:07:46.440 --> 01:07:52.410
Well, so when we look at the
way that our education world is

01:07:52.410 --> 01:07:57.390
thinking about technology
in relation to learning,

01:07:57.390 --> 01:08:00.585
it's, to my mind, shockingly
different from the implications

01:08:00.585 --> 01:08:04.070
of the picture I've
been trying to give you.

01:08:04.070 --> 01:08:06.650
I'm not talking
about what you will

01:08:06.650 --> 01:08:09.020
see in schools all
over the place, where

01:08:09.020 --> 01:08:12.560
somehow the idea that we
will use these computers

01:08:12.560 --> 01:08:17.560
to improve a little bit or a
lot the same kind of teaching

01:08:17.560 --> 01:08:21.290
of the same kind of material on
roughly the same sort of time

01:08:21.290 --> 01:08:23.080
scale.

01:08:23.080 --> 01:08:26.470
Well, that's the way in
which all technologies first

01:08:26.470 --> 01:08:27.399
enter the world.

01:08:27.399 --> 01:08:31.750
And Jurassic Park
we just looked at,

01:08:31.750 --> 01:08:34.270
and the first use
of a movie camera

01:08:34.270 --> 01:08:35.620
wasn't anything like that.

01:08:35.620 --> 01:08:39.160
The first movie was well, you
had this wonderful camera.

01:08:39.160 --> 01:08:44.310
You said, well, this is
technology-aided theater,

01:08:44.310 --> 01:08:47.609
so act a play and put
the camera in front of it

01:08:47.609 --> 01:08:50.850
and make the first movie.

01:08:50.850 --> 01:08:53.850
So you're using it
to improve, to make

01:08:53.850 --> 01:08:56.100
a slightly better
version of something

01:08:56.100 --> 01:08:57.880
that you've been
doing all along.

01:08:57.880 --> 01:09:03.270
And that's about where we were
at in our uses of computers

01:09:03.270 --> 01:09:05.970
in schools.

01:09:05.970 --> 01:09:08.279
At least they're not harmful.

01:09:08.279 --> 01:09:11.040
Where I think they
are harmful, where

01:09:11.040 --> 01:09:15.000
that idea of how to use
computers is very harmful

01:09:15.000 --> 01:09:21.090
is in what's happening to
children whose families own

01:09:21.090 --> 01:09:26.640
computers and who buy the
so-called education software

01:09:26.640 --> 01:09:28.140
that's flooding the market.

01:09:28.140 --> 01:09:31.790
And this is why I think
it's actually harmful

01:09:31.790 --> 01:09:35.630
because I see that transition
from stage one to stage two.

01:09:35.630 --> 01:09:41.029
In the past, it's
been a necessary evil,

01:09:41.029 --> 01:09:43.670
let's say, a
necessary price to pay

01:09:43.670 --> 01:09:47.450
because we had no
way in which children

01:09:47.450 --> 01:09:49.310
could continue the
kind of learning

01:09:49.310 --> 01:09:50.479
that happens before school.

01:09:50.479 --> 01:09:53.330
Let's call it home-style
learning or natural learning.

01:09:56.600 --> 01:09:58.670
We had no way in which
that learning could

01:09:58.670 --> 01:10:00.380
encompass all the
knowledge we thought

01:10:00.380 --> 01:10:01.710
children ought to have--

01:10:01.710 --> 01:10:03.230
incorrectly thought that.

01:10:03.230 --> 01:10:06.500
So we instituted something else
called school-style learning.

01:10:09.410 --> 01:10:11.690
And there was a
necessary evil, and it's

01:10:11.690 --> 01:10:13.160
an extremely dangerous thing.

01:10:13.160 --> 01:10:15.860
And it's an inferior
kind of learning,

01:10:15.860 --> 01:10:18.980
as evidenced by the
fact that none of you,

01:10:18.980 --> 01:10:22.070
if you need to know something,
go and take a course in it.

01:10:22.070 --> 01:10:23.660
You read the newspaper.

01:10:23.660 --> 01:10:27.770
You read the financial
reports if you

01:10:27.770 --> 01:10:31.160
want to make an investment.

01:10:31.160 --> 01:10:33.795
You don't submit yourself to
somebody else's curriculum

01:10:33.795 --> 01:10:34.295
in a school.

01:10:39.809 --> 01:10:41.350
So why do we put
children through it?

01:10:41.350 --> 01:10:42.808
Because they don't
have any choice.

01:10:42.808 --> 01:10:45.860
But now, a new
thing is happening,

01:10:45.860 --> 01:10:49.330
and that is most parents--

01:10:49.330 --> 01:10:51.100
certainly most
educated parents-- now

01:10:51.100 --> 01:10:55.870
have computers at home,
and a new kind of question

01:10:55.870 --> 01:10:58.600
is coming up for them.

01:10:58.600 --> 01:11:04.360
Previously, it was a division
of labor that children--

01:11:04.360 --> 01:11:06.340
certain kinds of things,
they learned at home.

01:11:06.340 --> 01:11:07.610
They learned to talk.

01:11:07.610 --> 01:11:12.360
They learned to walk, to
find their way around space,

01:11:12.360 --> 01:11:17.010
get around the house, get around
the parents, for that matter,

01:11:17.010 --> 01:11:19.500
learn to be psychologically
manipulative

01:11:19.500 --> 01:11:22.050
and get some insight into
psychology-- all this stuff

01:11:22.050 --> 01:11:24.450
they learned in home.

01:11:24.450 --> 01:11:29.170
On the other hand, stuff like
what we call math at school--

01:11:29.170 --> 01:11:33.780
I just can't bring myself
to call it mathematics.

01:11:33.780 --> 01:11:36.340
This stuff was the job
of teachers and schools.

01:11:36.340 --> 01:11:39.310
Parents never bothered
much about it,

01:11:39.310 --> 01:11:40.959
except when they got
a bad report card,

01:11:40.959 --> 01:11:42.750
and they had a conference
with the teacher.

01:11:42.750 --> 01:11:44.670
Now, all of a
sudden, parents are

01:11:44.670 --> 01:11:56.220
being flooded with advertising
saying, buy this CD-ROM.

01:11:56.220 --> 01:11:57.960
It will teach your child.

01:11:57.960 --> 01:11:59.520
It will be such fun.

01:11:59.520 --> 01:12:02.250
She won't even know
that she's learning.

01:12:02.250 --> 01:12:05.825
She won't even know
she's doing math.

01:12:05.825 --> 01:12:08.200
And there's several aspects
of that that I think are bad.

01:12:08.200 --> 01:12:12.010
One of them is that parents are
being asked to make decisions

01:12:12.010 --> 01:12:16.810
about issues that they
haven't thought about,

01:12:16.810 --> 01:12:19.206
and they're not
qualified to make.

01:12:19.206 --> 01:12:20.580
Although that's
the lesson there.

01:12:20.580 --> 01:12:23.430
If they would really
throw off their prejudices

01:12:23.430 --> 01:12:26.910
and just think, they
would do much better.

01:12:26.910 --> 01:12:28.620
But what, in fact,
they are doing

01:12:28.620 --> 01:12:33.780
is succumbing to
the marketing device

01:12:33.780 --> 01:12:37.590
that you will sell
best as software that

01:12:37.590 --> 01:12:39.390
has these characteristics--
it must look

01:12:39.390 --> 01:12:42.810
like school because in
many people's minds,

01:12:42.810 --> 01:12:44.730
school is the
safest way to think

01:12:44.730 --> 01:12:47.190
about what's good learning.

01:12:47.190 --> 01:12:49.560
It will serve a
babysitter function

01:12:49.560 --> 01:12:54.510
by keeping the kid focused there
without you having to bother

01:12:54.510 --> 01:12:59.350
about, without bothering you.

01:12:59.350 --> 01:13:01.510
And it should be--

01:13:01.510 --> 01:13:03.170
well, it comes the
same thing again--

01:13:03.170 --> 01:13:04.990
it should be just
run independently.

01:13:04.990 --> 01:13:07.550
The kid shouldn't have
to ask many questions.

01:13:07.550 --> 01:13:10.840
And it shouldn't plant too many
bad ideas in the child's mind,

01:13:10.840 --> 01:13:13.160
either.

01:13:13.160 --> 01:13:14.310
So you buy this stuff.

01:13:14.310 --> 01:13:16.910
It's the best-selling
software, and what you're doing

01:13:16.910 --> 01:13:21.330
is importing into the
home those early years,

01:13:21.330 --> 01:13:23.900
the kinds of learning and
the habits of learning

01:13:23.900 --> 01:13:26.990
that previously belonged
to the school years,

01:13:26.990 --> 01:13:30.770
and nobody knows how
bad a thing that is.

01:13:30.770 --> 01:13:32.660
I suspect it's a very
bad thing, and I'd

01:13:32.660 --> 01:13:36.500
like to emphasize that
by telling another story

01:13:36.500 --> 01:13:37.760
about a grandchild.

01:13:40.610 --> 01:13:45.210
A little while
ago, I spent a day

01:13:45.210 --> 01:13:52.960
in the school of my then 6
and 1/2 year old grandchild.

01:13:52.960 --> 01:13:54.720
I sort of hung out in school.

01:13:54.720 --> 01:13:56.620
I didn't sit next to
him, but I kept an eye

01:13:56.620 --> 01:13:57.670
on what he was doing.

01:13:57.670 --> 01:14:02.010
Two things that struck
me that I'd seen often--

01:14:02.010 --> 01:14:04.530
maybe I should have said
earlier, I do a lot of--

01:14:04.530 --> 01:14:09.360
for the last quite
a number of years,

01:14:09.360 --> 01:14:12.300
I've been increasingly
spending my time thinking

01:14:12.300 --> 01:14:14.790
about what computers
can mean for children.

01:14:14.790 --> 01:14:16.620
I've done a lot
of work in schools

01:14:16.620 --> 01:14:20.010
and done projects in them and
hung around in schools a lot.

01:14:20.010 --> 01:14:22.900
So it's not a new experience
for me to go into a school,

01:14:22.900 --> 01:14:24.900
but I hadn't done it
for awhile, and it

01:14:24.900 --> 01:14:28.110
was a new experience for me
to go into a school thinking

01:14:28.110 --> 01:14:32.460
about one individual child
whom I knew very, very well.

01:14:32.460 --> 01:14:34.960
And the two things that
struck me were these.

01:14:34.960 --> 01:14:39.880
First of all, it looked
like a different person.

01:14:39.880 --> 01:14:45.400
This kid I know as an
intellectually intense, highly

01:14:45.400 --> 01:14:50.949
energetic, vibrantly energetic
person who gets passionately

01:14:50.949 --> 01:14:52.990
interested in things, and
you can't take him away

01:14:52.990 --> 01:14:56.440
from the things he's
doing and he wants to do.

01:14:56.440 --> 01:14:58.980
Computers, yes.

01:14:58.980 --> 01:15:00.450
Video games, yes.

01:15:00.450 --> 01:15:04.440
But growing vegetables, he
was fascinated by for a while.

01:15:04.440 --> 01:15:06.270
He's involved in cooking.

01:15:06.270 --> 01:15:09.900
He's involved in some
very big projects

01:15:09.900 --> 01:15:12.420
and building out of LEGO--

01:15:12.420 --> 01:15:16.401
high-quality stuff,
self-directed.

01:15:16.401 --> 01:15:20.490
I go into school, and I
see the first thing strikes

01:15:20.490 --> 01:15:23.310
me is that this
level of intensity--

01:15:23.310 --> 01:15:25.530
instead of being up there,
it's down there somewhere.

01:15:25.530 --> 01:15:32.210
It's a kind of low, laid
back, not much is happening.

01:15:32.210 --> 01:15:34.000
Most of the time
they're sitting around,

01:15:34.000 --> 01:15:35.650
or when they're
paying attention,

01:15:35.650 --> 01:15:37.360
they're sort of
paying attention.

01:15:37.360 --> 01:15:40.180
They have little
bursts of energy.

01:15:40.180 --> 01:15:43.270
There's a good teacher who
sometimes sort of turns them on

01:15:43.270 --> 01:15:44.000
for a while.

01:15:44.000 --> 01:15:46.510
But taken over the
whole day, I would

01:15:46.510 --> 01:15:51.100
say that in terms of energy
level, it's about 10%

01:15:51.100 --> 01:15:56.790
of what I see this kid,
and that bothers me.

01:15:56.790 --> 01:15:59.634
And I think it's characteristic
of the nature of school

01:15:59.634 --> 01:16:01.050
and the nature of
that shift we're

01:16:01.050 --> 01:16:04.970
making in kinds of learning.

01:16:04.970 --> 01:16:09.646
And then the other thing
that bothered me was related,

01:16:09.646 --> 01:16:11.270
but I think a different
thing, and that

01:16:11.270 --> 01:16:12.635
was a corruption of values.

01:16:15.450 --> 01:16:17.150
And I think school,
in its nature,

01:16:17.150 --> 01:16:19.920
is deeply corrupting in values.

01:16:22.860 --> 01:16:25.970
And in this, the example
of that particularly

01:16:25.970 --> 01:16:29.540
struck me was intellectual
honesty and goals.

01:16:29.540 --> 01:16:33.780
This kid at home and
before going to school

01:16:33.780 --> 01:16:35.520
would do things
because he thought

01:16:35.520 --> 01:16:38.760
they were important and had
a real personal commitment

01:16:38.760 --> 01:16:43.330
to doing them and would
defend it, sometimes

01:16:43.330 --> 01:16:48.950
much to the causing a lot
of trouble for his parents.

01:16:48.950 --> 01:16:54.500
Now in school, instead of doing
things that came from his heart

01:16:54.500 --> 01:16:57.080
and came from deep commitment
that he believed in,

01:16:57.080 --> 01:16:59.045
he was doing things
because he got a check

01:16:59.045 --> 01:17:04.560
mark in a book or a star,
or a teacher approved of it.

01:17:04.560 --> 01:17:07.650
So he's being shifted
from acting out

01:17:07.650 --> 01:17:13.470
of conviction to acting out
of getting external approval.

01:17:13.470 --> 01:17:18.570
I can't imagine any worse
moral corruption than that.

01:17:18.570 --> 01:17:20.970
And it's intrinsic in
our school system--

01:17:20.970 --> 01:17:23.010
some schools worse than others.

01:17:23.010 --> 01:17:24.150
But it's not them--

01:17:24.150 --> 01:17:27.316
some teachers manage to
overcome this entirely.

01:17:27.316 --> 01:17:28.440
I'm not talking about that.

01:17:28.440 --> 01:17:32.200
Wonderful things happen in some
classrooms by some teachers.

01:17:32.200 --> 01:17:34.110
But if you think
about the nature

01:17:34.110 --> 01:17:42.600
of that system as a whole, these
are qualities of that system.

01:17:42.600 --> 01:17:44.310
Well, how could it be different?

01:17:47.560 --> 01:17:49.810
How can technology
make it different?

01:17:49.810 --> 01:17:56.400
I've mentioned one way in which
access to computers and the web

01:17:56.400 --> 01:17:58.890
and things like that
make a difference,

01:17:58.890 --> 01:18:00.420
and I want to mention
another, which

01:18:00.420 --> 01:18:06.630
I think is just as important,
maybe more important.

01:18:06.630 --> 01:18:08.610
The two that I'm
going to mention

01:18:08.610 --> 01:18:10.950
get their importance
by being put together.

01:18:10.950 --> 01:18:13.050
The one that I've
mentioned already

01:18:13.050 --> 01:18:17.700
is what's become the
popular kind of synonym

01:18:17.700 --> 01:18:20.340
for what computer stuff and
the network and all this

01:18:20.340 --> 01:18:22.510
is about-- it's information.

01:18:22.510 --> 01:18:25.710
It's information
technology, we even call it.

01:18:25.710 --> 01:18:30.194
Well, that's a wrong
approach, I think,

01:18:30.194 --> 01:18:32.360
because of what I'm going
to talk about in a moment.

01:18:35.970 --> 01:18:40.260
In 1967, I first came to
MIT, and before coming here,

01:18:40.260 --> 01:18:46.590
I'd had a lot of contact with
Jean Piaget, a great child

01:18:46.590 --> 01:18:49.140
psychologist in Switzerland.

01:18:49.140 --> 01:18:52.610
I was a mathematician.

01:18:52.610 --> 01:18:56.360
When I came to MIT, I'd played
with computers a little.

01:18:56.360 --> 01:18:59.150
This was in 1964.

01:18:59.150 --> 01:19:01.190
I'd played with a few
computers in Europe

01:19:01.190 --> 01:19:04.070
and had a little
contact with them.

01:19:04.070 --> 01:19:11.340
I came to MIT, and I was
waiting for Marvin Minsky

01:19:11.340 --> 01:19:15.990
to turn up late for an
appointment, and there he was.

01:19:15.990 --> 01:19:18.760
Next to his office
was a computer.

01:19:18.760 --> 01:19:27.490
In fact, it was the first model
of Digital Equipment's PDP-1.

01:19:27.490 --> 01:19:29.670
Well, maybe it was number two--

01:19:29.670 --> 01:19:34.830
a very early, early prototype.

01:19:34.830 --> 01:19:38.280
And I was sitting there, so I
just started fiddling with it,

01:19:38.280 --> 01:19:41.010
and fiddled with it
for about an hour.

01:19:41.010 --> 01:19:43.740
And when Minsky came in,
I sort of apologized.

01:19:43.740 --> 01:19:46.920
I said, I've been playing
with his computer.

01:19:46.920 --> 01:19:49.320
He looked at me in amazement
and said, well, of course.

01:19:49.320 --> 01:19:51.660
That's what it's there for.

01:19:51.660 --> 01:19:54.270
And this idea that you could
just take this computer

01:19:54.270 --> 01:19:58.680
and just fool with it
was totally alien to me,

01:19:58.680 --> 01:20:00.680
and for a long
time, it was going

01:20:00.680 --> 01:20:03.600
to become alien to most
people's ideas of computers.

01:20:03.600 --> 01:20:06.630
But of course, now we
have personal computers,

01:20:06.630 --> 01:20:08.760
and that's the standard model.

01:20:08.760 --> 01:20:11.490
Playing with that
computer, I suddenly

01:20:11.490 --> 01:20:15.720
found out that I could
take some problems that

01:20:15.720 --> 01:20:19.260
had been bothering me for
years, and with this computer,

01:20:19.260 --> 01:20:21.030
I could get a whole new insight.

01:20:21.030 --> 01:20:24.960
And in a few days, I cleaned
up a whole bunch of problems

01:20:24.960 --> 01:20:27.540
that had been on my agenda.

01:20:27.540 --> 01:20:30.330
And this was a
revelatory experience.

01:20:30.330 --> 01:20:35.190
And in fact, that's what caused
me to decide to come to MIT.

01:20:35.190 --> 01:20:39.480
There was what I referred to
as a short two-week visit,

01:20:39.480 --> 01:20:43.740
and then because of this, I
accepted a two-year invitation,

01:20:43.740 --> 01:20:45.630
and that turned into
the rest of my life.

01:20:45.630 --> 01:20:48.120
I'm still here.

01:20:48.120 --> 01:20:51.780
But this revelation
planted the seed in my mind

01:20:51.780 --> 01:20:54.300
that I'd been worrying
for years before that

01:20:54.300 --> 01:20:57.180
about how can the learning
of children change.

01:20:59.750 --> 01:21:01.870
And all of a sudden,
there it was.

01:21:01.870 --> 01:21:04.030
It seemed that here is the way.

01:21:04.030 --> 01:21:08.530
If children could only have
that enormous amplification

01:21:08.530 --> 01:21:11.860
of power, that exhilarating
experience of intellectual

01:21:11.860 --> 01:21:14.170
empowerment that
I had just getting

01:21:14.170 --> 01:21:18.430
at that what I see now as an
incredibly primitive computer.

01:21:18.430 --> 01:21:21.160
If children could
only get at that,

01:21:21.160 --> 01:21:27.040
that could change their patterns
of intellectual development.

01:21:27.040 --> 01:21:30.220
And that became a little
obsession of mine, and right

01:21:30.220 --> 01:21:33.910
through the '60s and '70s, when
the idea of children actually

01:21:33.910 --> 01:21:37.720
getting at a computer, except
in specially artificial set-ups,

01:21:37.720 --> 01:21:39.970
some of which we
were responsible for,

01:21:39.970 --> 01:21:42.910
seemed very remote.

01:21:42.910 --> 01:21:48.230
And I was accused by,
when I wrote proposals

01:21:48.230 --> 01:21:50.590
trying to get funds
to support it,

01:21:50.590 --> 01:21:54.020
they ranged from
romantic to elitist.

01:21:54.020 --> 01:21:56.500
I mean, maybe a few
millionaires' children

01:21:56.500 --> 01:21:58.210
will be able to
benefit from this,

01:21:58.210 --> 01:22:01.610
but it won't change the world.

01:22:01.610 --> 01:22:07.040
Well, it has changed the
world, and in all that time--

01:22:07.040 --> 01:22:08.350
it's now 30 years--

01:22:08.350 --> 01:22:13.760
a main thrust of our work that
was the Artificial Intelligence

01:22:13.760 --> 01:22:14.330
Lab.

01:22:14.330 --> 01:22:17.570
And then when the
Media Lab got founded,

01:22:17.570 --> 01:22:21.380
I was one of the original group
in founding the Media Lab,

01:22:21.380 --> 01:22:24.740
and we transferred our
activities into there

01:22:24.740 --> 01:22:27.170
and continued until now.

01:22:27.170 --> 01:22:29.750
And the thrust of
our work is not

01:22:29.750 --> 01:22:33.650
the computer as an
information source

01:22:33.650 --> 01:22:37.550
nor the computer as a
kind of automated teacher

01:22:37.550 --> 01:22:40.400
that treats the child as a kind
of answering machine-- now,

01:22:40.400 --> 01:22:42.559
what's seven plus eight?

01:22:42.559 --> 01:22:43.100
You're right.

01:22:43.100 --> 01:22:44.180
You wrong.

01:22:44.180 --> 01:22:46.510
Next question, next answer.

01:22:46.510 --> 01:22:48.500
We'd like to think
of the computer

01:22:48.500 --> 01:22:51.152
as a medium of construction.

01:22:54.590 --> 01:23:02.360
We saw those pictures of robots.

01:23:02.360 --> 01:23:04.070
We have children making--

01:23:04.070 --> 01:23:05.667
they're not as
elaborate as that.

01:23:05.667 --> 01:23:07.250
We're beginning to
have children being

01:23:07.250 --> 01:23:11.970
able to make that kind of
robot in a simplified form.

01:23:11.970 --> 01:23:14.490
We have children
programming the computer,

01:23:14.490 --> 01:23:16.530
making their own
video games, if that's

01:23:16.530 --> 01:23:18.930
what they're interested in.

01:23:18.930 --> 01:23:20.910
That is, they are
taking this technology

01:23:20.910 --> 01:23:24.150
and using it as a
constructive medium

01:23:24.150 --> 01:23:27.540
to carry out
projects that are not

01:23:27.540 --> 01:23:30.390
any more complex than
children have ever

01:23:30.390 --> 01:23:32.700
been able to do
in the past except

01:23:32.700 --> 01:23:35.760
on the psychological
plane of learning to maybe

01:23:35.760 --> 01:23:37.020
be part of a family.

01:23:37.020 --> 01:23:41.910
But in terms of having
matter and projects

01:23:41.910 --> 01:23:45.300
with the technical scientific
and mathematical content,

01:23:45.300 --> 01:23:47.760
until recently,
there was very little

01:23:47.760 --> 01:23:52.770
that a child could do that had
serious content of that sort

01:23:52.770 --> 01:23:56.990
that could be carried out
as a long-term project

01:23:56.990 --> 01:23:59.360
so that you could get the
feel of what it's like to make

01:23:59.360 --> 01:24:02.660
something really complex
work and run into the bugs

01:24:02.660 --> 01:24:05.690
and difficulties that
we saw a sample of

01:24:05.690 --> 01:24:10.610
and how to deal with now to
construct these Jurassic Park

01:24:10.610 --> 01:24:12.610
robots.

01:24:12.610 --> 01:24:15.330
In doing this, we've
worked with companies.

01:24:15.330 --> 01:24:19.350
We worked especially with
LEGO, the makers of LEGO,

01:24:19.350 --> 01:24:21.750
and one of the thrusts
of our work with LEGO

01:24:21.750 --> 01:24:29.130
has been developing a tiny
little computer which you could

01:24:29.130 --> 01:24:34.390
put inside a LEGO
model and sensors

01:24:34.390 --> 01:24:37.390
so this computer could
know about the environment,

01:24:37.390 --> 01:24:40.930
could detect light,
sound, touch, heat.

01:24:40.930 --> 01:24:47.830
And so you could make out of
LEGO extended in this way,

01:24:47.830 --> 01:24:50.620
you could make a real
robot that would really

01:24:50.620 --> 01:24:53.850
do something under
its own control

01:24:53.850 --> 01:24:56.730
with its little brain inside it.

01:24:56.730 --> 01:24:59.100
And that's been a
long, long haul,

01:24:59.100 --> 01:25:02.460
and I believe that
early next year

01:25:02.460 --> 01:25:04.560
or late this year,
they're actually

01:25:04.560 --> 01:25:08.360
going to put one of these
devices on the market.

01:25:08.360 --> 01:25:11.740
But in the last 15
years, we've been

01:25:11.740 --> 01:25:15.950
working with children
with prototypes of this,

01:25:15.950 --> 01:25:18.075
and we've actually got
some fancier ones that

01:25:18.075 --> 01:25:22.340
are much smaller, and it's quite
amazing what children can do.

01:25:22.340 --> 01:25:26.550
As examples of
that kind of thing,

01:25:26.550 --> 01:25:32.430
I worked one summer with two
girls-- one about six to seven,

01:25:32.430 --> 01:25:33.990
and the other about eight.

01:25:33.990 --> 01:25:38.370
And we worked together
on several afternoons

01:25:38.370 --> 01:25:40.740
a week for several
weeks in the summer.

01:25:40.740 --> 01:25:46.620
What they wanted to make was a
cat and a kitten out of LEGO.

01:25:46.620 --> 01:25:49.130
And they sort of dressed them
up, and they made whiskers.

01:25:49.130 --> 01:25:52.460
But also, inside the cat and
the kitten was real behavior.

01:25:52.460 --> 01:25:55.290
They were not only
constructing structures,

01:25:55.290 --> 01:25:57.320
they were actually
constructing behavior.

01:25:57.320 --> 01:26:01.940
And the behavior they wanted
was when the kitten squeaked,

01:26:01.940 --> 01:26:05.530
the cat would come to it.

01:26:05.530 --> 01:26:07.230
Well, how do you do that?

01:26:10.100 --> 01:26:12.770
Engineers know how to do that.

01:26:12.770 --> 01:26:14.690
Hardly anybody in
our society who

01:26:14.690 --> 01:26:17.570
hasn't been through engineering
school knows how to do that,

01:26:17.570 --> 01:26:22.340
but six-year-olds can
know how to do that.

01:26:22.340 --> 01:26:25.610
And they can understand
ideas of how to use sensors,

01:26:25.610 --> 01:26:30.090
how to use the idea of feedback,
how to control a process,

01:26:30.090 --> 01:26:31.590
how to do all sorts
of stuff that we

01:26:31.590 --> 01:26:35.990
teach in college-level
engineering courses on control

01:26:35.990 --> 01:26:36.920
engineering.

01:26:36.920 --> 01:26:40.070
They can do it, and
this kind of stuff

01:26:40.070 --> 01:26:44.750
is more accessible and easier
to learn than the dead language

01:26:44.750 --> 01:26:47.390
kind of mathematics we try
to teach them in school,

01:26:47.390 --> 01:26:51.050
and it includes it, because
in the course of working

01:26:51.050 --> 01:26:53.180
on making these
devices, they run

01:26:53.180 --> 01:26:59.330
into fractions and decimals
and shapes and configurations

01:26:59.330 --> 01:27:00.980
and all the things
that are normally

01:27:00.980 --> 01:27:04.891
treated like a dead language
in the school math and science

01:27:04.891 --> 01:27:05.390
classes.

01:27:05.390 --> 01:27:06.920
They learn like a
living language,

01:27:06.920 --> 01:27:08.640
like living in the country.

01:27:08.640 --> 01:27:11.720
So they learn math like
living in Mathland,

01:27:11.720 --> 01:27:15.200
which is the place which is
to mathematics like France

01:27:15.200 --> 01:27:19.100
is to French, where you learn
the language by speaking it,

01:27:19.100 --> 01:27:22.400
not by being taught
it in a classroom.

01:27:22.400 --> 01:27:26.090
And we see in this
a transformation

01:27:26.090 --> 01:27:31.760
of the kind of play activities
that children can do.

01:27:31.760 --> 01:27:34.390
But now to say
something about play--

01:27:34.390 --> 01:27:38.860
when I looked into that
school, what struck me,

01:27:38.860 --> 01:27:42.487
even when there was activity
that the teachers called play,

01:27:42.487 --> 01:27:43.570
I wouldn't call that play.

01:27:43.570 --> 01:27:44.656
It was play play.

01:27:44.656 --> 01:27:46.060
It was play play play.

01:27:46.060 --> 01:27:51.340
It wasn't even real play because
real play is the intensity

01:27:51.340 --> 01:27:54.930
that my little grandson had
when he was really engaged.

01:27:54.930 --> 01:27:58.450
Real play is the intensity
that my graduate students

01:27:58.450 --> 01:28:00.910
have when they are really
immersed in a project,

01:28:00.910 --> 01:28:03.430
playing with ideas
and are trying

01:28:03.430 --> 01:28:05.500
to explore and decide
how to do something,

01:28:05.500 --> 01:28:08.630
getting really excited.

01:28:08.630 --> 01:28:13.270
There's a kind of play
that we usually call work,

01:28:13.270 --> 01:28:16.450
and that's why I think
this title that I

01:28:16.450 --> 01:28:24.360
tried to give this talk of
play as child's work is--

01:28:24.360 --> 01:28:29.230
I think it points to a degree of
seriousness about serious play.

01:28:29.230 --> 01:28:31.300
And serious play
isn't making the child

01:28:31.300 --> 01:28:35.410
a kind of too-serious
person who doesn't know

01:28:35.410 --> 01:28:36.940
how to laugh and fool around.

01:28:36.940 --> 01:28:40.810
On the contrary, it's full of
laughing and fooling around,

01:28:40.810 --> 01:28:42.400
just like the best work is.

01:28:45.410 --> 01:28:49.610
So I think these two
things come together.

01:28:49.610 --> 01:28:51.680
An example of how they
might come together

01:28:51.680 --> 01:28:57.350
is a graduate student
working under my direction

01:28:57.350 --> 01:29:04.760
now, Michele Evard, is
doing a project that's

01:29:04.760 --> 01:29:08.210
a prime example of how these
two sides of the information

01:29:08.210 --> 01:29:09.470
process come together.

01:29:09.470 --> 01:29:15.200
She's working in an inner-city
elementary school in Boston,

01:29:15.200 --> 01:29:22.280
where for a number of years
we've had enough computers

01:29:22.280 --> 01:29:26.800
so that kids can have,
say, an hour or two a day

01:29:26.800 --> 01:29:28.060
working at the computers.

01:29:28.060 --> 01:29:30.140
And they do all sorts
of things with them,

01:29:30.140 --> 01:29:32.500
but one kind of
project that we've

01:29:32.500 --> 01:29:36.520
tried to foster there
is a project where,

01:29:36.520 --> 01:29:42.160
for a whole year for
that hour a day, say,

01:29:42.160 --> 01:29:43.790
you'll be working
on our project.

01:29:43.790 --> 01:29:46.180
So you get a feel of
what it's like to pursue

01:29:46.180 --> 01:29:48.340
a project over a long
enough period of time

01:29:48.340 --> 01:29:52.030
for it to inhabit you,
so that when you wake up

01:29:52.030 --> 01:29:53.710
in the morning, you
get an idea for it.

01:29:53.710 --> 01:29:55.990
So that you've got enough
time to try something out,

01:29:55.990 --> 01:29:59.520
find it's wrong, and
then try something else.

01:29:59.520 --> 01:30:01.540
The kind of project
that we've done

01:30:01.540 --> 01:30:04.810
many kinds of projects, some
of them involving this LEGO.

01:30:04.810 --> 01:30:12.520
This particular research
investigation of Michele Evard

01:30:12.520 --> 01:30:16.460
is about one that I mentioned
before-- making your own video

01:30:16.460 --> 01:30:17.140
game.

01:30:17.140 --> 01:30:19.462
The kids are going
to make a game,

01:30:19.462 --> 01:30:20.920
and together with
the game, they're

01:30:20.920 --> 01:30:24.460
going to make the advertising
material with the instruction

01:30:24.460 --> 01:30:25.972
book for how to use it.

01:30:25.972 --> 01:30:27.430
They're going to
present at the end

01:30:27.430 --> 01:30:31.300
of the year at a
kind of game fair

01:30:31.300 --> 01:30:34.570
where they and other
kids and parents

01:30:34.570 --> 01:30:36.250
and anyone who wants
to come will come,

01:30:36.250 --> 01:30:41.560
and they will each try to
sell their particular game.

01:30:41.560 --> 01:30:47.009
Well, one of the problems about
that way of learning is access.

01:30:47.009 --> 01:30:48.550
Where does the
information come from?

01:30:48.550 --> 01:30:52.550
And how do you get an
answer to a question?

01:30:52.550 --> 01:30:56.810
There you are trying
to make something move,

01:30:56.810 --> 01:30:59.960
make a figure jump.

01:30:59.960 --> 01:31:02.520
And you think,
well, what's a jump?

01:31:02.520 --> 01:31:03.380
What's it look like?

01:31:03.380 --> 01:31:06.660
And how do you describe it?

01:31:06.660 --> 01:31:09.500
Well, you say, teacher,
teacher, but the teacher's

01:31:09.500 --> 01:31:11.740
only got limited knowledge
and only limited time.

01:31:14.410 --> 01:31:16.840
What Michele Evard's
project is about

01:31:16.840 --> 01:31:21.490
is breaking down this
incredible inefficiency

01:31:21.490 --> 01:31:26.020
of having one teacher to
30 learners or 20 learners.

01:31:26.020 --> 01:31:27.640
It's crazy.

01:31:27.640 --> 01:31:31.790
Why don't we have 30
teachers and 30 learners?

01:31:31.790 --> 01:31:33.710
Well, this is a move
in that direction,

01:31:33.710 --> 01:31:35.890
that if you're stuck
with this problem,

01:31:35.890 --> 01:31:38.600
you might ask the kid next to
you, you might ask the teacher,

01:31:38.600 --> 01:31:42.040
but if you don't get
something immediate,

01:31:42.040 --> 01:31:44.260
there's another route that
you might prefer, anyway.

01:31:44.260 --> 01:31:49.450
She's constructed a
kind of net system.

01:31:49.450 --> 01:31:51.910
You throw your question
into cyberspace,

01:31:51.910 --> 01:31:53.190
so you put it in the computer.

01:31:53.190 --> 01:31:56.500
And then there's a
community of people

01:31:56.500 --> 01:32:01.041
that might include kids who
did this the year before,

01:32:01.041 --> 01:32:02.790
or it might include a
kid at the other end

01:32:02.790 --> 01:32:04.890
of the class who
you've never spoken to,

01:32:04.890 --> 01:32:06.870
or somebody outside altogether.

01:32:06.870 --> 01:32:10.410
But you throw your
problem out, and then we

01:32:10.410 --> 01:32:15.270
see that there are a lot
of kids who really enjoy

01:32:15.270 --> 01:32:17.967
acting as consultants and
picking up other people's

01:32:17.967 --> 01:32:19.800
problems and thinking
about them and drawing

01:32:19.800 --> 01:32:21.310
on their own experience.

01:32:21.310 --> 01:32:26.010
And we've noticed
through getting

01:32:26.010 --> 01:32:29.070
this access to being able
to consult with this larger

01:32:29.070 --> 01:32:34.080
community, a very marked
improvement in the quality

01:32:34.080 --> 01:32:37.620
of the work these kids can do.

01:32:37.620 --> 01:32:42.650
So we see them putting
the two things together.

01:32:42.650 --> 01:32:45.920
This is a kind of net
information access,

01:32:45.920 --> 01:32:49.070
person access kind of
function with having

01:32:49.070 --> 01:32:52.520
a real, hard, technical
kind of project to work on.

01:32:52.520 --> 01:32:57.680
Put them together, and the
kind of mindless surfing

01:32:57.680 --> 01:32:59.450
of the web that
you often get gets

01:32:59.450 --> 01:33:02.600
replaced by a purposeful
search for information

01:33:02.600 --> 01:33:06.880
that you need for a project
you're really engage in.

01:33:06.880 --> 01:33:09.370
And both come up,
and we're beginning

01:33:09.370 --> 01:33:11.710
to see a kind of
chemical reaction

01:33:11.710 --> 01:33:14.590
there that I think is
a little model of what

01:33:14.590 --> 01:33:19.690
the alternative to so-called
curriculum-based environment,

01:33:19.690 --> 01:33:21.850
curriculum-based
learning will be.

01:33:21.850 --> 01:33:24.460
And I think that
curriculum-based learning

01:33:24.460 --> 01:33:28.510
is going to disappear.

01:33:28.510 --> 01:33:32.140
Certainly, what I'm
sure will disappear

01:33:32.140 --> 01:33:35.560
is this perverse idea that
we should segregate people

01:33:35.560 --> 01:33:38.820
by ages and levels of knowledge.

01:33:38.820 --> 01:33:41.140
Yeah, it's amazing how
we take it for granted

01:33:41.140 --> 01:33:44.500
that it's natural that
you should be first grade,

01:33:44.500 --> 01:33:47.895
all these kids together; second
grade, all these kids together.

01:33:47.895 --> 01:33:48.395
Why?

01:33:48.395 --> 01:33:50.940
I mean, if I told you
to segregate yourself,

01:33:50.940 --> 01:33:54.350
and I won't talk to you unless
everybody between 20 and 25

01:33:54.350 --> 01:33:56.510
sits there--

01:33:56.510 --> 01:33:59.390
you'd think I was crazy,
and you wouldn't do it.

01:33:59.390 --> 01:34:02.220
Why do we do this in school?

01:34:02.220 --> 01:34:05.610
Now there's only one reason,
and that is that in the past,

01:34:05.610 --> 01:34:08.330
the only way we had of
getting this knowledge out

01:34:08.330 --> 01:34:11.230
was systematizing it
and spoon-feeding it

01:34:11.230 --> 01:34:13.290
in a kind of
industrial production.

01:34:13.290 --> 01:34:16.760
So what we're seeing there
is a production line,

01:34:16.760 --> 01:34:19.880
an old industrial-style
production line.

01:34:19.880 --> 01:34:22.120
And there's no other
justification for it.

01:34:22.120 --> 01:34:27.450
And people say, if you ask them,
well, it's for socialization.

01:34:27.450 --> 01:34:32.070
Gosh, well, it sounds
to me like what

01:34:32.070 --> 01:34:33.930
happens is when
you're seven, you

01:34:33.930 --> 01:34:36.540
spend your whole seventh
year learning how

01:34:36.540 --> 01:34:39.450
to deal with kids of age seven.

01:34:39.450 --> 01:34:40.950
And then in typical
school fashion,

01:34:40.950 --> 01:34:43.140
you never deal with that again.

01:34:43.140 --> 01:34:46.500
Having learned it, you
get onto the next thing,

01:34:46.500 --> 01:34:48.250
and you never see a
seven-year-old again,

01:34:48.250 --> 01:34:50.624
but now you've got to learn
to deal with eight-year-olds.

01:34:50.624 --> 01:34:55.540
[LAUGHTER]

01:34:55.540 --> 01:34:59.290
What this deprives
you of is that mixture

01:34:59.290 --> 01:35:02.470
of skills and levels of
understanding where people

01:35:02.470 --> 01:35:05.080
can be in a real
learning community

01:35:05.080 --> 01:35:07.810
where people who know a lot
and people who know a little

01:35:07.810 --> 01:35:11.410
are all together, and each
side learns and expands

01:35:11.410 --> 01:35:15.550
their knowledge by
exchanging with the others.

01:35:15.550 --> 01:35:18.280
And everywhere, that's
the better thing to do.

01:35:18.280 --> 01:35:20.600
It's not only in this
meeting and your lives,

01:35:20.600 --> 01:35:24.550
it's in these great
old extended families

01:35:24.550 --> 01:35:29.110
that we so deplore
the disappearance of.

01:35:29.110 --> 01:35:35.030
In one place, there would be
parents, children, grandparents

01:35:35.030 --> 01:35:36.620
all together, living together.

01:35:36.620 --> 01:35:40.180
And we thought it was wonderful
that these communities existed,

01:35:40.180 --> 01:35:42.490
and we deplore the fact
they're disappearing.

01:35:42.490 --> 01:35:46.350
But they weren't
segregated by age.

01:35:46.350 --> 01:35:49.200
Everybody got richer
lives and richer learning

01:35:49.200 --> 01:35:52.150
because they were all together.

01:35:52.150 --> 01:35:54.300
And I think the perversion of--

01:35:56.884 --> 01:35:58.050
well, it's not a perversion.

01:35:58.050 --> 01:36:00.008
It's only a perversion
if you think that that's

01:36:00.008 --> 01:36:01.740
the natural thing to do.

01:36:01.740 --> 01:36:06.210
I want to recognize the
whole structure of school,

01:36:06.210 --> 01:36:09.660
the idea of grades,
the idea of curriculum

01:36:09.660 --> 01:36:13.770
is a way of dealing
with a now-obsolete or

01:36:13.770 --> 01:36:19.800
rapidly-becoming obsolescent
kind of relationship

01:36:19.800 --> 01:36:22.700
with knowledge and
its dissemination,

01:36:22.700 --> 01:36:23.960
and all that should go.

01:36:23.960 --> 01:36:26.900
So technology and
education doesn't mean

01:36:26.900 --> 01:36:30.020
using it to improve the system.

01:36:30.020 --> 01:36:32.420
It's very hard to get this
through people's heads.

01:36:32.420 --> 01:36:36.020
I give talks like this to
meetings of superintendents

01:36:36.020 --> 01:36:37.790
of schools, and at
the end, somebody

01:36:37.790 --> 01:36:41.180
says, but tell me concretely,
how do you think in the year

01:36:41.180 --> 01:36:45.440
2020 we will teach,
use computers to teach

01:36:45.440 --> 01:36:48.747
the fourth-grade
math curriculum?

01:36:48.747 --> 01:36:50.330
And you'll want to
tear your hair out.

01:36:50.330 --> 01:36:51.860
There won't be any fourth grade.

01:36:51.860 --> 01:36:52.880
We won't be teaching.

01:36:52.880 --> 01:36:53.760
We'll be learning.

01:36:53.760 --> 01:36:55.800
There won't be a separate
subject called math.

01:36:55.800 --> 01:36:57.790
It will be part of--

01:36:57.790 --> 01:37:01.880
but these categories are
so deeply ingrained in us,

01:37:01.880 --> 01:37:04.230
we need a serious
struggle to break out,

01:37:04.230 --> 01:37:06.800
and that's something that
we can all do and make

01:37:06.800 --> 01:37:12.790
a big difference in the world
by fighting these categories

01:37:12.790 --> 01:37:14.847
and just calling
them in question.

01:37:14.847 --> 01:37:16.930
And maybe we don't even
have to say they're wrong.

01:37:16.930 --> 01:37:20.850
You only have to say,
well, think about it.

01:37:20.850 --> 01:37:24.050
Have you thought about whether
they could be gotten away with?

01:37:27.610 --> 01:37:33.540
I'm going to use the rest of
my time on that word world.

01:37:33.540 --> 01:37:37.050
And it's impossible to talk
about things like this,

01:37:37.050 --> 01:37:39.750
about the role of technology
and expensive machinery,

01:37:39.750 --> 01:37:42.630
without facing the fact
that the world doesn't

01:37:42.630 --> 01:37:48.270
consist of economically
homogeneous people.

01:37:48.270 --> 01:37:54.030
There are about 20 countries
in the world in which I would

01:37:54.030 --> 01:37:56.610
say there's a
process afoot that's

01:37:56.610 --> 01:37:59.430
bringing computers and
connectivity into the lives

01:37:59.430 --> 01:38:00.810
of most children.

01:38:00.810 --> 01:38:03.270
They don't all get it,
and we're very far from it

01:38:03.270 --> 01:38:04.950
in the United
States, and we should

01:38:04.950 --> 01:38:07.120
be thoroughly
ashamed of ourselves

01:38:07.120 --> 01:38:10.310
that our President can
stand up and suggest

01:38:10.310 --> 01:38:12.220
that putting one
computer in each school

01:38:12.220 --> 01:38:17.790
or in each classroom is a worthy
objective for our country.

01:38:17.790 --> 01:38:19.930
A computer on every
desk and another one

01:38:19.930 --> 01:38:24.040
in every home for every child
would be maybe a little better,

01:38:24.040 --> 01:38:26.770
and we could afford it.

01:38:26.770 --> 01:38:30.970
But anyway, past that, there
are 180 other countries

01:38:30.970 --> 01:38:36.560
in the world where the chance
of children getting access

01:38:36.560 --> 01:38:40.610
to any of this is extremely
slim unless something is done.

01:38:40.610 --> 01:38:47.810
And so a few of us have started
a foundation which we call 2B1.

01:38:47.810 --> 01:38:50.060
Does that technology work?

01:38:50.060 --> 01:38:56.300
You can look on its
website, www.2B1.org.

01:38:56.300 --> 01:39:00.230
The goal of this
foundation is to promote

01:39:00.230 --> 01:39:03.470
every way we can find
of bringing access

01:39:03.470 --> 01:39:09.770
to computers and connectivity to
those parts of the world where,

01:39:09.770 --> 01:39:12.920
for reasons of geographic
isolation, poverty,

01:39:12.920 --> 01:39:16.340
political opposition,
whatever the reason,

01:39:16.340 --> 01:39:19.910
where there are obstacles.

01:39:19.910 --> 01:39:25.610
And the first action of this
foundation is going to happen

01:39:25.610 --> 01:39:30.140
this July, when we're going
to try to bring together right

01:39:30.140 --> 01:39:33.830
here-- well, not in this
room, but in this campus--

01:39:33.830 --> 01:39:36.030
we're trying to round
up at the moment

01:39:36.030 --> 01:39:39.910
100 activists from
developing countries.

01:39:39.910 --> 01:39:43.280
And by activists, we mean
people who, out there,

01:39:43.280 --> 01:39:46.460
against all odds, have done
something bringing computers

01:39:46.460 --> 01:39:49.220
to kids where it seemed
very unlikely that they

01:39:49.220 --> 01:39:51.700
would get them.

01:39:51.700 --> 01:39:55.750
And the more what they
did is Mission Impossible,

01:39:55.750 --> 01:39:59.530
the more we like them, and
we're trying to cast out a net

01:39:59.530 --> 01:40:02.710
and get 100 such people
and invite them in here.

01:40:02.710 --> 01:40:07.770
And we would have a six-day
brainstorming workshop where

01:40:07.770 --> 01:40:09.760
the 100 activists--

01:40:09.760 --> 01:40:13.480
together with 100
other people who

01:40:13.480 --> 01:40:15.610
come along with
technological knowledge,

01:40:15.610 --> 01:40:18.370
with experience in
organizing, with ideas

01:40:18.370 --> 01:40:23.620
about learning, with ideas about
funding, with other resources--

01:40:23.620 --> 01:40:26.380
can come together in
a mix from which we

01:40:26.380 --> 01:40:29.710
would like to
generate new ideas,

01:40:29.710 --> 01:40:33.950
spreading the ideas
that already exist.

01:40:33.950 --> 01:40:37.030
The beginnings of a
community formation

01:40:37.030 --> 01:40:42.310
of trying to move
this extension of,

01:40:42.310 --> 01:40:48.610
move this wonderful thing we
call digital technology out

01:40:48.610 --> 01:40:55.340
of the range of the
elite, the elite countries

01:40:55.340 --> 01:40:59.210
and elite people within
countries, to that wider world.

01:40:59.210 --> 01:41:04.190
And we sort of feel,
romantic as it might sound,

01:41:04.190 --> 01:41:06.170
that children come
into the world

01:41:06.170 --> 01:41:13.880
without a sense of
hatred, isolation,

01:41:13.880 --> 01:41:17.350
and that they want to
preserve the environment,

01:41:17.350 --> 01:41:20.060
and they like the
world they live in.

01:41:20.060 --> 01:41:25.480
And if we can get children into
a new kind of internationalism

01:41:25.480 --> 01:41:29.680
through knowing more about the
world, through connectivity

01:41:29.680 --> 01:41:32.170
and knowing more about
ways of knowledge that

01:41:32.170 --> 01:41:37.340
have been our privilege, this
might make a better world.

01:41:37.340 --> 01:41:38.440
And so the name 2B1--

01:41:42.700 --> 01:41:45.370
you can read it all
sorts of ways you like,

01:41:45.370 --> 01:41:53.650
but written like that expresses
a political and social ideology

01:41:53.650 --> 01:41:56.620
as well as an intent
to do something,

01:41:56.620 --> 01:42:01.060
to change the learning
environment of the planet.

01:42:01.060 --> 01:42:03.510
So an appeal-- look at that.

01:42:03.510 --> 01:42:07.010
If you know people, or if
you have any contact anywhere

01:42:07.010 --> 01:42:11.710
so we can find one
more of these activists

01:42:11.710 --> 01:42:16.130
in some unlikely place, you'll
be doing a real service--

01:42:16.130 --> 01:42:19.640
not just to us and to
yourself, but to everybody

01:42:19.640 --> 01:42:21.365
and next generations
of this planet.

01:42:27.530 --> 01:42:30.770
I think that's the time
I'm supposed to stop,

01:42:30.770 --> 01:42:32.140
so I'll stop there.

01:42:32.140 --> 01:42:34.390
I don't know how many minutes
there are for questions.

01:42:34.390 --> 01:42:37.855
[APPLAUSE]

01:42:50.725 --> 01:42:51.715
Thank you.

01:43:02.620 --> 01:43:06.190
CRAWLEY: I think it's time for
that fine tradition in baseball

01:43:06.190 --> 01:43:08.380
called the seventh
inning stretch.

01:43:08.380 --> 01:43:11.742
So if you'd just like to stand
in your seat for one second--

01:43:11.742 --> 01:43:12.700
OK, that's long enough.

01:43:12.700 --> 01:43:13.480
Now you can sit down.

01:43:13.480 --> 01:43:14.313
Thank you very much.

01:43:16.950 --> 01:43:18.110
I'm Ed Crawley.

01:43:18.110 --> 01:43:19.741
That really was just a second.

01:43:26.430 --> 01:43:29.490
Welcome to Technology Day.

01:43:29.490 --> 01:43:30.300
I'm Ed Crawley.

01:43:30.300 --> 01:43:33.900
I'm the rocket scientist
here at MIT, the real one.

01:43:33.900 --> 01:43:36.947
I'm the head of the
Aero-Astro Department,

01:43:36.947 --> 01:43:38.530
and what I'd like
to talk to you today

01:43:38.530 --> 01:43:45.320
about is airplanes, baseballs,
and the fun of flight.

01:43:49.112 --> 01:43:50.570
Now to commemorate
this event, I've

01:43:50.570 --> 01:43:54.887
worn my baseball cap, which
I wear when I'm flying,

01:43:54.887 --> 01:43:57.220
or is it my flying cap that
I wear when I play baseball?

01:43:57.220 --> 01:43:58.930
I'm not quite sure.

01:43:58.930 --> 01:44:01.720
What do we do at MIT?

01:44:01.720 --> 01:44:05.864
We develop technology, and we
invent things that never were.

01:44:05.864 --> 01:44:09.720
[LAUGHTER]

01:44:09.720 --> 01:44:11.370
Isn't that what we do?

01:44:11.370 --> 01:44:14.310
Science is the discovery of
things that always have been.

01:44:14.310 --> 01:44:16.980
Technology as the inventions
of things that never were--

01:44:16.980 --> 01:44:19.590
Theodore von Kármán.

01:44:19.590 --> 01:44:22.050
We encourage learning.

01:44:22.050 --> 01:44:24.060
Sometimes we call this teaching.

01:44:24.060 --> 01:44:26.790
But of course, the real
objective is learning.

01:44:26.790 --> 01:44:29.130
We make contributions
to society,

01:44:29.130 --> 01:44:31.950
and we form the future.

01:44:31.950 --> 01:44:35.190
Those are modest goals that
we have here with technology.

01:44:35.190 --> 01:44:39.920
Well, that's what we do, and
that's the outline of my talk.

01:44:39.920 --> 01:44:42.910
So first, to have a
short advertisement,

01:44:42.910 --> 01:44:45.730
I'd like to tell you
about my department, which

01:44:45.730 --> 01:44:48.690
is called Aero-Astro,
Aeronautics and Astronautics,

01:44:48.690 --> 01:44:53.230
course 16, whose objectives
are to advance and disseminate

01:44:53.230 --> 01:44:57.250
knowledge of the principles
and conception, design,

01:44:57.250 --> 01:45:01.450
implementation, and operation
of things within its domain.

01:45:01.450 --> 01:45:09.270
And its domain starts
here and goes up.

01:45:09.270 --> 01:45:11.801
So it includes aircraft,
launch vehicles, spacecraft,

01:45:11.801 --> 01:45:13.550
things that go on the
surface of the moon,

01:45:13.550 --> 01:45:16.010
things that go on
the surface of Mars,

01:45:16.010 --> 01:45:18.630
photons which pass
through that medium--

01:45:18.630 --> 01:45:20.690
we're in the
telecommunication business--

01:45:20.690 --> 01:45:24.730
and, of course, baseballs,
softballs, tennis balls,

01:45:24.730 --> 01:45:26.420
and such.

01:45:26.420 --> 01:45:30.000
Now is this a surprise
that we should

01:45:30.000 --> 01:45:33.060
think about sports and
baseballs in the Department

01:45:33.060 --> 01:45:34.500
of Aeronautics and Astronautics?

01:45:34.500 --> 01:45:37.360
I don't think so.

01:45:37.360 --> 01:45:40.540
Go out on Briggs Field
on a Saturday afternoon,

01:45:40.540 --> 01:45:42.220
and what do you find
the students doing?

01:45:42.220 --> 01:45:44.620
Enjoying themselves
by playing sports.

01:45:44.620 --> 01:45:46.420
They go home, they watch sports.

01:45:46.420 --> 01:45:48.520
You know, of course, all
the dorms are now wired

01:45:48.520 --> 01:45:50.960
with cable TV and the web.

01:45:50.960 --> 01:45:53.080
And of course, we know
what they use those for,

01:45:53.080 --> 01:45:54.790
which is not getting their
homework assignments.

01:45:54.790 --> 01:45:57.010
It's really watching the
Saturday afternoon baseball

01:45:57.010 --> 01:45:57.850
game.

01:45:57.850 --> 01:46:01.690
Well Aero-Astro is
also exciting and cool.

01:46:01.690 --> 01:46:03.840
The students really love it.

01:46:03.840 --> 01:46:05.400
We get students
in our department

01:46:05.400 --> 01:46:08.190
who by and large are
those students who

01:46:08.190 --> 01:46:10.770
rode their bicycle down to the
airport when they were young

01:46:10.770 --> 01:46:13.510
and watched the
airplanes take off,

01:46:13.510 --> 01:46:17.190
who played hooky from school to
watch the space vehicles take

01:46:17.190 --> 01:46:17.890
off.

01:46:17.890 --> 01:46:19.660
There's an excitement
about our business,

01:46:19.660 --> 01:46:22.030
as there's an
excitement about sports.

01:46:22.030 --> 01:46:26.020
Sports, if you haven't
noticed, are pretty high-tech.

01:46:26.020 --> 01:46:30.400
The second largest consumer
of graphite epoxy and titanium

01:46:30.400 --> 01:46:32.510
materials in the United
States, for example,

01:46:32.510 --> 01:46:34.450
as a measure of
high-tech material usage,

01:46:34.450 --> 01:46:37.050
is the sporting goods industry.

01:46:37.050 --> 01:46:40.020
The largest usage of
graphite epoxy and titanium

01:46:40.020 --> 01:46:44.900
in the United States is
the Aero-Astro business.

01:46:44.900 --> 01:46:48.280
And if you really wanted
to build a sporting good--

01:46:48.280 --> 01:46:51.450
I just happen to have one
here that I'll talk about

01:46:51.450 --> 01:46:53.670
later-- if you really
wanted to build this,

01:46:53.670 --> 01:46:56.250
what would you
need to know about?

01:46:56.250 --> 01:47:00.870
Well, you'd need to know
about lightweight structures.

01:47:00.870 --> 01:47:03.400
It has to be light.

01:47:03.400 --> 01:47:07.150
Aerodynamics-- it has
to pass through the air.

01:47:07.150 --> 01:47:09.880
Human factors-- it has
to have a good grip,

01:47:09.880 --> 01:47:12.230
can't give you tennis elbow.

01:47:12.230 --> 01:47:15.607
Information-- maybe.

01:47:15.607 --> 01:47:17.690
How long will it be before
you buy a tennis racket

01:47:17.690 --> 01:47:19.482
with a microprocessor in it?

01:47:19.482 --> 01:47:23.770
[LAUGHTER]

01:47:23.770 --> 01:47:24.821
A year or two.

01:47:24.821 --> 01:47:27.530
[LAUGHTER]

01:47:27.530 --> 01:47:30.260
And of course, you have
to know about design.

01:47:30.260 --> 01:47:34.100
And what do you need to do
to know about Aero-Astro,

01:47:34.100 --> 01:47:35.720
to build things in Aero-Astro?

01:47:35.720 --> 01:47:37.636
You need to know about
lightweight structures,

01:47:37.636 --> 01:47:39.560
like airplanes and
spacecraft; aerodynamics

01:47:39.560 --> 01:47:42.110
to pass through the atmosphere;
human factors in order

01:47:42.110 --> 01:47:44.840
to satisfy the
pilots; information

01:47:44.840 --> 01:47:48.030
processing and flow;
and, of course, design.

01:47:48.030 --> 01:47:51.560
So is it any surprise that
people who do Aero-Astro

01:47:51.560 --> 01:47:53.765
are also interested
in sporting equipment?

01:47:53.765 --> 01:47:55.160
It shouldn't be.

01:47:55.160 --> 01:47:58.170
In fact, when I took a poll
in preparation for this talk,

01:47:58.170 --> 01:48:02.780
I found that more than 50% of
the faculty in our department

01:48:02.780 --> 01:48:06.140
had an avocational interest
in sporting good design.

01:48:06.140 --> 01:48:07.071
They had consulted.

01:48:07.071 --> 01:48:07.820
They had designed.

01:48:07.820 --> 01:48:10.154
They had helped set
standards, and so forth.

01:48:10.154 --> 01:48:11.570
So I want to show
you a little bit

01:48:11.570 --> 01:48:15.630
about the technology of sports.

01:48:15.630 --> 01:48:17.980
We'll have the first video.

01:48:21.740 --> 01:48:24.980
This is a high-speed
photo of a skier,

01:48:24.980 --> 01:48:30.200
and I want you to look at
the amount of vibration

01:48:30.200 --> 01:48:34.120
there is in these skis.

01:48:34.120 --> 01:48:36.510
Watch this.

01:48:36.510 --> 01:48:38.700
In fact, you turn on skis.

01:48:38.700 --> 01:48:40.410
You turn on the edge of skis--

01:48:40.410 --> 01:48:41.500
we all know that.

01:48:41.500 --> 01:48:43.380
But you actually turn
on the edge of skis--

01:48:43.380 --> 01:48:45.357
look at this guy's
leg, by the way.

01:48:45.357 --> 01:48:47.190
If you don't think there
isn't human factors

01:48:47.190 --> 01:48:48.690
going on in there.

01:48:48.690 --> 01:48:50.340
You turn on this
edge of a ski that's

01:48:50.340 --> 01:48:51.822
on contact with the snow.

01:48:51.822 --> 01:48:54.030
But you can look at this
and see that there's usually

01:48:54.030 --> 01:48:55.530
about a centimeter
of displacement

01:48:55.530 --> 01:48:58.620
between the snow
and the ski edge.

01:48:58.620 --> 01:49:01.220
Look at this guy's leg.

01:49:01.220 --> 01:49:05.510
Skis, structures,
dynamics, human factors.

01:49:05.510 --> 01:49:06.980
Watch the tennis ball go by.

01:49:06.980 --> 01:49:09.290
That was in modestly
slow motion.

01:49:09.290 --> 01:49:11.510
You're a line judge
at the US Open,

01:49:11.510 --> 01:49:13.910
and you have to
call that, right?

01:49:13.910 --> 01:49:16.820
Now, here it is in slow-mo.

01:49:16.820 --> 01:49:19.460
And those two little lines,
of course, represent the line.

01:49:19.460 --> 01:49:23.660
This is human perception
and ball dynamics.

01:49:23.660 --> 01:49:28.580
The rule actually is if any
of the footprint of the ball

01:49:28.580 --> 01:49:31.250
touches the line, it's in.

01:49:31.250 --> 01:49:34.850
But the human eye can resolve
about five milliseconds,

01:49:34.850 --> 01:49:37.950
and this dynamic event
is significantly short--

01:49:37.950 --> 01:49:39.790
I'm sorry, 20 milliseconds.

01:49:39.790 --> 01:49:43.340
The dynamic event of this
is about five seconds.

01:49:43.340 --> 01:49:45.650
There's the footprint.

01:49:45.650 --> 01:49:48.080
Was that ball in or out?

01:49:48.080 --> 01:49:51.200
That ball was in, but if you
just saw it at that glimpse,

01:49:51.200 --> 01:49:51.830
it was out.

01:49:51.830 --> 01:49:55.840
Here's a bow, one of the
oldest tools of mankind.

01:49:55.840 --> 01:49:57.320
There's the arrow going off.

01:49:57.320 --> 01:50:01.100
Watch the dynamics of the bow,
and particularly this piece

01:50:01.100 --> 01:50:04.140
right in here.

01:50:04.140 --> 01:50:06.960
Never would have thought that
a bow shook around that much.

01:50:06.960 --> 01:50:07.650
Now watch this.

01:50:07.650 --> 01:50:09.870
This is an absolutely
amazing shot

01:50:09.870 --> 01:50:14.020
by this archer, who
is going to shoot

01:50:14.020 --> 01:50:16.330
an arrow, which is right
about in there, towards us.

01:50:16.330 --> 01:50:17.577
Watch out, Doc.

01:50:17.577 --> 01:50:18.160
Here it comes.

01:50:18.160 --> 01:50:21.900
He's about to release
it right there.

01:50:21.900 --> 01:50:22.860
There's the release.

01:50:22.860 --> 01:50:23.820
Watch the arrow.

01:50:26.700 --> 01:50:31.030
[LAUGHTER]

01:50:31.030 --> 01:50:34.480
It's the highest-stiffness arrow
that's made, a graphite fiber

01:50:34.480 --> 01:50:34.981
shaft arrow.

01:50:34.981 --> 01:50:35.646
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

01:50:35.646 --> 01:50:37.450
- --away from the club's
center of gravity,

01:50:37.450 --> 01:50:40.200
giving the club a very high
resistance to twisting.

01:50:40.200 --> 01:50:41.530
CRAWLEY: Listen to the claims.

01:50:41.530 --> 01:50:45.700
- Thus, off-centered hits do not
twist a metal-wood club as much

01:50:45.700 --> 01:50:47.680
as conventional wood.

01:50:47.680 --> 01:50:50.380
Less twisting,
reduced torque results

01:50:50.380 --> 01:50:53.030
in straighter shots
and increased distance.

01:50:53.030 --> 01:50:54.730
CRAWLEY: May be true or may not.

01:50:54.730 --> 01:50:57.850
- The external weight placement
in metal-wood also allows

01:50:57.850 --> 01:51:00.790
the weight to be placed lower
and farther behind the ball

01:51:00.790 --> 01:51:02.280
than a conventional wood.

01:51:02.280 --> 01:51:04.290
The resulting lower
center of gravity

01:51:04.290 --> 01:51:07.570
creates a more efficient energy
transfer of the club head's

01:51:07.570 --> 01:51:11.330
mass through the ball for
more consistent, longer hits.

01:51:11.330 --> 01:51:12.580
CRAWLEY: May be true, may not.

01:51:12.580 --> 01:51:15.610
- The metal-wood design also
offers advantages and increased

01:51:15.610 --> 01:51:18.320
club head speed
through the ball.

01:51:18.320 --> 01:51:22.570
The narrow hosel and aerodynamic
design minimize air resistance,

01:51:22.570 --> 01:51:25.300
generating greater
club head speed.

01:51:25.300 --> 01:51:26.710
The consistent quality of--

01:51:26.710 --> 01:51:28.236
CRAWLEY: May be true, may not.

01:51:28.236 --> 01:51:30.620
[END PLAYBACK]

01:51:30.620 --> 01:51:34.730
Now, why is it interesting
to talk with students

01:51:34.730 --> 01:51:37.740
about such things?

01:51:37.740 --> 01:51:42.610
Well, we actually use,
through design or accident,

01:51:42.610 --> 01:51:46.690
the discussion of sporting
goods throughout almost

01:51:46.690 --> 01:51:49.390
the entire curriculum.

01:51:49.390 --> 01:51:53.980
There are several of us who
teach freshmen seminars.

01:51:53.980 --> 01:51:56.440
Freshmen seminars are
a process now at MIT,

01:51:56.440 --> 01:51:59.320
if you don't quite remember
them, where every freshman is

01:51:59.320 --> 01:52:03.280
assigned to a faculty advisor
mentor who advises them

01:52:03.280 --> 01:52:06.070
through the freshmen year
before they designate a course

01:52:06.070 --> 01:52:08.350
but also teaches them a seminar.

01:52:08.350 --> 01:52:09.970
I teach one on
sporting good design,

01:52:09.970 --> 01:52:14.440
and I use it to teach students
the business of engineering,

01:52:14.440 --> 01:52:16.930
which is making products that
make people's lives better--

01:52:16.930 --> 01:52:18.430
in this case, sporting goods--

01:52:18.430 --> 01:52:20.930
because students can get
their hands around a sporting

01:52:20.930 --> 01:52:22.540
good, a piece of
sporting equipment.

01:52:22.540 --> 01:52:23.860
They can understand it.

01:52:23.860 --> 01:52:29.471
I also subtlely use it to show
the value of Freshman Physics.

01:52:29.471 --> 01:52:31.720
Freshman Physics is in one
of those big lecture halls,

01:52:31.720 --> 01:52:33.770
and you're not quite
sure what's going on.

01:52:33.770 --> 01:52:39.510
So, for example, I show them the
video that you just saw there,

01:52:39.510 --> 01:52:42.270
and I let them listen
to the claims that

01:52:42.270 --> 01:52:44.860
are made about that golf club.

01:52:44.860 --> 01:52:46.010
There were three claims.

01:52:46.010 --> 01:52:48.130
Let's see if we can
remember what they are.

01:52:48.130 --> 01:52:51.950
They were that perimeter
weighting the head of the club

01:52:51.950 --> 01:52:55.730
increases its moment
of inertia, and it

01:52:55.730 --> 01:52:58.668
causes the ball to go straighter
with an off-center hit.

01:53:01.870 --> 01:53:04.510
True or false?

01:53:04.510 --> 01:53:05.660
False.

01:53:05.660 --> 01:53:09.760
I'd like to see you and revoke
your 801 grade after the--

01:53:09.760 --> 01:53:11.550
more or less true.

01:53:11.550 --> 01:53:12.450
More or less true.

01:53:12.450 --> 01:53:14.670
If you work out the
dynamics, if you

01:53:14.670 --> 01:53:16.530
have a high moment
of inertia club head,

01:53:16.530 --> 01:53:19.440
and you hit off-center,
it's less likely to twist

01:53:19.440 --> 01:53:22.300
during the time of impact.

01:53:22.300 --> 01:53:26.230
A low CG, low center
of gravity, causes

01:53:26.230 --> 01:53:30.700
the club to strike the
ball more consistently

01:53:30.700 --> 01:53:33.980
and drive it farther,
was the claim.

01:53:33.980 --> 01:53:36.530
True or false?

01:53:36.530 --> 01:53:38.610
There is no model of
the dynamics of a club

01:53:38.610 --> 01:53:42.280
that I can construct that
would cause that to be true.

01:53:42.280 --> 01:53:47.590
And thirdly, making
the head aerodynamic

01:53:47.590 --> 01:53:52.760
increases the head velocity
at the time of ball impact.

01:53:52.760 --> 01:53:55.758
True or false?

01:53:55.758 --> 01:53:58.500
Hmm.

01:53:58.500 --> 01:54:00.880
How many people think false?

01:54:00.880 --> 01:54:03.540
How many people think true?

01:54:03.540 --> 01:54:06.670
Well, it sort of
depends, is the answer.

01:54:06.670 --> 01:54:10.030
[LAUGHTER]

01:54:10.030 --> 01:54:11.950
Let me ask you a
simpler question, which

01:54:11.950 --> 01:54:13.600
I ask my freshman.

01:54:13.600 --> 01:54:16.610
What's the optimum head
mass for a golf club?

01:54:19.140 --> 01:54:21.000
Fix the mass of the ball.

01:54:21.000 --> 01:54:24.842
What's the optimum head mass
of the head of the club?

01:54:24.842 --> 01:54:27.300
All the mass is in the head,
by the way, for all practical.

01:54:27.300 --> 01:54:29.197
Purposes.

01:54:29.197 --> 01:54:31.030
You can't exceed a
certain amount, according

01:54:31.030 --> 01:54:32.320
to the rules of golf, I think.

01:54:32.320 --> 01:54:32.861
That's right.

01:54:32.861 --> 01:54:33.400
Thank you.

01:54:33.400 --> 01:54:34.191
There's the lawyer.

01:54:34.191 --> 01:54:37.460
[LAUGHTER]

01:54:37.460 --> 01:54:39.780
And this stresses
an important point,

01:54:39.780 --> 01:54:42.860
which is that regulation comes
into sports, just as it does

01:54:42.860 --> 01:54:44.570
into aircraft and spacecraft.

01:54:44.570 --> 01:54:46.584
So in fact, you can't
exceed the head that's

01:54:46.584 --> 01:54:47.750
listed in the rules of golf.

01:54:47.750 --> 01:54:52.970
But aside from that, would you
rather make it lower or higher?

01:54:52.970 --> 01:54:56.300
Well, it depends on your
model of the human kinematics,

01:54:56.300 --> 01:54:57.160
really.

01:54:57.160 --> 01:54:59.470
If you model the
body as something

01:54:59.470 --> 01:55:04.290
that can produce a
constant velocity,

01:55:04.290 --> 01:55:05.990
you'll want to make it higher.

01:55:05.990 --> 01:55:07.640
If you model the
body as something

01:55:07.640 --> 01:55:10.190
that produces a
torque, which has

01:55:10.190 --> 01:55:12.380
to accelerate the
head of the club,

01:55:12.380 --> 01:55:15.750
you'll want to make it lower.

01:55:15.750 --> 01:55:20.710
So are you a torque source
or a velocity source

01:55:20.710 --> 01:55:22.120
when you swing your club?

01:55:22.120 --> 01:55:25.030
Well, I'll let you
think about that.

01:55:25.030 --> 01:55:27.755
But lastly, and certainly,
letting students

01:55:27.755 --> 01:55:29.380
think about sporting
goods like letting

01:55:29.380 --> 01:55:31.840
them think about
anything, In fact, is

01:55:31.840 --> 01:55:34.060
an outlet for their
creative instincts.

01:55:34.060 --> 01:55:36.880
Let me show you the final
assignment in my freshman

01:55:36.880 --> 01:55:41.650
seminar last fall, which
was to design a new sport.

01:55:41.650 --> 01:55:43.450
Let's forget about
a new tennis racket.

01:55:43.450 --> 01:55:45.990
Let's design a whole
new sport, right?

01:55:45.990 --> 01:55:48.500
This was the idea
that came up with.

01:55:48.500 --> 01:55:50.690
First, they said the
fastest growing sport

01:55:50.690 --> 01:55:53.620
amongst our generation
is lacrosse.

01:55:53.620 --> 01:55:54.820
I didn't know that.

01:55:54.820 --> 01:55:57.250
But it indicated
to me that I had

01:55:57.250 --> 01:55:59.830
taught them to think about
market factors, which

01:55:59.830 --> 01:56:02.584
was a valuable thing to learn.

01:56:02.584 --> 01:56:04.250
Seems like an obvious
lesson if you ever

01:56:04.250 --> 01:56:07.190
tried to sell anything, but not
if you're a freshman, perhaps.

01:56:07.190 --> 01:56:10.520
Then they observed that lacrosse
sticks are hard to use and hard

01:56:10.520 --> 01:56:13.310
to learn to use, so they
redesigned the lacrosse stick

01:56:13.310 --> 01:56:15.840
using 801 Freshman Physics.

01:56:15.840 --> 01:56:17.190
That made me happy.

01:56:17.190 --> 01:56:20.692
But then they observed
that essentially, sports

01:56:20.692 --> 01:56:28.890
are a way to represent
on a small field warfare,

01:56:28.890 --> 01:56:33.180
and warfare is tactical, and you
push people forward and back.

01:56:33.180 --> 01:56:38.750
And essentially, all sports,
except for baseball and cricket

01:56:38.750 --> 01:56:40.860
and a few others--

01:56:40.860 --> 01:56:42.390
certainly all field sports--

01:56:42.390 --> 01:56:45.120
involve the dynamics of
pushing forward on the offense

01:56:45.120 --> 01:56:46.590
and retreating on the defense.

01:56:46.590 --> 01:56:50.630
And they said, what would
happen if we changed that?

01:56:50.630 --> 01:56:53.570
What would happen if we
said that Team A defends

01:56:53.570 --> 01:56:56.000
this goal and this
goal, and Team B defends

01:56:56.000 --> 01:56:57.334
this goal and this goal?

01:56:57.334 --> 01:57:01.050
[LAUGHTER]

01:57:01.050 --> 01:57:04.040
So now, a ball in
the middle here--

01:57:04.040 --> 01:57:06.380
if it pops out
that way, you have

01:57:06.380 --> 01:57:08.090
to run back and be on defense.

01:57:08.090 --> 01:57:09.920
But if it pops out
that way, you also

01:57:09.920 --> 01:57:13.070
have to run back
and be on defense.

01:57:13.070 --> 01:57:15.080
Sort of an interesting idea.

01:57:15.080 --> 01:57:17.030
The sort of things
that MIT students are

01:57:17.030 --> 01:57:23.310
likely to come up with if you
just say, invent something.

01:57:23.310 --> 01:57:26.190
So there's a pedagogic
value in our education

01:57:26.190 --> 01:57:28.590
to talking about the
design of things,

01:57:28.590 --> 01:57:31.400
letting students build
things, whether they're

01:57:31.400 --> 01:57:34.140
sporting goods or spacecraft.

01:57:34.140 --> 01:57:36.330
The first is, and
this may seem a little

01:57:36.330 --> 01:57:39.728
obvious to all of the people in
the room except the faculty--

01:57:39.728 --> 01:57:42.470
[LAUGHTER]

01:57:42.470 --> 01:57:46.100
--that the conception, design,
implementation, and operation

01:57:46.100 --> 01:57:52.100
of things which serve humanity
is the business of engineering.

01:57:52.100 --> 01:57:55.640
And once in a while, we
should say that to students.

01:57:55.640 --> 01:57:58.550
Mixed in amongst the
discussion of theory

01:57:58.550 --> 01:58:01.190
and the quest for
cutting-edge research,

01:58:01.190 --> 01:58:03.140
we should signal
them to the fact

01:58:03.140 --> 01:58:07.270
that it's OK to really build
things and help people.

01:58:07.270 --> 01:58:10.930
If you are trying to construct
a pedagogic structure

01:58:10.930 --> 01:58:13.300
for an Aero-Astro
curriculum, you

01:58:13.300 --> 01:58:16.800
would then say, OK, let's have
students build some spacecraft.

01:58:19.320 --> 01:58:23.550
Possible, but not
really accessible

01:58:23.550 --> 01:58:26.480
at the undergraduate level.

01:58:26.480 --> 01:58:28.670
Let's have them
build some aircraft.

01:58:28.670 --> 01:58:31.850
Possible, but not really
expectable in the scope

01:58:31.850 --> 01:58:35.040
of a semester subject.

01:58:35.040 --> 01:58:36.750
So what could you
have them build

01:58:36.750 --> 01:58:39.750
in the course of a semester
subject, which would conceive,

01:58:39.750 --> 01:58:42.030
design, implement, and
operate, incorporate

01:58:42.030 --> 01:58:44.310
the technologies of aerospace?

01:58:44.310 --> 01:58:46.840
A piece of sporting good
equipment is not a bad idea.

01:58:46.840 --> 01:58:49.440
So sports give us
a ready application

01:58:49.440 --> 01:58:53.760
centered on the same disciplines
that we use in aerospace.

01:58:53.760 --> 01:58:56.010
The next piece of video
is going to show you

01:58:56.010 --> 01:58:58.980
another thing that we do
in the sophomore year--

01:58:58.980 --> 01:59:01.510
freshman and sophomore year.

01:59:01.510 --> 01:59:03.070
It isn't possible
to have students--

01:59:03.070 --> 01:59:04.090
I'm sorry freshman year.

01:59:04.090 --> 01:59:07.120
It isn't possible to have
students build an airplane,

01:59:07.120 --> 01:59:11.930
but you can have them build
a blimp in a semester.

01:59:11.930 --> 01:59:13.580
And this is a--

01:59:13.580 --> 01:59:16.310
coming right into focus now--

01:59:16.310 --> 01:59:19.610
subject taught by one of my
colleagues, David Newman.

01:59:19.610 --> 01:59:22.730
And it's the second-term
freshman subject,

01:59:22.730 --> 01:59:27.200
and at the end of the semester,
they design and build a blimp.

01:59:27.200 --> 01:59:28.910
This is a
radio-controlled blimp.

01:59:28.910 --> 01:59:31.670
It looks like someone's holding
it, but in fact, they aren't.

01:59:31.670 --> 01:59:33.350
They're just sort
of standing by it.

01:59:33.350 --> 01:59:34.880
Blimps move slow.

01:59:34.880 --> 01:59:37.220
They're relatively simple.

01:59:37.220 --> 01:59:39.320
They produce lift
with buoyancy, so you

01:59:39.320 --> 01:59:41.660
don't have to know much
about aerodynamics.

01:59:41.660 --> 01:59:44.360
And this may in fact be the
next NCAA sport, you see.

01:59:44.360 --> 01:59:48.100
[LAUGHTER]

01:59:48.100 --> 01:59:52.160
The students really enjoy this,
and we enjoy watching them.

01:59:52.160 --> 01:59:52.660
Too.

01:59:52.660 --> 01:59:55.330
And taking after our
colleague Woodie Flowers,

01:59:55.330 --> 01:59:57.550
we actually let them compete.

01:59:57.550 --> 02:00:00.760
That's it for that video.

02:00:00.760 --> 02:00:03.940
When they're sophomores,
we have an approach

02:00:03.940 --> 02:00:06.400
called Unified
Engineering, which

02:00:06.400 --> 02:00:11.020
takes four subjects
in the sophomore year

02:00:11.020 --> 02:00:13.750
and mashes them all
together, so they

02:00:13.750 --> 02:00:16.151
are educated in an
integrative environment where

02:00:16.151 --> 02:00:18.400
they learn that the structures
person has to know what

02:00:18.400 --> 02:00:20.380
the fluids person is doing.

02:00:20.380 --> 02:00:23.290
They all have to be organized
by the control person.

02:00:23.290 --> 02:00:25.570
There's some
thermodynamics involved,

02:00:25.570 --> 02:00:29.320
and they do problems that are
called systems problems, which

02:00:29.320 --> 02:00:32.020
cause them to bring together
all of these aspects

02:00:32.020 --> 02:00:34.390
and either do an
integrative analysis

02:00:34.390 --> 02:00:36.190
or an integrative design.

02:00:36.190 --> 02:00:38.410
Now, one of my
colleagues, Steve Hall,

02:00:38.410 --> 02:00:40.780
decided that an interesting
integrative design

02:00:40.780 --> 02:00:47.100
project this past spring would
be to have them build a glider.

02:00:47.100 --> 02:00:51.060
A glider is actually not a bad
thing for sophomores to build.

02:00:51.060 --> 02:00:54.150
It has aerodynamics, but
it doesn't have propulsion.

02:00:54.150 --> 02:00:57.150
It's relatively easy to
test, and he got the idea

02:00:57.150 --> 02:01:02.030
by some collaborative
interaction of actually making

02:01:02.030 --> 02:01:05.240
contact with a toy
company, Hasbro,

02:01:05.240 --> 02:01:08.690
the manufacturer of Nerf,
and using the sophomore class

02:01:08.690 --> 02:01:14.620
to design the replacement
for this, the Nerf airplane.

02:01:14.620 --> 02:01:19.330
So the Hasbro guys came and
talked about the market.

02:01:19.330 --> 02:01:22.520
The toy business is
segmented in a certain way,

02:01:22.520 --> 02:01:26.190
and the market size is
so much, and so forth.

02:01:26.190 --> 02:01:27.940
They talked about the
design requirements,

02:01:27.940 --> 02:01:31.270
and the subtle one
for this toy is

02:01:31.270 --> 02:01:33.953
it has to be enjoyable by
four or five-year-olds,

02:01:33.953 --> 02:01:35.925
who will throw it like this.

02:01:42.960 --> 02:01:44.410
I am a rated pilot, so it's OK.

02:01:44.410 --> 02:01:46.050
[LAUGHTER]

02:01:46.050 --> 02:01:48.604
But it has to also be
enjoyable by 10-year-olds,

02:01:48.604 --> 02:01:49.770
who will throw it like this.

02:01:53.640 --> 02:01:57.300
And this toy actually fails
because it trims nose up

02:01:57.300 --> 02:01:59.730
in steady-state flight, so
that when you throw it hard,

02:01:59.730 --> 02:02:01.770
it pitches up and stalls.

02:02:01.770 --> 02:02:02.950
There are regulations.

02:02:02.950 --> 02:02:05.520
Sure, there's no regulations
on toys, right, in America.

02:02:05.520 --> 02:02:08.310
[LAUGHTER]

02:02:08.310 --> 02:02:10.710
It's probably easier to satisfy
the federal airworthiness

02:02:10.710 --> 02:02:13.500
regulations than the
toy safety regulations.

02:02:13.500 --> 02:02:16.080
So they were actually
presented with a book

02:02:16.080 --> 02:02:20.440
of essentially the
regulations of building toys.

02:02:20.440 --> 02:02:22.600
Then they had to come
up with a concept.

02:02:22.600 --> 02:02:24.850
They had to do the aerodynamic
analysis and the stress

02:02:24.850 --> 02:02:29.320
analysis, and they had to make
a design for manufacturability,

02:02:29.320 --> 02:02:32.300
another innovative concept
to get across to students.

02:02:32.300 --> 02:02:34.090
And in fact, this is
a very low-cost toy.

02:02:34.090 --> 02:02:36.020
It's stamped out
of pieces of foam.

02:02:36.020 --> 02:02:38.020
So if you could get it
out of one piece of foam,

02:02:38.020 --> 02:02:38.680
that was the best.

02:02:38.680 --> 02:02:40.596
If you could get it out
of two pieces of foam,

02:02:40.596 --> 02:02:42.230
that was better, and so forth.

02:02:42.230 --> 02:02:44.680
And then finally, we had
a set of flight tests.

02:02:44.680 --> 02:02:47.620
The students were
captivated by this project.

02:02:47.620 --> 02:02:52.480
The instructors said that they
absorb this stuff that we teach

02:02:52.480 --> 02:02:55.450
them in the second
semester like sponges,

02:02:55.450 --> 02:02:58.252
compared to semesters when we
didn't do a project like this

02:02:58.252 --> 02:02:59.710
because, of course,
while they were

02:02:59.710 --> 02:03:03.380
learning about flight dynamics,
they were designing a glider.

02:03:03.380 --> 02:03:05.190
While they were
learning about beams,

02:03:05.190 --> 02:03:07.580
they were doing the
analysis of a wing.

02:03:07.580 --> 02:03:10.100
The more of that
we can do at MIT,

02:03:10.100 --> 02:03:12.110
the happier our
students will be.

02:03:12.110 --> 02:03:15.740
We have an exercise in the
junior and senior years

02:03:15.740 --> 02:03:17.810
where teams of
students work together

02:03:17.810 --> 02:03:20.300
to identify a research
project and then

02:03:20.300 --> 02:03:24.620
execute it over the
course of two semesters.

02:03:24.620 --> 02:03:26.810
They have to design
the experiment,

02:03:26.810 --> 02:03:28.970
and they have to take data.

02:03:28.970 --> 02:03:30.480
It's not sufficient
to, for example,

02:03:30.480 --> 02:03:32.030
write a computer program.

02:03:32.030 --> 02:03:34.640
There has to be an experiment.

02:03:34.640 --> 02:03:36.470
There has to be
data produced which

02:03:36.470 --> 02:03:39.290
they have to be able to analyze
and compare with a model,

02:03:39.290 --> 02:03:40.880
and then, of
course, they report.

02:03:40.880 --> 02:03:43.820
We use this as a vehicle for
a lot of communication skills,

02:03:43.820 --> 02:03:45.290
in particular.

02:03:45.290 --> 02:03:47.270
We let the students,
by and large,

02:03:47.270 --> 02:03:49.460
choose their own projects.

02:03:49.460 --> 02:03:51.080
And not surprisingly,
a lot of them

02:03:51.080 --> 02:03:53.910
come up with sporting goods.

02:03:53.910 --> 02:03:58.390
This is one project which
is, of course, a plaster

02:03:58.390 --> 02:04:01.660
cast of a hand, which was
used by a team of students

02:04:01.660 --> 02:04:05.510
to determine the
aerodynamic drag on a hand

02:04:05.510 --> 02:04:08.870
because, of course, they
were competitive swimmers.

02:04:08.870 --> 02:04:11.517
And the question that rages
in the swimming community

02:04:11.517 --> 02:04:13.850
is do you get the maximum
efficiency by having your hand

02:04:13.850 --> 02:04:16.690
like this or like this?

02:04:16.690 --> 02:04:19.930
Is it better to form a little
more projected plan form

02:04:19.930 --> 02:04:21.750
or to cup it?

02:04:21.750 --> 02:04:23.810
So they actually
did the aerodynamic

02:04:23.810 --> 02:04:27.080
at the right Reynolds number
of two plaster casts of hands

02:04:27.080 --> 02:04:29.660
to see which had the
higher drag coefficient.

02:04:29.660 --> 02:04:32.180
And the answer is--

02:04:32.180 --> 02:04:34.075
I don't quite remember,
unfortunately.

02:04:34.075 --> 02:04:38.530
[LAUGHTER]

02:04:38.530 --> 02:04:41.140
But the MIT swim team
has been winning.

02:04:43.750 --> 02:04:46.960
So just in case there are any
alumna of other universities

02:04:46.960 --> 02:04:53.824
here, we don't want
to give up any--

02:04:53.824 --> 02:04:55.240
back to golf clubs,
you of course,

02:04:55.240 --> 02:04:57.270
recognize this as your
Saturday afternoon hobby.

02:04:57.270 --> 02:05:01.200
Usually, it's instrumented
about like this, right?

02:05:01.200 --> 02:05:05.070
This is given to us
kindly by Calloway.

02:05:05.070 --> 02:05:10.170
We've done quite a few
tests on golf club dynamics.

02:05:10.170 --> 02:05:12.930
I actually use this in
my freshman seminar,

02:05:12.930 --> 02:05:15.960
and I've had a few
undergraduate projects.

02:05:15.960 --> 02:05:20.080
The real questions are things
like does anything matter?

02:05:20.080 --> 02:05:23.080
[LAUGHTER]

02:05:26.008 --> 02:05:29.340
[APPLAUSE]

02:05:31.720 --> 02:05:33.940
If you went out to the
average Saturday golfer

02:05:33.940 --> 02:05:36.580
and gave him a driver and told
him that Tiger Woods had just

02:05:36.580 --> 02:05:42.390
won with this one, he or she
would probably drive it farther

02:05:42.390 --> 02:05:45.210
because there's some interesting
psychophysiological feedback

02:05:45.210 --> 02:05:46.350
that occurs in golf.

02:05:46.350 --> 02:05:50.460
If you think you're going to hit
the ball well, you usually do.

02:05:50.460 --> 02:05:52.740
The average drive
distance on the Pro Tour

02:05:52.740 --> 02:05:56.610
over the last decade
has gone up five yards.

02:05:56.610 --> 02:05:59.310
So every time you see
a Saturday afternoon ad

02:05:59.310 --> 02:06:03.770
that says our driver
drives 20 yards farther--

02:06:03.770 --> 02:06:05.160
good marketing.

02:06:05.160 --> 02:06:07.370
But the fact of the
matter is that the ball

02:06:07.370 --> 02:06:11.880
is in contact with the head of
a club for a half a millisecond,

02:06:11.880 --> 02:06:13.470
and there's not an
awful lot you can

02:06:13.470 --> 02:06:19.080
do to anything to change the
events in a half a millisecond.

02:06:19.080 --> 02:06:22.140
There are some quasistatic
effects that you actually

02:06:22.140 --> 02:06:24.720
get to teach them about
centrifugal destiffening,

02:06:24.720 --> 02:06:27.170
as you bring the club down
at 100 feet per second,

02:06:27.170 --> 02:06:28.290
and the club bends.

02:06:28.290 --> 02:06:30.150
That's a real effect.

02:06:30.150 --> 02:06:31.710
So we play around
with golf clubs.

02:06:34.270 --> 02:06:36.710
Tennis is a big one.

02:06:36.710 --> 02:06:39.750
These are two experimental
tennis rackets.

02:06:39.750 --> 02:06:42.180
This is my colleague, Ramnath.

02:06:42.180 --> 02:06:46.300
This one-- I don't know
if you can hear it,

02:06:46.300 --> 02:06:48.590
hear that-- has powder in it.

02:06:48.590 --> 02:06:50.690
So this is to
provide extra damping

02:06:50.690 --> 02:06:55.070
when you impact the ball, which
is thought to reduce the tennis

02:06:55.070 --> 02:06:56.390
elbow problem.

02:06:56.390 --> 02:07:02.330
And this one has fluid, which
zings around in the outside.

02:07:02.330 --> 02:07:04.532
There's actually a
fluid tube in here,

02:07:04.532 --> 02:07:05.990
which is thought,
according to Ram,

02:07:05.990 --> 02:07:09.680
somehow to increase the
actual delivered power because

02:07:09.680 --> 02:07:11.060
of variable geometry.

02:07:11.060 --> 02:07:12.680
Students do experiments in this.

02:07:12.680 --> 02:07:14.690
We actually have
a test facility,

02:07:14.690 --> 02:07:19.940
if you want to call it that,
where we do measurements on--

02:07:26.540 --> 02:07:28.040
if I pull this, I'm
almost certainly

02:07:28.040 --> 02:07:32.214
going to take the skin off
of this part of my arm.

02:07:32.214 --> 02:07:34.130
I used to do that in
summer camp all the time,

02:07:34.130 --> 02:07:35.900
but they weren't this strong.

02:07:35.900 --> 02:07:40.600
The compound bow-- perhaps
one of the oldest pieces

02:07:40.600 --> 02:07:42.790
of technology on the planet.

02:07:42.790 --> 02:07:46.480
Found in the tomb of King
Tut was something called

02:07:46.480 --> 02:07:52.410
a compound bow, where there
were three types of materials

02:07:52.410 --> 02:07:55.080
used to build up
the bow, starting

02:07:55.080 --> 02:07:57.780
from the hardest on the
center line, which I think

02:07:57.780 --> 02:08:01.410
was ivory, to wood, to leather.

02:08:01.410 --> 02:08:04.650
The ancient Egyptians
understood that by varying

02:08:04.650 --> 02:08:07.290
the modulus of elasticity
of the material

02:08:07.290 --> 02:08:09.510
away from the neutral
axis, an archer

02:08:09.510 --> 02:08:12.360
could store more strain
energy in the bow

02:08:12.360 --> 02:08:14.990
and shoot the arrow farther.

02:08:14.990 --> 02:08:17.290
Well, we're still doing
that, except now, they're

02:08:17.290 --> 02:08:20.500
advanced composite graphite
epoxy and all sorts of bells

02:08:20.500 --> 02:08:21.350
and whistles.

02:08:21.350 --> 02:08:23.320
This one, again, is
heavily strain-guaged

02:08:23.320 --> 02:08:27.950
to understand the strain energy
distribution during launch.

02:08:27.950 --> 02:08:29.700
The students love
to do these things.

02:08:29.700 --> 02:08:34.860
We find consistently that the
projects the students initiate

02:08:34.860 --> 02:08:37.920
are significantly more
successful than the ones

02:08:37.920 --> 02:08:41.100
that the professors say, well,
let's test this new widget.

02:08:43.620 --> 02:08:46.090
At MIT, we have a
tradition of actually doing

02:08:46.090 --> 02:08:49.869
things in addition to
educating students.

02:08:49.869 --> 02:08:52.410
And this is just a list of some
of the things in the sporting

02:08:52.410 --> 02:08:55.230
good business that my
colleagues and I have done

02:08:55.230 --> 02:08:58.680
over the last decade or so.

02:08:58.680 --> 02:09:04.020
If you go down a ski slope
now, you're about three times

02:09:04.020 --> 02:09:08.250
less likely to injure yourself
through an ankle or leg break

02:09:08.250 --> 02:09:10.050
than you would
have been a decade

02:09:10.050 --> 02:09:16.350
or so ago, thanks to the ASTM
ski binding regulations set

02:09:16.350 --> 02:09:19.920
by the ASTM Snow
Skiing Committee

02:09:19.920 --> 02:09:22.200
formerly chaired by Larry
Young, one of my colleagues.

02:09:22.200 --> 02:09:26.700
It's reduced the ski
binding-related injuries

02:09:26.700 --> 02:09:31.570
from six per 1,000 skier days
to two per 1,000 skier days.

02:09:31.570 --> 02:09:34.710
Pretty significant benefit.

02:09:34.710 --> 02:09:37.880
JAR22 is Joint
Airworthiness Requirements,

02:09:37.880 --> 02:09:40.710
a European standard
for powerless aircraft,

02:09:40.710 --> 02:09:42.140
including gliders.

02:09:42.140 --> 02:09:44.390
It used to be thought
that you could not

02:09:44.390 --> 02:09:48.050
protect a pilot in a
high-performance glider

02:09:48.050 --> 02:09:51.950
from a crash impact, a
nose-down crash impact.

02:09:51.950 --> 02:09:53.510
And actually, a
student of mine was

02:09:53.510 --> 02:09:56.660
killed in one of such
impacts, which caused me

02:09:56.660 --> 02:10:00.050
and one of my colleagues
Hansman to consider whether this

02:10:00.050 --> 02:10:01.430
was really the case.

02:10:01.430 --> 02:10:04.550
We had a set of students over
several years build 1/4-scale

02:10:04.550 --> 02:10:07.850
scale models and crash them
in our laboratory and prove

02:10:07.850 --> 02:10:10.550
to the world that you can,
in fact, protect a glider,

02:10:10.550 --> 02:10:14.510
a pilot, as well as any
pilot in any aircraft.

02:10:14.510 --> 02:10:16.490
And as a result, the
airworthiness requirements

02:10:16.490 --> 02:10:20.810
were changed to require
such protection.

02:10:20.810 --> 02:10:23.160
On a little lighter note,
one of my other colleagues,

02:10:23.160 --> 02:10:26.990
Ramnath, has established the
standards for the Pro Tennis

02:10:26.990 --> 02:10:35.210
Tour line-calling machines.

02:10:35.210 --> 02:10:37.760
As you're maybe aware,
increasingly in pro tennis,

02:10:37.760 --> 02:10:42.740
the lines are called by
machine, not by human eye.

02:10:42.740 --> 02:10:45.750
And we haven't actually
invented one of the machines,

02:10:45.750 --> 02:10:47.900
but we've been involved
in the regulation of what

02:10:47.900 --> 02:10:51.950
the specification of the
line-calling machines are.

02:10:51.950 --> 02:10:54.860
Now these three form
a pattern, which

02:10:54.860 --> 02:10:56.720
is that they put us
in a research role

02:10:56.720 --> 02:11:00.060
for the regulatory agency.

02:11:00.060 --> 02:11:02.675
Skis, airplanes, pro tennis--

02:11:02.675 --> 02:11:04.050
and that's something
that I think

02:11:04.050 --> 02:11:07.950
is appropriate for MIT,
as an organization, to do.

02:11:07.950 --> 02:11:11.670
We have also been involved
in for-profit ventures--

02:11:11.670 --> 02:11:13.920
that is to say,
individuals amongst us.

02:11:13.920 --> 02:11:16.720
I don't know if you
can see this well.

02:11:16.720 --> 02:11:18.180
But this is a softball bat.

02:11:18.180 --> 02:11:21.330
By the way, softball
is the big market.

02:11:21.330 --> 02:11:23.310
Nobody plays pro baseball
with a wooden bat.

02:11:23.310 --> 02:11:26.550
Everyone plays Saturday
afternoon softball and Little

02:11:26.550 --> 02:11:28.020
League with metal bats.

02:11:28.020 --> 02:11:32.500
And this is a dimpled bat,
dimpled just like a golf ball,

02:11:32.500 --> 02:11:38.620
so that when you
swing, it'll go faster,

02:11:38.620 --> 02:11:41.260
provided of course that you
model the human as a velocity

02:11:41.260 --> 02:11:44.470
source and not as
a torque source.

02:11:44.470 --> 02:11:46.630
Now, what I'm very
proud of in this ball,

02:11:46.630 --> 02:11:48.670
and you can't see this,
so I'll read it to you--

02:11:48.670 --> 02:11:51.010
is that it says
Power Stick, which

02:11:51.010 --> 02:11:52.500
is sort of good marketing.

02:11:55.020 --> 02:11:58.590
And it actually says on
here delayed boundary layer

02:11:58.590 --> 02:11:59.786
separation.

02:11:59.786 --> 02:12:03.188
[LAUGHTER]

02:12:06.110 --> 02:12:08.614
Now, I'm not sure who they
think out there in America

02:12:08.614 --> 02:12:09.780
is going to understand that.

02:12:09.780 --> 02:12:11.987
[LAUGHTER]

02:12:11.987 --> 02:12:14.070
But I'm particularly proud
that something came out

02:12:14.070 --> 02:12:16.260
of the Aero department
that has on the side of it,

02:12:16.260 --> 02:12:19.650
delayed boundary
layer separation.

02:12:19.650 --> 02:12:21.540
Because it might actually
cause some kid out

02:12:21.540 --> 02:12:23.940
there someplace to ask
his dad, as they're

02:12:23.940 --> 02:12:26.430
getting the ice cream
after the baseball game--

02:12:26.430 --> 02:12:28.390
Dad, what's a boundary layer?

02:12:28.390 --> 02:12:33.300
[LAUGHTER]

02:12:33.300 --> 02:12:36.510
The best-selling ski in
America, the K2 Four,

02:12:36.510 --> 02:12:40.450
has a brain inside the ski,
done by a colleague of mine,

02:12:40.450 --> 02:12:42.870
Haygood, who has
the original idea

02:12:42.870 --> 02:12:45.900
and manufactured by another
former student of mine,

02:12:45.900 --> 02:12:48.480
Lazarus, at a
company called ACX.

02:12:48.480 --> 02:12:50.400
The ski uses new technology.

02:12:50.400 --> 02:12:52.830
This a little point-of-purchase
sticker, which

02:12:52.830 --> 02:12:54.630
is on the ski when you buy it.

02:12:54.630 --> 02:12:56.790
This ski uses new
technology developed

02:12:56.790 --> 02:12:59.430
by MIT-trained engineers.

02:12:59.430 --> 02:13:02.699
[LAUGHTER AND APPLAUSE]

02:13:05.970 --> 02:13:10.640
Now we're really making an
impact in America, you see.

02:13:10.640 --> 02:13:12.820
Now, this ski really works.

02:13:12.820 --> 02:13:16.000
You see, what happens is you
saw the video of the vibration

02:13:16.000 --> 02:13:18.310
of the ski on the snow.

02:13:18.310 --> 02:13:22.920
And in fact, the average height
of the ski off the snow surface

02:13:22.920 --> 02:13:24.520
is almost a centimeter.

02:13:24.520 --> 02:13:27.280
If you reduce the
vibration amplitude

02:13:27.280 --> 02:13:30.190
by increasing the damping
or dissipation in the ski,

02:13:30.190 --> 02:13:32.020
it vibrates less,
and more of the ski

02:13:32.020 --> 02:13:35.110
is in contact with the surface,
so you can really plant an edge

02:13:35.110 --> 02:13:36.530
and turn on it.

02:13:36.530 --> 02:13:39.580
And that's done by a little
piezoelectric material patch

02:13:39.580 --> 02:13:42.550
here, which takes the energy
from mechanical energy

02:13:42.550 --> 02:13:44.560
into electrical energy,
and then dissipates it

02:13:44.560 --> 02:13:46.990
in a resistor, which is in here.

02:13:46.990 --> 02:13:48.730
But now, the really
cool thing is

02:13:48.730 --> 02:13:51.170
that when you're in the
showroom, and you whack it,

02:13:51.170 --> 02:13:54.110
there's a little LED
that lights up there.

02:13:54.110 --> 02:13:57.540
[LAUGHTER]

02:14:01.950 --> 02:14:04.890
Now we know that's going
to help your skiing.

02:14:04.890 --> 02:14:08.320
[LAUGHTER]

02:14:11.770 --> 02:14:15.290
There are three great
traditions at MIT--

02:14:15.290 --> 02:14:17.030
hacks, sports, and technology.

02:14:19.694 --> 02:14:21.860
So if you can find something
that combines all three

02:14:21.860 --> 02:14:25.580
of these, boy,
you've got a winner.

02:14:25.580 --> 02:14:29.450
And we have these things from
time to time in the department

02:14:29.450 --> 02:14:33.110
that we just call special
projects, which are almost

02:14:33.110 --> 02:14:35.450
always student-initiated.

02:14:35.450 --> 02:14:38.680
The successful ones are almost
always student-initiated

02:14:38.680 --> 02:14:42.400
that combine these aspects of
hacks, sports, and technology.

02:14:42.400 --> 02:14:45.250
They're an outlet for
creativity and competitiveness.

02:14:45.250 --> 02:14:47.050
They're usually long-duration.

02:14:47.050 --> 02:14:49.380
They're almost always voluntary.

02:14:49.380 --> 02:14:52.600
The students do it nights
and weekends and vacations

02:14:52.600 --> 02:14:55.660
and summers, and I
think that they really

02:14:55.660 --> 02:14:58.600
add a tremendous amount
to the educational value

02:14:58.600 --> 02:15:00.050
of the students.

02:15:00.050 --> 02:15:04.520
We have a couple of slides of
the history of some of these.

02:15:08.520 --> 02:15:13.356
I built one of these when I
was an undergraduate in MIT.

02:15:13.356 --> 02:15:15.284
And they're of the flavor of--

02:15:18.176 --> 02:15:22.040
oh, thank you.

02:15:22.040 --> 02:15:25.310
They are of the flavor of
human-powered airplanes.

02:15:25.310 --> 02:15:27.020
We've built other
things, but I'll

02:15:27.020 --> 02:15:28.940
show you the human-powered
airplane family.

02:15:28.940 --> 02:15:32.600
This was the first one, called
the biplane ultralight research

02:15:32.600 --> 02:15:40.890
device, BIRD, which was a
two-pedaler biplane intended

02:15:40.890 --> 02:15:42.570
to fly for the
first Kramer Prize,

02:15:42.570 --> 02:15:46.860
which was to fly a mile
around a figure-eight pylon.

02:15:46.860 --> 02:15:49.530
The pylons were
half a mile apart.

02:15:49.530 --> 02:15:51.180
And there's the propeller.

02:15:51.180 --> 02:15:52.680
And the pedalers
would sit there,

02:15:52.680 --> 02:15:55.660
and it flies that
way, by the way.

02:15:55.660 --> 02:15:58.080
And there's a young future
ahead of the Aero department

02:15:58.080 --> 02:16:01.810
right there, with more hair.

02:16:01.810 --> 02:16:03.360
This one crashed.

02:16:03.360 --> 02:16:03.900
Next slide.

02:16:03.900 --> 02:16:06.430
[LAUGHTER]

02:16:06.430 --> 02:16:08.660
Here IS the next
one in the family.

02:16:08.660 --> 02:16:11.360
This is called Crystallis,
also a biplane.

02:16:11.360 --> 02:16:14.340
Now it flies like a
conventional airplane.

02:16:14.340 --> 02:16:16.820
It was built by some
colleagues of mine,

02:16:16.820 --> 02:16:18.980
including Mark
Drela, John Langford,

02:16:18.980 --> 02:16:21.050
who now runs Aurora Flight.

02:16:21.050 --> 02:16:25.580
This was a very good trainer,
and it was also built by us

02:16:25.580 --> 02:16:28.670
to compete with Paul McCreadie
in the cross-English Channel

02:16:28.670 --> 02:16:30.980
competition, which Paul beat us.

02:16:30.980 --> 02:16:33.799
But this would have
flown Dover to Calais.

02:16:33.799 --> 02:16:34.299
Next slide.

02:16:37.070 --> 02:16:39.090
This is Monarch.

02:16:39.090 --> 02:16:43.290
There's the single pilot
flying in that direction.

02:16:43.290 --> 02:16:46.490
This was built by essentially
the same team of students

02:16:46.490 --> 02:16:50.160
from the last airplane.

02:16:50.160 --> 02:16:53.450
And this was designed to
win the third Kramer Prize

02:16:53.450 --> 02:16:55.070
competition of the
Royal Aeronautical

02:16:55.070 --> 02:17:02.299
Society, which was for speed,
for human-powered speed flight.

02:17:02.299 --> 02:17:05.299
And the first winner would be
for the first team that flew

02:17:05.299 --> 02:17:07.600
more than 20 miles an hour.

02:17:07.600 --> 02:17:13.190
And this plane achieved that,
flying out at Hanscom Airfield.

02:17:13.190 --> 02:17:16.459
So this was our first win
in the human-powered flight.

02:17:16.459 --> 02:17:18.160
Now you have to think
for just a second,

02:17:18.160 --> 02:17:19.660
and I think the
next slide will show

02:17:19.660 --> 02:17:22.010
this, what it really takes--

02:17:22.010 --> 02:17:23.370
oops.

02:17:23.370 --> 02:17:26.053
Could you go back one slide?

02:17:26.053 --> 02:17:27.219
Fig leaves is what it takes.

02:17:27.219 --> 02:17:30.090
[LAUGHTER]

02:17:30.090 --> 02:17:33.331
--what it takes for a group of
students to build one of these.

02:17:33.331 --> 02:17:35.580
I mean, first, they have to
decide they want to do it.

02:17:35.580 --> 02:17:38.345
This is a several-year effort.

02:17:38.345 --> 02:17:39.969
They have to decide
they want to do it,

02:17:39.969 --> 02:17:40.950
and they know they're
going to do it

02:17:40.950 --> 02:17:43.770
nights and weekends and holidays
and vacations and so forth.

02:17:43.770 --> 02:17:45.990
They have to design the plane.

02:17:45.990 --> 02:17:49.689
They have to raise money to
build it within the MIT system.

02:17:49.689 --> 02:17:52.230
Then they have to find the space
to build it, one of the most

02:17:52.230 --> 02:17:54.780
daunting tasks at MIT.

02:17:54.780 --> 02:17:57.299
Then they have to actually
engage in the construction,

02:17:57.299 --> 02:17:59.190
go out to the flight
facility in Bedford,

02:17:59.190 --> 02:18:01.440
put the whole thing
together, flight test it.

02:18:01.440 --> 02:18:03.660
And you flight test
human-powered airplanes,

02:18:03.660 --> 02:18:07.530
by the way, when the winds are
very low, early in the morning.

02:18:07.530 --> 02:18:09.719
And then, of course,
coordinate the whole team.

02:18:09.719 --> 02:18:12.000
It's really a marvelous
effort, and the students

02:18:12.000 --> 02:18:15.000
who participate in projects
like this in any department

02:18:15.000 --> 02:18:17.639
derive a tremendous
benefit from the education.

02:18:17.639 --> 02:18:22.860
Well, after we had won the
human-powered speed prize,

02:18:22.860 --> 02:18:26.060
there was only one grand
challenge left-- next slide--

02:18:28.900 --> 02:18:33.530
which was to reproduce the
mythical flight of Daedalus.

02:18:33.530 --> 02:18:38.480
And Daedalus, of course,
was a real person.

02:18:38.480 --> 02:18:41.809
He was an engineer in the
court of the King of Athens,

02:18:41.809 --> 02:18:45.320
who fled to Crete, where he
got in trouble with the king

02:18:45.320 --> 02:18:47.000
there and fled again.

02:18:47.000 --> 02:18:52.500
And in one of those flights, he
is said to actually have flown.

02:18:52.500 --> 02:18:55.620
Icarus, by the way, was added
by the Romans to the myth.

02:18:55.620 --> 02:18:57.990
Daedalus is a real person.

02:18:57.990 --> 02:19:02.490
He is credited with inventing
the saw and the lathe,

02:19:02.490 --> 02:19:03.750
among other things.

02:19:03.750 --> 02:19:06.629
Icarus was added by the Romans
to make the moral of the story,

02:19:06.629 --> 02:19:08.019
listen to your father.

02:19:08.019 --> 02:19:10.840
[LAUGHTER]

02:19:10.840 --> 02:19:14.799
So if I could have
the last video now--

02:19:14.799 --> 02:19:18.334
this team of students
created this airplane.

02:19:18.334 --> 02:19:19.000
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

02:19:19.000 --> 02:19:21.250
- Everyone who has witnessed
the flight of the Monarch

02:19:21.250 --> 02:19:23.379
agrees it is truly
a thrilling sight

02:19:23.379 --> 02:19:25.809
and almost takes you back
to the days of early flight

02:19:25.809 --> 02:19:27.730
with Wilbur and Orville Wright.

02:19:27.730 --> 02:19:29.980
And the MIT students
tell us they fulfilled

02:19:29.980 --> 02:19:32.469
all the requirements,
followed all the rules,

02:19:32.469 --> 02:19:34.180
and they're just waiting--

02:19:34.180 --> 02:19:39.430
CRAWLEY: This is Daedalus taking
off for an airport in Crete.

02:19:39.430 --> 02:19:43.690
This is Professor Musolari
in the chase boat.

02:19:43.690 --> 02:19:45.530
The flight of Daedalus--

02:19:45.530 --> 02:19:48.970
there is no agreement amongst
the historians and mythologies

02:19:48.970 --> 02:19:52.270
about which two islands
he flew between.

02:19:52.270 --> 02:19:57.310
So we flew from Crete,
the capital of Knossos,

02:19:57.310 --> 02:19:59.350
to the island of
Santorini, which

02:19:59.350 --> 02:20:02.860
is agreed to be amongst one
of the probable courses.

02:20:02.860 --> 02:20:04.240
It's about a three-hour flight.

02:20:04.240 --> 02:20:06.670
It's about 80 miles.

02:20:06.670 --> 02:20:09.362
We had four pilots in training--

02:20:09.362 --> 02:20:11.320
I believe four or five
pilots in training-- one

02:20:11.320 --> 02:20:14.950
of whom was a Greek
Olympic bicycle athlete.

02:20:14.950 --> 02:20:17.830
They were all
Olympic-caliber bicyclists.

02:20:17.830 --> 02:20:21.310
And they rotated, just like
the Mercury astronauts.

02:20:21.310 --> 02:20:24.160
And on the morning of the
actual good conditions,

02:20:24.160 --> 02:20:26.980
it turned out that it was the
Greeks day, which made everyone

02:20:26.980 --> 02:20:29.530
in Greece very happy,
that a Greek man actually

02:20:29.530 --> 02:20:31.120
got the flight.

02:20:31.120 --> 02:20:34.480
So we take off from
Crete, in the background

02:20:34.480 --> 02:20:36.623
there, at a Greek
military installation.

02:20:39.730 --> 02:20:43.575
Why don't you just turn up
the volume enough so the--

02:20:43.575 --> 02:20:45.066
- It's better than perfect.

02:20:45.066 --> 02:20:48.420
My pulse now goes
to 30s, upper 30s.

02:20:48.420 --> 02:20:51.430
CRAWLEY: Among other things,
we invented human fuel

02:20:51.430 --> 02:20:52.680
in the course of this project.

02:20:52.680 --> 02:20:54.888
- The Greek Navy, Coast
Guard, and Air Force are on--

02:20:54.888 --> 02:20:57.030
CRAWLEY: The pilot actually
got dribbled to him

02:20:57.030 --> 02:21:00.700
a specially-prepared
solution of sugars.

02:21:00.700 --> 02:21:04.200
and such carbohydrates
so that he can basically

02:21:04.200 --> 02:21:05.190
suck human fuel.

02:21:05.190 --> 02:21:06.759
If you could do
that in a marathon,

02:21:06.759 --> 02:21:07.800
you'd never hit the wall.

02:21:07.800 --> 02:21:12.462
- The only concern is that
it will all be over too soon.

02:21:12.462 --> 02:21:14.170
CRAWLEY: On the actual
day of the flight,

02:21:14.170 --> 02:21:15.930
there was a
considerable tailwind.

02:21:15.930 --> 02:21:19.450
So although we were only
flying about 15 knots,

02:21:19.450 --> 02:21:23.500
the speed over the ground or
over the bottom of the ocean

02:21:23.500 --> 02:21:26.720
was about 20 knots.

02:21:26.720 --> 02:21:30.080
- The only landmarks now are
the world records falling one

02:21:30.080 --> 02:21:31.680
by one.

02:21:31.680 --> 02:21:34.610
First down is the Gossamer
Albatross straight-line record

02:21:34.610 --> 02:21:35.249
of 22 miles.

02:21:35.249 --> 02:21:37.040
CRAWLEY: That was the
cross-channel flight.

02:21:37.040 --> 02:21:39.350
- Then, it's the Light
Eagle's absolute distance

02:21:39.350 --> 02:21:40.740
mark at 37 miles.

02:21:40.740 --> 02:21:42.420
CRAWLEY: Which was
one of our planes.

02:21:42.420 --> 02:21:45.000
- Finally, the Gossamer
Albatross duration

02:21:45.000 --> 02:21:47.730
record at two hours
and 49 minutes.

02:21:47.730 --> 02:21:49.840
CRAWLEY: That was the
cross-channel flight.

02:21:49.840 --> 02:21:52.120
- But the records have
become almost a footnote

02:21:52.120 --> 02:21:53.170
to the experience.

02:21:56.080 --> 02:21:58.430
The greater
satisfaction now comes

02:21:58.430 --> 02:22:00.940
from the realization
of a dream--

02:22:00.940 --> 02:22:05.360
a modern mythical creature,
half man, half machine,

02:22:05.360 --> 02:22:07.685
living out the earliest
fantasy of flight.

02:22:13.800 --> 02:22:16.080
Almost four hours
into the journey,

02:22:16.080 --> 02:22:21.786
Daedalus approaches its final
goal, the island of Santorini.

02:22:21.786 --> 02:22:22.770
- OK.

02:22:22.770 --> 02:22:23.440
Santorini Beach.

02:22:23.440 --> 02:22:24.900
This is Command Boat.

02:22:24.900 --> 02:22:29.480
We're going to want to get
ready to lay the smoke for us.

02:22:29.480 --> 02:22:32.301
- It's just over those boats.

02:22:32.301 --> 02:22:34.990
- I see it.

02:22:34.990 --> 02:22:38.170
- The flight has been
flawless, almost routine.

02:22:38.170 --> 02:22:41.500
Canellos could probably
keep going for hours,

02:22:41.500 --> 02:22:46.360
but the gods are not going to
let the mortals off so easily.

02:22:46.360 --> 02:22:48.380
The wind has suddenly picked up.

02:22:48.380 --> 02:22:51.380
- Steve, the smoke
is coming out.

02:22:51.380 --> 02:22:54.040
- Canellos we'll have to
come in parallel to the beach

02:22:54.040 --> 02:22:57.050
for an upwind landing.

02:22:57.050 --> 02:22:59.650
- OK, let's get the beach
clear, beach people.

02:22:59.650 --> 02:23:02.060
And Canellos, I want 20
degrees to the right.

02:23:02.060 --> 02:23:04.140
Give me a good right turn.

02:23:04.140 --> 02:23:05.515
I want to give
you plenty of room

02:23:05.515 --> 02:23:07.090
to approach that landing site.

02:23:07.090 --> 02:23:08.190
Over.

02:23:08.190 --> 02:23:10.180
- OK, don't worry.

02:23:10.180 --> 02:23:11.020
- I never worry.

02:23:11.020 --> 02:23:12.330
OK, you look good now.

02:23:16.266 --> 02:23:20.202
[INDISTINCT CHATTER]

02:23:22.125 --> 02:23:23.770
OK, we've got a
big headwind here.

02:23:23.770 --> 02:23:26.266
We're barely make
it any way at all.

02:23:26.266 --> 02:23:28.420
Canellos, that looks good.

02:23:28.420 --> 02:23:29.740
Maintain this heading.

02:23:29.740 --> 02:23:33.690
There are people running
on the beach now.

02:23:33.690 --> 02:23:36.080
- I think there is
a lot of people.

02:23:36.080 --> 02:23:37.950
- OK, well don't
worry about that.

02:23:37.950 --> 02:23:39.940
We're going to land
where they aren't.

02:23:39.940 --> 02:23:42.100
- Caught in the strong
headwind, Canellos

02:23:42.100 --> 02:23:45.580
is having trouble
approaching the beach.

02:23:45.580 --> 02:23:47.872
- OK, maintain that heading.

02:23:47.872 --> 02:23:52.780
CRAWLEY: So you can see he's
about 30 yards from Santorini.

02:23:52.780 --> 02:23:55.330
- OK, you're looking
good, Canellos.

02:23:55.330 --> 02:23:57.010
A little bit of a
right-hand correction.

02:23:57.010 --> 02:24:00.306
A little bit of
right-hand correction.

02:24:00.306 --> 02:24:02.393
Be easy-- uh oh.

02:24:02.393 --> 02:24:02.892
Uh oh.

02:24:05.610 --> 02:24:09.240
- A gust of wind snapped the
tail boom and then the wing,

02:24:09.240 --> 02:24:12.750
turning a Daedelus into Icarus.

02:24:12.750 --> 02:24:13.950
CRAWLEY: Listen to your dad.

02:24:13.950 --> 02:24:15.582
- The first thought is for
the safety of Canellos.

02:24:15.582 --> 02:24:17.123
CRAWLEY: You can
cut the video there.

02:24:17.123 --> 02:24:18.534
Thanks.

02:24:18.534 --> 02:24:20.994
[END PLAYBACK]

02:24:20.994 --> 02:24:22.962
I was not on the
beach that morning.

02:24:22.962 --> 02:24:24.438
Yeah.

02:24:24.438 --> 02:24:27.882
[APPLAUSE]

02:24:33.292 --> 02:24:35.000
That applause, of
course, is for the team

02:24:35.000 --> 02:24:36.590
of students and
faculty who did that,

02:24:36.590 --> 02:24:37.756
of which I was not a member.

02:24:37.756 --> 02:24:42.020
But I will accept
it on their behalf.

02:24:42.020 --> 02:24:43.730
I was not there that
morning, but I'm

02:24:43.730 --> 02:24:47.060
told by John Langford, who was
the project leader, that if you

02:24:47.060 --> 02:24:50.180
don't believe in
the gods of Olympus,

02:24:50.180 --> 02:24:53.600
that their influence that
day was just palpable.

02:24:53.600 --> 02:24:56.300
They were going to let
these mortals get so far.

02:24:56.300 --> 02:24:58.490
[LAUGHTER]

02:24:58.490 --> 02:25:01.340
And then they were just going
to reach down at the last second

02:25:01.340 --> 02:25:02.840
and crush that little airplane.

02:25:05.480 --> 02:25:08.150
Well, in conclusion,
Barton Rogers

02:25:08.150 --> 02:25:11.132
said some interesting things--

02:25:11.132 --> 02:25:12.590
that we should
provide an education

02:25:12.590 --> 02:25:15.050
relevant to an
industrializing nation.

02:25:18.010 --> 02:25:20.740
Maybe now, we'd say it's an
industrialized or maybe even

02:25:20.740 --> 02:25:22.890
post-industrial nation.

02:25:22.890 --> 02:25:26.570
That our education should
be pragmatic and practical,

02:25:26.570 --> 02:25:28.310
that we should have
teaching coupled

02:25:28.310 --> 02:25:32.270
with hands-on education, and
that technical education is

02:25:32.270 --> 02:25:35.930
an appropriate basis for
a professional education

02:25:35.930 --> 02:25:37.700
at the undergraduate level.

02:25:37.700 --> 02:25:41.420
That is the founding
dream of the Institute.

02:25:41.420 --> 02:25:43.550
I think that the more
we keep that in mind,

02:25:43.550 --> 02:25:46.520
and the more we keep pushing
ourselves towards that goal,

02:25:46.520 --> 02:25:50.030
the better job we do for
our students and our future.

02:25:50.030 --> 02:25:52.505
But Barton Rogers never
said it couldn't be fun.

02:25:55.102 --> 02:25:56.560
That was something
that sort of got

02:25:56.560 --> 02:26:00.800
added in the interpretation
over the years.

02:26:00.800 --> 02:26:03.200
So we try and keep
it fun, as well.

02:26:03.200 --> 02:26:05.920
And in fact, we think
it's so much fun

02:26:05.920 --> 02:26:10.120
that we've decided to create a
center for sports engineering

02:26:10.120 --> 02:26:12.180
here at MIT.

02:26:12.180 --> 02:26:15.490
And partly inspired by
preparing for this talk,

02:26:15.490 --> 02:26:17.170
we pulled together
all of the faculty

02:26:17.170 --> 02:26:19.540
who were involved in this and
who had done these things,

02:26:19.540 --> 02:26:21.760
and we said, boy, there's
really something here.

02:26:21.760 --> 02:26:24.580
So we've put together a
cooperative group of people

02:26:24.580 --> 02:26:28.150
from Aero-Astro, Warren
Seering's NSF Center

02:26:28.150 --> 02:26:30.460
for Innovation and
Product Development,

02:26:30.460 --> 02:26:33.235
and the Department of Athletics,
who are thrilled by this,

02:26:33.235 --> 02:26:35.560
by the way, because
now they have

02:26:35.560 --> 02:26:37.330
some access into the
intellectual life

02:26:37.330 --> 02:26:38.398
of the Institute.

02:26:38.398 --> 02:26:44.140
[LAUGHTER]

02:26:44.140 --> 02:26:48.550
And our goal is to create
new sports, new products,

02:26:48.550 --> 02:26:50.677
and new standards for sports.

02:26:50.677 --> 02:26:52.510
We're going to start
this in the fall of '97

02:26:52.510 --> 02:26:56.399
with some freshmen
seminars, and this activity

02:26:56.399 --> 02:26:58.690
is going to be home-based
and the Wright brothers' wind

02:26:58.690 --> 02:27:01.531
tunnel, which is a big enough
wind tunnel to stand bicyclists

02:27:01.531 --> 02:27:03.280
and tennis players up
and actually measure

02:27:03.280 --> 02:27:04.630
their aerodynamics.

02:27:04.630 --> 02:27:08.950
So there is, in some senses,
a long-standing legacy

02:27:08.950 --> 02:27:11.140
of this presentation
this morning.

02:27:11.140 --> 02:27:13.356
Well, I enjoyed
putting this together.

02:27:13.356 --> 02:27:16.814
[APPLAUSE]

02:27:36.120 --> 02:27:38.880
And I have the honor of being
followed here on the podium

02:27:38.880 --> 02:27:42.870
this morning by one of my
truly esteemed colleagues,

02:27:42.870 --> 02:27:45.180
who has, in fact,
been the guy who

02:27:45.180 --> 02:27:48.450
has shown many of us
the way to incorporate

02:27:48.450 --> 02:27:50.970
design and engineering into
the undergraduate curriculum.

02:27:50.970 --> 02:27:54.362
Professor Woodie Flowers.

02:27:54.362 --> 02:27:55.838
FLOWERS: Thank you, sir.

02:27:55.838 --> 02:27:59.774
[APPLAUSE]

02:28:04.694 --> 02:28:08.630
[LAUGHTER]

02:28:09.614 --> 02:28:13.058
[APPLAUSE]

02:28:15.060 --> 02:28:16.170
Good morning.

02:28:16.170 --> 02:28:17.318
More about that later.

02:28:21.900 --> 02:28:23.710
It's great to be here.

02:28:23.710 --> 02:28:24.270
Welcome back.

02:28:24.270 --> 02:28:26.430
I've seen some very young
people in the audience,

02:28:26.430 --> 02:28:29.700
so we probably should do a duel.

02:28:29.700 --> 02:28:33.160
There's some people
here for the first time.

02:28:33.160 --> 02:28:35.440
I'm really glad you could come.

02:28:35.440 --> 02:28:37.860
It's a very, very
special event when

02:28:37.860 --> 02:28:45.660
you get a thousand MIT alums and
their family members in a room.

02:28:45.660 --> 02:28:46.630
It's impressive.

02:28:46.630 --> 02:28:49.980
There is a lot of
power here today.

02:28:49.980 --> 02:28:52.580
In fact, you're a wonderfully
intimidating audience,

02:28:52.580 --> 02:28:56.340
and I'm looking forward
to talking to you.

02:28:56.340 --> 02:28:59.010
The opportunity cost of
this meeting is really high,

02:28:59.010 --> 02:29:01.980
so I will try to make
it worth your while.

02:29:01.980 --> 02:29:06.690
Now, the title of my talk
was a bit presumptuous,

02:29:06.690 --> 02:29:08.490
but the real title wouldn't fit.

02:29:08.490 --> 02:29:10.830
The words that I've
added at the top

02:29:10.830 --> 02:29:13.100
are to make it a
little more humble.

02:29:13.100 --> 02:29:17.880
I don't know about hard fun, but
I think there are some things.

02:29:17.880 --> 02:29:20.190
I think that you guys
really know quite a lot.

02:29:23.830 --> 02:29:25.690
Dave, if you could
get on focus--

02:29:29.220 --> 02:29:30.450
you can read that list.

02:29:30.450 --> 02:29:33.450
The examples are going
to be five examples--

02:29:33.450 --> 02:29:35.160
I will warn you,
I'm going to go fast

02:29:35.160 --> 02:29:38.160
because I want you to
get to lunch on time.

02:29:38.160 --> 02:29:41.760
I have 10 and 1/2 minutes of
video and about 100 slides,

02:29:41.760 --> 02:29:44.170
so heads up.

02:29:44.170 --> 02:29:45.970
But I will get you there.

02:29:45.970 --> 02:29:48.300
So what is hard fun?

02:29:48.300 --> 02:29:51.090
Well, I don't know.

02:29:51.090 --> 02:29:53.250
There are lots of
ways to interpret it.

02:29:53.250 --> 02:29:56.160
Several people have talked
about it this morning.

02:29:56.160 --> 02:29:59.140
This is hard fun for me.

02:29:59.140 --> 02:30:01.390
You're, as I said, an
intimidating audience,

02:30:01.390 --> 02:30:04.330
so it was really hard
to think about what

02:30:04.330 --> 02:30:06.420
I might say to you
that might represent

02:30:06.420 --> 02:30:09.270
a reasonably cohesive message.

02:30:09.270 --> 02:30:13.050
So I think that hard fun lines
at the intersection of learning

02:30:13.050 --> 02:30:18.690
and hard work and some fun,
and I think you guys probably

02:30:18.690 --> 02:30:21.030
really understand that.

02:30:21.030 --> 02:30:25.440
And I think Vladimir was right--

02:30:25.440 --> 02:30:29.070
you do have to have both,
and you do have to mix them.

02:30:29.070 --> 02:30:32.730
But I think this quote probably
talks more directly to you

02:30:32.730 --> 02:30:34.394
guys than the other.

02:30:34.394 --> 02:30:36.810
You can't make people happy
by taking away the things that

02:30:36.810 --> 02:30:38.880
make them unhappy.

02:30:38.880 --> 02:30:42.690
You have spent your
careers doing hard fun

02:30:42.690 --> 02:30:47.190
and using hard fun to obtain
various kinds of satisfaction,

02:30:47.190 --> 02:30:51.450
some of them very transient or
orgasmic, and some of them long

02:30:51.450 --> 02:30:53.520
and subtle things
that develop that have

02:30:53.520 --> 02:30:55.350
to do with satisfaction
with the life

02:30:55.350 --> 02:30:57.730
of your family and your career.

02:30:57.730 --> 02:31:04.110
But you have engaged in lots
of hard fun, so I defer to you.

02:31:04.110 --> 02:31:07.020
You probably know
more about it than me.

02:31:07.020 --> 02:31:09.960
In the talk, I'm going to make
reference to several authors.

02:31:09.960 --> 02:31:13.020
I'm going to cop out
and talk about maybe

02:31:13.020 --> 02:31:17.170
what some other people might
have said about the topic.

02:31:17.170 --> 02:31:19.290
And it's appropriate
to start with seasons.

02:31:19.290 --> 02:31:21.780
For the ladies in the audience,
you can think passages.

02:31:21.780 --> 02:31:25.530
But I'm reminded of the
infamous survey which

02:31:25.530 --> 02:31:29.730
discovered that MIT alums about
five years out think, boy, I

02:31:29.730 --> 02:31:32.280
wish I'd learned calculus
and physics a little better.

02:31:32.280 --> 02:31:34.342
And about 15 years
out they say, ah,

02:31:34.342 --> 02:31:36.300
if I'd just gotten the
economics and management

02:31:36.300 --> 02:31:37.780
stuff down a little better.

02:31:37.780 --> 02:31:39.910
And then about 20
or 30 years later,

02:31:39.910 --> 02:31:42.360
they say, boy, if I
just studied philosophy.

02:31:42.360 --> 02:31:45.450
I think I have shifted.

02:31:45.450 --> 02:31:48.945
I'm kind of on schedule,
and I'm changing.

02:31:51.450 --> 02:31:54.970
This process started
for me in about 1970.

02:31:54.970 --> 02:31:59.250
I don't know exactly when
this picture was taken.

02:31:59.250 --> 02:32:01.882
My hair was a little
denser at that point

02:32:01.882 --> 02:32:04.030
and a little more plentiful.

02:32:04.030 --> 02:32:06.930
But if I were beginning
this talk then,

02:32:06.930 --> 02:32:09.390
I would have talked more about
the details of the machines

02:32:09.390 --> 02:32:10.390
and the process.

02:32:10.390 --> 02:32:15.390
But I want to pull back today
and talk in a more general way.

02:32:15.390 --> 02:32:17.800
Now, as I said, I don't
think we know the answers,

02:32:17.800 --> 02:32:19.550
but we have learned
a few things,

02:32:19.550 --> 02:32:21.760
and I'd like to give
you some examples.

02:32:21.760 --> 02:32:24.450
I'm going to stick to
things that I know about

02:32:24.450 --> 02:32:27.730
because I was directly involved
with them and therefore

02:32:27.730 --> 02:32:31.650
maybe a little safer
in making comments.

02:32:31.650 --> 02:32:34.320
First, I'd like to show you a
video about the 2.70 contest.

02:32:34.320 --> 02:32:39.090
It is a whole bunch of
students, a kit of materials,

02:32:39.090 --> 02:32:41.850
and for the most part, it's
a sophomore-level course.

02:32:41.850 --> 02:32:44.430
For the most part, they're
designing and building

02:32:44.430 --> 02:32:46.600
something for the first
time in their life.

02:32:46.600 --> 02:32:50.202
So if we could have the
first video, please.

02:32:50.202 --> 02:32:52.200
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

02:32:52.200 --> 02:32:54.570
- Christina's deadly
wedge is a perfect example

02:32:54.570 --> 02:32:56.399
of the fast and low
design strategy.

02:32:56.399 --> 02:32:56.940
- You got it.

02:32:56.940 --> 02:32:57.439
You got it.

02:32:57.439 --> 02:32:59.400
Oh yeah.

02:32:59.400 --> 02:33:01.518
- She notches yet another
convincing victory,

02:33:01.518 --> 02:33:06.458
as more and more
opponents bit the dust.

02:33:06.458 --> 02:33:09.602
FLOWERS: This is a three-minute
clip from the program Discover.

02:33:09.602 --> 02:33:11.310
- --I had my thing
right underneath them,

02:33:11.310 --> 02:33:12.810
and you couldn't
get into the area.

02:33:12.810 --> 02:33:16.030
- This power shovel machine
is also moving up fast.

02:33:16.030 --> 02:33:17.900
Tony chose a good design
and built it well.

02:33:17.900 --> 02:33:18.858
- I took a lot of time.

02:33:18.858 --> 02:33:21.180
It took me a long
time, but I thought

02:33:21.180 --> 02:33:25.530
that I think a lot of
care in what I built.

02:33:25.530 --> 02:33:27.990
- Tony deploys his
shovel and moves steadily

02:33:27.990 --> 02:33:29.730
toward the summit.

02:33:29.730 --> 02:33:32.670
He rocks the joystick
confidently and gets the shovel

02:33:32.670 --> 02:33:33.820
under the other machine.

02:33:33.820 --> 02:33:37.236
[AUDIENCE CHEERING]

02:33:41.630 --> 02:33:43.370
It's another win
for Tony, and he

02:33:43.370 --> 02:33:48.780
advances toward the semifinal
round of the competition.

02:33:48.780 --> 02:33:50.670
Every battle now
is a fierce one,

02:33:50.670 --> 02:33:52.588
and the losers go out in style.

02:33:52.588 --> 02:33:56.004
[AUDIENCE CHEERING AND LAUGHING]

02:33:57.429 --> 02:33:59.720
- There's really only one
person I'm really scared now,

02:33:59.720 --> 02:34:02.340
and I think I have to go
against her next, too.

02:34:02.340 --> 02:34:04.978
- Christina and her wedge
machine Kee's low rider

02:34:04.978 --> 02:34:06.874
in the first semifinal match.

02:34:09.720 --> 02:34:11.460
They both hope to
plant their ping pong

02:34:11.460 --> 02:34:15.170
balls on the mountain top,
but Christina is faster.

02:34:15.170 --> 02:34:17.970
Her wedge gets under
Kee's defenses,

02:34:17.970 --> 02:34:20.076
and gravity does the rest.

02:34:20.076 --> 02:34:23.492
[AUDIENCE CHEERING]

02:34:24.956 --> 02:34:26.420
- She has the best car here.

02:34:26.420 --> 02:34:29.360
She's going to win, no problem.

02:34:29.360 --> 02:34:32.000
- Now it's Bob with an arm
machine up against Tony

02:34:32.000 --> 02:34:34.010
in the other semifinal.

02:34:34.010 --> 02:34:36.970
Tony takes off confidently
and lowers the shovel.

02:34:36.970 --> 02:34:39.040
Bob rolls up to
meet him, but Tony

02:34:39.040 --> 02:34:42.520
has something in reserve,
a design that combines

02:34:42.520 --> 02:34:45.350
the best of both strategies.

02:34:45.350 --> 02:34:48.560
He raises a short, stubby
arm, backs into the zone,

02:34:48.560 --> 02:34:50.378
and pulls off another win.

02:34:50.378 --> 02:34:54.770
[AUDIENCE CHEERING]

02:34:54.770 --> 02:34:59.170
And so Tony lines up against
Christina in the final battle.

02:34:59.170 --> 02:35:00.120
- Ready.

02:35:00.120 --> 02:35:01.545
Get set.

02:35:01.545 --> 02:35:03.450
Go.

02:35:03.450 --> 02:35:05.460
- Christina races toward
the top in pursuit

02:35:05.460 --> 02:35:08.550
of another quick victory,
but Tony digs in and waits

02:35:08.550 --> 02:35:11.000
for her to release her ball.

02:35:11.000 --> 02:35:13.540
The power shovel
gets under her wedge.

02:35:13.540 --> 02:35:14.980
She's almost upended.

02:35:14.980 --> 02:35:17.376
Tony goes to work on the
defenseless ping pong ball.

02:35:17.376 --> 02:35:18.334
- Back up.

02:35:18.334 --> 02:35:19.320
Back up.

02:35:19.320 --> 02:35:19.820
Now back up.

02:35:19.820 --> 02:35:21.380
Back up again.

02:35:21.380 --> 02:35:26.619
- To heighten the victory, Tony
fires his arm, and he wins.

02:35:26.619 --> 02:35:30.112
[AUDIENCE CHEERING]

02:35:33.605 --> 02:35:35.601
[END PLAYBACK]

02:35:35.601 --> 02:35:40.090
[APPLAUSE]

02:35:40.090 --> 02:35:41.340
FLOWERS: Remember all of that.

02:35:41.340 --> 02:35:42.986
There will be a quiz later.

02:35:42.986 --> 02:35:44.610
I'd like to get on
with another example

02:35:44.610 --> 02:35:47.550
and tell you a little bit about
an organization called FIRST,

02:35:47.550 --> 02:35:50.220
which is founded by a
wonderfully crazy, wild,

02:35:50.220 --> 02:35:52.910
and successful man, an
extremely intelligent man named

02:35:52.910 --> 02:35:54.090
Dean Kamen.

02:35:54.090 --> 02:35:56.310
FIRST has been
around for six years.

02:35:56.310 --> 02:36:01.770
Now, the design competition part
of it started just after Dean

02:36:01.770 --> 02:36:05.580
and I met, and it's a little
bit like 2,70 on steroids.

02:36:05.580 --> 02:36:10.350
The recent competition
involved 155 teams.

02:36:10.350 --> 02:36:12.930
There was a regional in
Chicago, in New Hampshire,

02:36:12.930 --> 02:36:16.920
and in New Jersey, and then
the Nationals were at Epcot.

02:36:16.920 --> 02:36:19.860
We're looking forward to a very,
very big year this coming year.

02:36:19.860 --> 02:36:23.130
In the last year, President
Best was kind enough

02:36:23.130 --> 02:36:25.110
to sponsor the MIT team.

02:36:25.110 --> 02:36:27.490
If you really love
me, during this video,

02:36:27.490 --> 02:36:30.240
you will write down
that 800 number--

02:36:30.240 --> 02:36:33.090
in particular, if you're
involved with a company

02:36:33.090 --> 02:36:35.070
that might be
interested in working

02:36:35.070 --> 02:36:38.610
with MIT and a high school
in order to do this.

02:36:38.610 --> 02:36:40.110
The video that I
want you to see now

02:36:40.110 --> 02:36:43.410
is a three-minute
rap show that was

02:36:43.410 --> 02:36:46.410
part of the introduction
to the awards ceremony

02:36:46.410 --> 02:36:52.790
at the recent finals
at Epcot in Florida.

02:36:52.790 --> 02:36:55.784
So if we can start
the second video.

02:36:55.784 --> 02:36:59.776
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

02:36:59.776 --> 02:37:03.269
[UPBEAT ELECTRONIC MUSIC]

02:37:29.716 --> 02:37:33.209
That's Dean Kamen.

02:37:33.209 --> 02:40:14.801
[UPBEAT ELECTRONIC MUSIC]

02:40:14.801 --> 02:40:15.384
[END PLAYBACK]

02:40:15.384 --> 02:40:16.080
[APPLAUSE]

02:40:16.080 --> 02:40:20.430
So that was a--

02:40:20.430 --> 02:40:22.230
unless something
unexpected happens,

02:40:22.230 --> 02:40:24.540
you will all get a
chance to see some

02:40:24.540 --> 02:40:29.100
of the stories associated with
FIRST in the fall or winter.

02:40:29.100 --> 02:40:30.700
Sorry I can't say
more about that,

02:40:30.700 --> 02:40:32.370
but I think there's
a good chance

02:40:32.370 --> 02:40:35.130
that lots of folks in the
country will know about it.

02:40:35.130 --> 02:40:37.470
Now, the other thing
I'd like to show you

02:40:37.470 --> 02:40:41.845
is a quick video clip which was
very personal to me, if we can

02:40:41.845 --> 02:40:43.560
start the next video, please.

02:40:51.890 --> 02:41:00.260
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

02:41:00.260 --> 02:41:04.550
- At GTE, the power is on.

02:41:04.550 --> 02:41:07.170
- Hi, my name's Woodie Flowers.

02:41:07.170 --> 02:41:09.530
I know that sounds like the
name of a cartoon character,

02:41:09.530 --> 02:41:12.920
but it's easy to remember, and
it's always served me well.

02:41:12.920 --> 02:41:15.560
I'm actually a professor at
MIT with a lot of curiosity

02:41:15.560 --> 02:41:17.630
about the world around
me and how it works,

02:41:17.630 --> 02:41:21.170
which is why I'm standing
here dressed like a skydiver.

02:41:21.170 --> 02:41:22.850
This new series is
going to explore

02:41:22.850 --> 02:41:24.530
the frontiers of
science, and I'm

02:41:24.530 --> 02:41:26.660
going to get to go
along for the ride.

02:41:26.660 --> 02:41:30.740
I'm going to ask some questions,
maybe answer a few, poke fun

02:41:30.740 --> 02:41:34.100
occasionally, and test some
of the frontiers myself, which

02:41:34.100 --> 02:41:36.554
brings me back to
the skydiving outfit.

02:41:36.554 --> 02:41:37.970
I've always wondered
what it would

02:41:37.970 --> 02:41:40.751
be like to jump out of a plane.

02:41:40.751 --> 02:41:42.242
So I'm going to find out.

02:41:42.242 --> 02:41:45.721
[TRIUMPHANT MUSIC]

02:41:48.206 --> 02:41:52.182
[PLANE ENGINE RUNNING]

02:41:52.182 --> 02:41:54.170
- Make sure your head's
up, nice and high.

02:41:54.170 --> 02:41:55.164
Hard arch.

02:42:12.559 --> 02:42:14.528
- It's fantastic.

02:42:14.528 --> 02:42:17.881
Oh, I hope you guys can hear me.

02:42:21.520 --> 02:42:22.020
- All right.

02:42:22.020 --> 02:42:23.460
Give me a left turn, Woodie.

02:42:23.460 --> 02:42:24.300
Give me a left turn.

02:42:24.300 --> 02:42:25.410
- OK.

02:42:25.410 --> 02:42:26.880
- Left turn, left
turn, left turn.

02:42:26.880 --> 02:42:27.810
Stop, left turn.

02:42:27.810 --> 02:42:29.810
- That was a nice, swift turn.

02:42:29.810 --> 02:42:31.810
This is a 3D sportscard.

02:42:34.810 --> 02:42:36.310
Here I come.

02:42:36.310 --> 02:42:38.310
- Three, two, one.

02:42:38.310 --> 02:42:40.310
Clear.

02:42:40.310 --> 02:42:42.140
All right, great.

02:42:42.140 --> 02:42:43.300
[END PLAYBACK]

02:42:43.300 --> 02:42:45.860
FLOWERS: There will be a
quiz on that, too, later.

02:42:45.860 --> 02:42:47.750
So those are three examples.

02:42:47.750 --> 02:42:49.690
Let me move on to some more.

02:42:49.690 --> 02:42:54.070
We talk a little bit about
our senior Product Engineering

02:42:54.070 --> 02:42:57.941
course and what we do there.

02:42:57.941 --> 02:43:00.430
We're really mean
to the students.

02:43:00.430 --> 02:43:02.380
In order to force some
of the issues that

02:43:02.380 --> 02:43:04.780
are very important
to engineering

02:43:04.780 --> 02:43:08.310
teamwork and projects, we
give them the problem too big

02:43:08.310 --> 02:43:10.220
in a time too short
and groups too large

02:43:10.220 --> 02:43:11.650
and a budget too small--

02:43:11.650 --> 02:43:13.300
very close simulation
to the stuff

02:43:13.300 --> 02:43:15.230
that Steve talked about earlier.

02:43:15.230 --> 02:43:20.650
Now the students' reaction
is, as you might expect,

02:43:20.650 --> 02:43:24.820
but they've really been
quite fantastic in the past.

02:43:24.820 --> 02:43:26.759
They start the process
with more meetings

02:43:26.759 --> 02:43:28.300
than they've ever
had in their lives,

02:43:28.300 --> 02:43:29.883
and they have to
learn about meetings.

02:43:29.883 --> 02:43:31.950
And then they get into
doing some calculations

02:43:31.950 --> 02:43:33.449
and using their
engineering science,

02:43:33.449 --> 02:43:35.770
and they find themselves
poring over catalogs

02:43:35.770 --> 02:43:37.960
and finding out that
dealers and that vendors

02:43:37.960 --> 02:43:40.720
don't deliver when they
said they were going to.

02:43:40.720 --> 02:43:43.690
The learn a lot of lessons
about simple tools.

02:43:43.690 --> 02:43:46.720
They use some fairly
more elegant tools.

02:43:46.720 --> 02:43:49.690
They use some modern
processes in doing

02:43:49.690 --> 02:43:52.870
this wonderful job
of building mock-ups

02:43:52.870 --> 02:43:54.640
and then, finally, a prototype.

02:43:54.640 --> 02:43:57.460
And they work together.

02:43:57.460 --> 02:43:58.760
They help each other.

02:43:58.760 --> 02:43:59.900
They work very intensely.

02:43:59.900 --> 02:44:03.190
A team of 25 students who are
taking several other courses

02:44:03.190 --> 02:44:06.910
at the same time have to learn
to break the problem up, trust

02:44:06.910 --> 02:44:08.590
one another, and get it done.

02:44:08.590 --> 02:44:11.770
So there is an enormous
amount of tension associated

02:44:11.770 --> 02:44:13.280
with making this happen.

02:44:13.280 --> 02:44:16.370
But we've been doing
it for six years now,

02:44:16.370 --> 02:44:19.900
and every time, the
students have finished

02:44:19.900 --> 02:44:23.140
with a machine or a
device that met spec,

02:44:23.140 --> 02:44:25.180
was on time, and under budget.

02:44:25.180 --> 02:44:27.090
That's really quite amazing.

02:44:27.090 --> 02:44:30.880
Now they make a mess in the
process, but at the end of it,

02:44:30.880 --> 02:44:32.770
they have some quite
remarkable things.

02:44:32.770 --> 02:44:34.810
From last fall,
for example, this

02:44:34.810 --> 02:44:36.760
was a battery-operated
boogie board

02:44:36.760 --> 02:44:39.670
for kids, a little
battery-operated surfboard,

02:44:39.670 --> 02:44:41.290
essentially, a toy.

02:44:41.290 --> 02:44:43.930
Here was an industrial
scooter for use inside plants.

02:44:43.930 --> 02:44:47.260
It had a nice
extendable cargo deck.

02:44:47.260 --> 02:44:52.690
One of the machines was the
most powerful and fast sea sled

02:44:52.690 --> 02:44:55.600
for wreck divers, and
a great video of it

02:44:55.600 --> 02:44:59.890
outrunning the fastest
sled on the market today.

02:44:59.890 --> 02:45:01.900
And there was a
battery-operated wheelbarrow,

02:45:01.900 --> 02:45:04.535
which they aimed at
the domestic market

02:45:04.535 --> 02:45:06.910
and discovered that there were
lots of construction sites

02:45:06.910 --> 02:45:10.540
where, inside buildings, you
couldn't use gasoline engines,

02:45:10.540 --> 02:45:13.600
and that was a very
good idea, as well.

02:45:13.600 --> 02:45:16.960
There was a very delightful
design of a confined space

02:45:16.960 --> 02:45:18.550
rescue system.

02:45:18.550 --> 02:45:21.940
That serpentine capstone that
you see there gripped the rope

02:45:21.940 --> 02:45:24.550
and would allow a battery
pack to lift someone out

02:45:24.550 --> 02:45:29.810
of a well or some of the other
confined space rescue areas.

02:45:29.810 --> 02:45:34.840
So that was celebrated by
the Cambridge Rescue Team.

02:45:34.840 --> 02:45:36.850
And the scooter
that you just saw

02:45:36.850 --> 02:45:39.970
is a commuter scooter
that allows one to fold it

02:45:39.970 --> 02:45:41.650
down and take off in it.

02:45:41.650 --> 02:45:46.150
As you see, it works quite well,
and it's really a lot of fun.

02:45:46.150 --> 02:45:49.690
Now, they then at the
end of this process

02:45:49.690 --> 02:45:51.630
give very professional
presentations

02:45:51.630 --> 02:45:53.380
in which they present
their business plan,

02:45:53.380 --> 02:45:55.180
they present their
knowledge of the market,

02:45:55.180 --> 02:45:57.179
and they do that in front
of a critical audience

02:45:57.179 --> 02:45:58.870
and get some real
feedback about what

02:45:58.870 --> 02:46:00.880
people think of their ideas.

02:46:00.880 --> 02:46:03.430
We think that that
kind of whole process

02:46:03.430 --> 02:46:05.720
is very, very important.

02:46:05.720 --> 02:46:09.940
Now, they also experience a
kind of bonding and unity.

02:46:09.940 --> 02:46:11.890
At the end of this
whole thing, there's

02:46:11.890 --> 02:46:16.550
a lot of shared feeling of
relief, among other things.

02:46:16.550 --> 02:46:18.550
But several years
ago, for instance,

02:46:18.550 --> 02:46:21.190
when we did a battery-operated
riding lawnmowers,

02:46:21.190 --> 02:46:23.800
all the teams were anxious to
gather out in the Great Court.

02:46:23.800 --> 02:46:26.020
In fact, the entire
course gathered out

02:46:26.020 --> 02:46:27.760
in the Great Court.

02:46:27.760 --> 02:46:30.820
They'd been through something
that pulls them all together,

02:46:30.820 --> 02:46:34.510
and it had to do with pushing
the envelope as far as it would

02:46:34.510 --> 02:46:36.970
tolerate without tearing it.

02:46:36.970 --> 02:46:38.820
So it's hard fun.

02:46:38.820 --> 02:46:41.530
Let me talk a little bit
about a graduate design course

02:46:41.530 --> 02:46:45.220
that Professor David
Wallace and I teach.

02:46:45.220 --> 02:46:49.210
Most of the focus is on
aesthetics and human factors.

02:46:49.210 --> 02:46:52.510
We tell them this is a
course about process.

02:46:52.510 --> 02:46:54.190
And process is really
first, but then we

02:46:54.190 --> 02:46:56.680
say we lie-- it's about
good products, too.

02:46:56.680 --> 02:46:59.656
So the students, even
though many of them

02:46:59.656 --> 02:47:01.030
have never had a
drafting course,

02:47:01.030 --> 02:47:04.000
even do a very good job of
raising the bar for one another

02:47:04.000 --> 02:47:06.280
and doing renderings,
for example,

02:47:06.280 --> 02:47:09.179
that look quite good to
industrial designers.

02:47:09.179 --> 02:47:11.470
They're not going to take an
industrial designer's job,

02:47:11.470 --> 02:47:14.680
but they've done a lot
about communicating

02:47:14.680 --> 02:47:17.035
their ideas to other people--

02:47:17.035 --> 02:47:21.430
a very sophisticated move
forward in a very short time.

02:47:21.430 --> 02:47:24.280
They then make sketch
models out of foam,

02:47:24.280 --> 02:47:26.470
and they all assemble
together, and each person

02:47:26.470 --> 02:47:28.060
has three sketch models.

02:47:28.060 --> 02:47:31.180
And they present them,
and what they then do

02:47:31.180 --> 02:47:33.820
is exchange ideas
among one another

02:47:33.820 --> 02:47:35.410
about the sketch models.

02:47:35.410 --> 02:47:38.500
And that particular exchange
is very rich in communication.

02:47:38.500 --> 02:47:40.330
It's rich in critique.

02:47:40.330 --> 02:47:43.390
Engineers don't generally want
to have anyone criticizing

02:47:43.390 --> 02:47:45.700
their ideas, but
we try to get them

02:47:45.700 --> 02:47:48.250
to understand that critique
is not criticizing.

02:47:48.250 --> 02:47:50.450
It's helping to
develop a better idea.

02:47:50.450 --> 02:47:53.080
So they do a fantastic job
of helping one another,

02:47:53.080 --> 02:47:56.200
working together, and,
in fact, almost always,

02:47:56.200 --> 02:48:00.317
their design evolves in some
way during that process.

02:48:00.317 --> 02:48:01.900
Now, at the end of
that, they get down

02:48:01.900 --> 02:48:04.960
to the hard business of
making the real visual model,

02:48:04.960 --> 02:48:07.370
and they spend a lot of
time in the lab together.

02:48:07.370 --> 02:48:09.580
They get to know each
other very, very well,

02:48:09.580 --> 02:48:11.680
and they see one
another doing things,

02:48:11.680 --> 02:48:14.590
and they all kind of
do it better and better

02:48:14.590 --> 02:48:15.850
because of that.

02:48:15.850 --> 02:48:20.980
And as that process
moves through,

02:48:20.980 --> 02:48:22.930
all of the stuff that
each student does

02:48:22.930 --> 02:48:24.610
is done with their own hands.

02:48:24.610 --> 02:48:27.100
And they end up with a
very proud attachment

02:48:27.100 --> 02:48:28.780
to what they've done.

02:48:28.780 --> 02:48:31.820
Then, they have created
these nice visual models.

02:48:31.820 --> 02:48:34.210
This was a series that had
to do with a personal digital

02:48:34.210 --> 02:48:36.880
assistant for
traffic and weather,

02:48:36.880 --> 02:48:39.820
and the ideas are quite diverse.

02:48:39.820 --> 02:48:43.450
And it's really great to see
the range and creativity.

02:48:43.450 --> 02:48:46.120
Some of the devices are
very, very convincing.

02:48:46.120 --> 02:48:48.040
They looked like
real prototypes.

02:48:48.040 --> 02:48:50.969
What they then do--

02:48:50.969 --> 02:48:52.510
if could have the
next tray, please--

02:48:52.510 --> 02:48:58.120
is we gather a very substantial
audience for them, a jury.

02:48:58.120 --> 02:49:00.630
This last semester,
there were 35 students.

02:49:00.630 --> 02:49:02.170
We had 40 jurors.

02:49:02.170 --> 02:49:05.200
And the students present
their ideas to the jury,

02:49:05.200 --> 02:49:07.510
and the jury includes
people like Bill Mitchell,

02:49:07.510 --> 02:49:10.030
Dean of Architecture,
and representatives

02:49:10.030 --> 02:49:13.190
of most of the design
firms in the Boston area.

02:49:13.190 --> 02:49:16.360
So they get real pros
to look over their work,

02:49:16.360 --> 02:49:19.750
give them feedback, fill
out forms, exchange ideas

02:49:19.750 --> 02:49:23.650
with them, and it's done with
a great deal of enthusiasm,

02:49:23.650 --> 02:49:25.540
and the students really
get a lot out of it,

02:49:25.540 --> 02:49:28.780
and they are very anxious
to see what the people said.

02:49:28.780 --> 02:49:30.580
One of the nice things
is is that puts us

02:49:30.580 --> 02:49:34.270
in a position of being the
coach rather than the evaluator.

02:49:34.270 --> 02:49:37.960
This last semester was
wearable computers.

02:49:37.960 --> 02:49:41.200
One of the models, a
quite elegantly done model

02:49:41.200 --> 02:49:42.430
by Tom Almi.

02:49:42.430 --> 02:49:44.986
He here is getting some feedback
from an industrial designer

02:49:44.986 --> 02:49:47.110
from a local firm who had
spent quite a lot of time

02:49:47.110 --> 02:49:49.220
on exactly that same problem.

02:49:49.220 --> 02:49:51.430
A lot of rich feedback.

02:49:51.430 --> 02:49:56.480
So that process is
different than a final exam.

02:49:56.480 --> 02:49:59.550
It's very rich in
information back.

02:49:59.550 --> 02:50:01.490
So I've given you five examples.

02:50:01.490 --> 02:50:01.990
Great.

02:50:01.990 --> 02:50:05.290
A lot of good stuff happening,
but what does it mean?

02:50:05.290 --> 02:50:07.920
Well, I don't know,
but some thoughts.

02:50:11.010 --> 02:50:15.470
One is, for sure, that we
think Einstein is right.

02:50:15.470 --> 02:50:18.980
Creativity is a very, very
important part of this process,

02:50:18.980 --> 02:50:25.120
and we must overtly charge the
students with being creative.

02:50:25.120 --> 02:50:26.310
Warren Seering is right.

02:50:26.310 --> 02:50:29.012
The way you get students to
be creative is you assign it.

02:50:29.012 --> 02:50:30.720
If you don't make it
part of the process,

02:50:30.720 --> 02:50:31.761
it ain't going to happen.

02:50:31.761 --> 02:50:33.150
It's the painful part.

02:50:33.150 --> 02:50:34.580
It's not the default thing.

02:50:34.580 --> 02:50:35.490
It's risky.

02:50:35.490 --> 02:50:37.170
It makes your brain hurt.

02:50:37.170 --> 02:50:40.300
And it's delightful when we
say please do it, they do it.

02:50:40.300 --> 02:50:41.950
If you look at this
series of machines,

02:50:41.950 --> 02:50:44.580
they were all in 2.70
a year or so ago,

02:50:44.580 --> 02:50:48.030
and they were all done to
accomplish the same task.

02:50:48.030 --> 02:50:50.850
Delightful variety in
what they look like-- they

02:50:50.850 --> 02:50:52.920
were all effective
in their own ways.

02:50:52.920 --> 02:50:55.170
This one had
counter-rotating augers

02:50:55.170 --> 02:50:59.130
to screw its way through
the medium of the contest

02:50:59.130 --> 02:51:00.010
that year.

02:51:00.010 --> 02:51:02.850
Now here is Alex Slocum
with a group of the students

02:51:02.850 --> 02:51:05.410
that went off to do the
international version

02:51:05.410 --> 02:51:06.344
of the design contest.

02:51:06.344 --> 02:51:08.760
Alex has been running the
course for the last three years.

02:51:08.760 --> 02:51:10.010
Beforehand, it was Harry West.

02:51:10.010 --> 02:51:11.430
Harry did a great
job of creating

02:51:11.430 --> 02:51:14.580
the international version--
a wonderful tradition,

02:51:14.580 --> 02:51:16.090
it continues to grow.

02:51:16.090 --> 02:51:18.610
We think it has some
very powerful lessons.

02:51:18.610 --> 02:51:21.360
But the context for
all of these things

02:51:21.360 --> 02:51:27.120
is exercising, developing
your creative design skills

02:51:27.120 --> 02:51:29.620
and creating a different
self-image of yourself.

02:51:29.620 --> 02:51:33.270
You have to be able to trust
your ability to be creative.

02:51:33.270 --> 02:51:35.170
A lot of people,
including myself,

02:51:35.170 --> 02:51:37.140
think that Scott
Adams will be regarded

02:51:37.140 --> 02:51:39.690
as one of the 20th century's
great philosophers.

02:51:39.690 --> 02:51:42.260
I think that's probably
right, but here's one example.

02:51:45.463 --> 02:51:48.090
Wally and I have come up with
a great new product idea.

02:51:48.090 --> 02:51:52.240
Management inspiration alert
in effect, danger, new idea.

02:51:52.240 --> 02:51:54.050
Now, here's
Crush-o-Matic, which is

02:51:54.050 --> 02:51:58.040
about to splat the
creator of the new idea.

02:51:58.040 --> 02:52:00.711
In order to have an
impact on the world

02:52:00.711 --> 02:52:02.960
and push creative ideas, you
have to have the courage.

02:52:02.960 --> 02:52:06.700
It takes about a dozen your baby
is uglies to get one Eureka.

02:52:06.700 --> 02:52:09.470
And so you have to leave
the educational system

02:52:09.470 --> 02:52:12.890
fortified enough to take
a beating over and over

02:52:12.890 --> 02:52:15.950
and still come back
asking for more.

02:52:15.950 --> 02:52:19.559
I still remember this
student's coming into my office

02:52:19.559 --> 02:52:21.850
and crying because she didn't
want to take the courses.

02:52:21.850 --> 02:52:23.058
She had never built anything.

02:52:23.058 --> 02:52:25.010
She hated the whole
idea, and she ended up

02:52:25.010 --> 02:52:27.860
feeling so good
about having made

02:52:27.860 --> 02:52:31.190
this machine do what she
wanted it to do that she became

02:52:31.190 --> 02:52:32.510
a design engineer.

02:52:32.510 --> 02:52:35.400
That, I think, is one
of the real victories,

02:52:35.400 --> 02:52:36.990
what it's all about.

02:52:36.990 --> 02:52:40.160
Another thing that is a facet
of this hard fun activity

02:52:40.160 --> 02:52:43.310
is what I've started calling
gracious professionalism.

02:52:43.310 --> 02:52:46.010
It's really, really nice to
see students working closely

02:52:46.010 --> 02:52:51.800
together, helping one another,
treating the competition

02:52:51.800 --> 02:52:53.750
as the celebration
of the learning that

02:52:53.750 --> 02:52:56.460
has happened to that
point, which is what it is.

02:52:56.460 --> 02:52:58.310
It's not cutthroat competition.

02:52:58.310 --> 02:52:59.360
It is a celebration.

02:52:59.360 --> 02:53:00.440
The students dress up.

02:53:00.440 --> 02:53:02.360
They do it with a
great deal of style,

02:53:02.360 --> 02:53:08.420
and it's very, very nice to
see 20-year-old men giving

02:53:08.420 --> 02:53:11.460
each other a hug after they
just had a competition.

02:53:11.460 --> 02:53:13.550
That's the right
stuff, and we want

02:53:13.550 --> 02:53:15.920
very much to keep that alive.

02:53:15.920 --> 02:53:18.440
There are lots of stories
about that sort of activity

02:53:18.440 --> 02:53:20.000
in the first competition.

02:53:20.000 --> 02:53:22.520
I will spare you some of those.

02:53:22.520 --> 02:53:29.200
Now, I think Goldman has some
very, very powerful points

02:53:29.200 --> 02:53:31.720
in the book Emotional
Intelligence,

02:53:31.720 --> 02:53:36.670
and I think it is very, very
much a part of hard fun.

02:53:36.670 --> 02:53:40.180
When you push
really hard, to me,

02:53:40.180 --> 02:53:43.660
it is very clear that pressure
is a major part of creativity.

02:53:43.660 --> 02:53:47.120
We don't do creative
things for the most part,

02:53:47.120 --> 02:53:49.320
unless we have to.

02:53:49.320 --> 02:53:51.250
It's hard.

02:53:51.250 --> 02:53:54.250
We do them when we're running
or doing other things,

02:53:54.250 --> 02:53:56.740
but in fact, they
don't just fall out.

02:53:56.740 --> 02:54:00.124
So dealing with the pressure
is a very important thing.

02:54:00.124 --> 02:54:01.540
I tell the students
that they have

02:54:01.540 --> 02:54:04.540
to learn to make friends with
the knot in their stomach

02:54:04.540 --> 02:54:06.400
and recognize it as an asset.

02:54:06.400 --> 02:54:09.140
I have a knot in
my stomach today.

02:54:09.140 --> 02:54:10.810
It's part of life.

02:54:10.810 --> 02:54:11.650
It's there.

02:54:11.650 --> 02:54:13.552
You learn to use it
rather than block you.

02:54:13.552 --> 02:54:15.510
There's another part of
emotional intelligence,

02:54:15.510 --> 02:54:19.210
though, that is best illustrated
by a tragic story that

02:54:19.210 --> 02:54:20.950
goes with FIRST.

02:54:20.950 --> 02:54:24.070
In the early years,
a large company

02:54:24.070 --> 02:54:27.550
worked with a school in a very,
very rough part of the town

02:54:27.550 --> 02:54:30.280
where the company was located.

02:54:30.280 --> 02:54:33.430
And that year, the finals were
in Manchester, New Hampshire.

02:54:33.430 --> 02:54:34.240
They came.

02:54:34.240 --> 02:54:35.590
President Bush was campaigning.

02:54:35.590 --> 02:54:36.340
He came by.

02:54:36.340 --> 02:54:38.575
He met the students,
drove their machine.

02:54:38.575 --> 02:54:42.190
The students were then in a
gymnasium with several thousand

02:54:42.190 --> 02:54:44.950
people cheering, bands playing,
cheerleaders, the whole thing.

02:54:44.950 --> 02:54:48.090
It was an amazing celebration.

02:54:48.090 --> 02:54:50.331
The linkage that developed
between the high school

02:54:50.331 --> 02:54:52.080
students and the
engineers in this company

02:54:52.080 --> 02:54:53.460
was just phenomenal.

02:54:53.460 --> 02:54:58.170
One of the students got home,
returned to the same old stuff,

02:54:58.170 --> 02:55:02.130
committed suicide the next
day, and left a note explaining

02:55:02.130 --> 02:55:05.410
that, as far as he could
tell, he had reached the peak,

02:55:05.410 --> 02:55:08.290
and it was never going
to be that good again.

02:55:08.290 --> 02:55:12.330
So in that particular instance,
many, many alarms went off,

02:55:12.330 --> 02:55:15.000
and we have learned
something about re-entry.

02:55:15.000 --> 02:55:17.400
If we take people out
of an environment that

02:55:17.400 --> 02:55:21.750
has no opportunity and
bury them in opportunity,

02:55:21.750 --> 02:55:24.120
we have to be careful about
letting them slip back

02:55:24.120 --> 02:55:25.210
into that.

02:55:25.210 --> 02:55:27.990
So there are many kinds of
emotional intelligence that

02:55:27.990 --> 02:55:32.750
are, I think,
inherent in hard fun.

02:55:32.750 --> 02:55:35.340
Forgiveness is another
thing that's very important.

02:55:35.340 --> 02:55:37.340
The students have to learn
to trust one another.

02:55:37.340 --> 02:55:39.920
They have to, when
someone fails to deliver

02:55:39.920 --> 02:55:42.350
their part of
something on time, they

02:55:42.350 --> 02:55:46.140
have to forgive them,
recognize what's

02:55:46.140 --> 02:55:48.090
the agenda that
has to move ahead,

02:55:48.090 --> 02:55:50.370
and get back to the business.

02:55:50.370 --> 02:55:54.300
For me, that skydiving
thing was a wonderful piece

02:55:54.300 --> 02:55:57.000
of learning for me because
I went out the week before,

02:55:57.000 --> 02:55:59.130
and they said you can
go do one if you want

02:55:59.130 --> 02:56:00.450
to do one before the shoot.

02:56:00.450 --> 02:56:01.230
I said, great.

02:56:01.230 --> 02:56:02.760
I've always wanted to do that.

02:56:02.760 --> 02:56:04.890
I went out and went through
the training course.

02:56:04.890 --> 02:56:06.810
During the dive, you
have to do a whole bunch

02:56:06.810 --> 02:56:08.811
of things the instructors
are telling you to do.

02:56:08.811 --> 02:56:10.435
You have to look at
their ride, and you

02:56:10.435 --> 02:56:11.610
have to read your altimeter.

02:56:11.610 --> 02:56:12.810
You have to look at
the person on the left,

02:56:12.810 --> 02:56:14.910
look at the person on the right,
and I knew that the next week,

02:56:14.910 --> 02:56:16.930
I was going to be asked
to do lines in between.

02:56:16.930 --> 02:56:18.900
And you have to go
through that four cycles

02:56:18.900 --> 02:56:20.760
before you pull the cord.

02:56:20.760 --> 02:56:24.330
The jump master had to keep
me from pulling the wrong cord

02:56:24.330 --> 02:56:26.530
because I was thinking about
all these other things.

02:56:26.530 --> 02:56:28.980
And when I realized
what had happened,

02:56:28.980 --> 02:56:33.360
or what I had almost done wrong,
I was really hard on myself.

02:56:33.360 --> 02:56:37.140
And I was going into my first
TV shoot beating myself up

02:56:37.140 --> 02:56:39.974
for hours about how can I
have done something so dumb.

02:56:39.974 --> 02:56:40.890
I'm going to be there.

02:56:40.890 --> 02:56:42.070
There's going to be a
camera plane, camera

02:56:42.070 --> 02:56:43.890
in the airplane, camera
on the other guy's helmet.

02:56:43.890 --> 02:56:45.723
There's going to be all
this stuff going on,

02:56:45.723 --> 02:56:48.480
and I'm not going
do the right thing.

02:56:48.480 --> 02:56:50.520
About Thursday of
the week before I

02:56:50.520 --> 02:56:52.320
did the jump on
Saturday, I finally

02:56:52.320 --> 02:56:55.740
decided, buddy, you've got to
forgive yourself and get on

02:56:55.740 --> 02:56:56.767
with it.

02:56:56.767 --> 02:56:58.350
And I think that's
one of the lessons.

02:56:58.350 --> 02:57:00.880
Robin [? Casserman's ?]
book makes that point very,

02:57:00.880 --> 02:57:01.600
very well.

02:57:01.600 --> 02:57:04.350
I think it's an important
part of hard fun.

02:57:04.350 --> 02:57:09.480
We have to help the students
pull this nice sweep over some

02:57:09.480 --> 02:57:10.770
of the difficult parts.

02:57:10.770 --> 02:57:13.410
Partway through a
big complex project,

02:57:13.410 --> 02:57:15.330
things are going rough.

02:57:15.330 --> 02:57:18.690
But after it's
over, it feels good,

02:57:18.690 --> 02:57:20.384
and you want to
keep that in mind

02:57:20.384 --> 02:57:22.800
and recognize that that's where
you think you want to get.

02:57:25.265 --> 02:57:26.640
In thinking about
this, I thought

02:57:26.640 --> 02:57:30.330
that the cup of knowledge and
stuff is just much too big.

02:57:30.330 --> 02:57:33.540
All we can do is kind of set
the boundary conditions right.

02:57:33.540 --> 02:57:38.280
The best we can do is build a
foundation, as in the bricks,

02:57:38.280 --> 02:57:42.800
and have people start to
learn about themselves,

02:57:42.800 --> 02:57:45.950
learn about nature,
and learn about people.

02:57:45.950 --> 02:57:48.530
And I made an arrow pointing
up and then realized

02:57:48.530 --> 02:57:51.174
quite accidentally I'd
made an image of a house.

02:57:51.174 --> 02:57:53.090
And given that house of
quality is a big deal,

02:57:53.090 --> 02:57:55.400
I decided this must be
the house of qualities.

02:57:55.400 --> 02:57:58.189
But this is one of the
representations of what

02:57:58.189 --> 02:57:59.230
we're trying to do here--

02:57:59.230 --> 02:58:02.510
set the boundary conditions
right so that growth happens.

02:58:02.510 --> 02:58:04.820
Now learning about
nature is the thing

02:58:04.820 --> 02:58:09.110
that we've done well
at MIT for a long time.

02:58:09.110 --> 02:58:11.690
That might be what
you call physics.

02:58:11.690 --> 02:58:15.480
It's one of the things that you
do have to come to understand,

02:58:15.480 --> 02:58:18.080
though, is that she will not
give you a reprieve, even

02:58:18.080 --> 02:58:20.180
for a nanosecond.

02:58:20.180 --> 02:58:22.640
She sticks to the
rules all the time,

02:58:22.640 --> 02:58:24.620
and students, in their
first design project,

02:58:24.620 --> 02:58:27.340
are very, very inclined
toward wishful thinking,

02:58:27.340 --> 02:58:28.940
and that's a
designer's worst enemy.

02:58:28.940 --> 02:58:31.580
[LAUGHTER]

02:58:31.580 --> 02:58:34.680
Learning about yourself,
as I mentioned,

02:58:34.680 --> 02:58:37.490
having a creative self-image
is very, very important.

02:58:37.490 --> 02:58:39.350
You have to be able
to rely on that.

02:58:39.350 --> 02:58:41.510
You learn lots of other
things, and that list

02:58:41.510 --> 02:58:43.460
could go on and on.

02:58:43.460 --> 02:58:45.170
You learn about other people.

02:58:45.170 --> 02:58:47.090
You learn about how to
deal with other folks.

02:58:47.090 --> 02:58:49.730
This thing that I mentioned
about the bonding that

02:58:49.730 --> 02:58:51.500
happens when a team
works together,

02:58:51.500 --> 02:58:53.150
and how you have
to learn to give up

02:58:53.150 --> 02:58:56.240
part of the project and trust
somebody else to do it--

02:58:56.240 --> 02:58:57.830
the process of sharing.

02:58:57.830 --> 02:59:04.080
So another part of this thing
is learning about society--

02:59:04.080 --> 02:59:06.690
the deal that's made when
you become a professional.

02:59:06.690 --> 02:59:08.700
You get to stand
on the shoulders

02:59:08.700 --> 02:59:12.390
of the giants in exchange
for an obligation

02:59:12.390 --> 02:59:14.190
to do the right thing
when you're up there

02:59:14.190 --> 02:59:16.290
and can see a long way.

02:59:16.290 --> 02:59:18.370
Students designing this
scooter for instance,

02:59:18.370 --> 02:59:22.420
had to think really hard about
the safety implications of it,

02:59:22.420 --> 02:59:24.990
and I think they did a
very responsible job.

02:59:24.990 --> 02:59:27.300
They made sure that the
brakes were really quite good.

02:59:27.300 --> 02:59:31.710
The students that designed
that battery-operated water

02:59:31.710 --> 02:59:35.190
scooter for kids went through
a lot of experiments and stuff

02:59:35.190 --> 02:59:37.770
about the length of kids' hair
and how far back the propeller

02:59:37.770 --> 02:59:39.450
head to be from the
grid to keep air

02:59:39.450 --> 02:59:41.730
from getting trapped in there
so that no one could get

02:59:41.730 --> 02:59:44.050
held under water by it.

02:59:44.050 --> 02:59:46.300
When the projects
become real, the ethics

02:59:46.300 --> 02:59:49.420
become much more obvious
and an integral part

02:59:49.420 --> 02:59:51.730
of what they're doing.

02:59:51.730 --> 02:59:55.040
Now, another cut on
this is Gardner's.

02:59:55.040 --> 02:59:57.910
I think he's up to
eight or so now.

02:59:57.910 --> 03:00:00.740
I think eight's probably
a pretty big number,

03:00:00.740 --> 03:00:03.350
but I do think it's very clear
that there are different kinds

03:00:03.350 --> 03:00:04.700
of intelligence.

03:00:04.700 --> 03:00:08.150
Someone once summarized the
multiple intelligence thing

03:00:08.150 --> 03:00:12.650
in this way, and I think that
that's probably pretty close.

03:00:12.650 --> 03:00:15.560
We need some of all of
those, but various of us

03:00:15.560 --> 03:00:17.150
are stronger in various suits.

03:00:17.150 --> 03:00:22.370
And I think if you
look at that, there

03:00:22.370 --> 03:00:23.700
are some skills and tools--

03:00:23.700 --> 03:00:25.820
CAE, CAD, et cetera--

03:00:25.820 --> 03:00:28.910
that can help you
in things smart,

03:00:28.910 --> 03:00:32.930
but judgment is probably
what it's really about.

03:00:32.930 --> 03:00:34.970
If you look at the
people smart thing,

03:00:34.970 --> 03:00:37.460
there are some planning
tools and computer aids

03:00:37.460 --> 03:00:39.230
and lots to learn
from the Sloan School,

03:00:39.230 --> 03:00:43.800
but leadership is probably
what it's really all about.

03:00:43.800 --> 03:00:46.450
And if you look at
situation smart,

03:00:46.450 --> 03:00:49.370
there are a lot of ways to apply
logic and think very carefully,

03:00:49.370 --> 03:00:52.820
but wisdom is probably what
you're really talking about.

03:00:52.820 --> 03:00:55.400
And again, I think Einstein
was right when he said,

03:00:55.400 --> 03:00:58.760
wisdom is knowing
what's important.

03:00:58.760 --> 03:01:02.270
So we try to get students
involved in bumping

03:01:02.270 --> 03:01:03.930
into all of those things.

03:01:03.930 --> 03:01:05.750
Now, it ain't easy.

03:01:05.750 --> 03:01:07.190
There is some trouble.

03:01:07.190 --> 03:01:09.509
Things are not as simple
as they might seem.

03:01:09.509 --> 03:01:11.300
I have a doctoral
student named Ben Linder.

03:01:11.300 --> 03:01:12.380
He's a great student.

03:01:12.380 --> 03:01:16.590
He's doing a thesis on
estimation, a doctoral thesis.

03:01:16.590 --> 03:01:19.350
Because of his thesis,
we have gathered data

03:01:19.350 --> 03:01:22.770
from seniors in
Mechanical Engineering

03:01:22.770 --> 03:01:26.250
from six of the 12 most
highly-rated schools

03:01:26.250 --> 03:01:28.050
of engineering in the country.

03:01:28.050 --> 03:01:31.380
I'm just going to focus
on one part of that data.

03:01:31.380 --> 03:01:33.690
We asked them to
take five minutes

03:01:33.690 --> 03:01:39.314
to estimate the energy content
of a 9-volt transistor battery,

03:01:39.314 --> 03:01:40.980
one of these things,
one of these things

03:01:40.980 --> 03:01:43.960
that we all know very well.

03:01:43.960 --> 03:01:47.080
The data-- we have
lots of data, so it's

03:01:47.080 --> 03:01:51.440
statistically significant data.

03:01:51.440 --> 03:01:55.670
It's here.

03:01:55.670 --> 03:01:59.530
I don't expect you to see
that, but the sad thing

03:01:59.530 --> 03:02:02.800
is there's only data from
about 80% of the students

03:02:02.800 --> 03:02:06.470
because 20% of them used
the wrong units for energy.

03:02:06.470 --> 03:02:09.440
Now another cut on
this is Gardner's.

03:02:09.440 --> 03:02:12.490
I think he's up to
eight or so now.

03:02:12.490 --> 03:02:15.290
I think eight's probably
