03:04:10.180 --> 03:04:14.030
I don't expect you to see
that, but the sad thing

03:04:14.030 --> 03:04:17.300
is there's only data from
about 80% of the students

03:04:17.300 --> 03:04:20.670
because 20% of them used
the wrong units for energy.

03:04:20.670 --> 03:04:23.830
So we couldn't plot that.

03:04:23.830 --> 03:04:27.130
The scatter in the data is
merely a billion to one.

03:04:30.040 --> 03:04:34.840
So somehow, we've missed making
the numbers mean anything.

03:04:34.840 --> 03:04:39.400
The students don't know whether
a 9-volt transistor battery

03:04:39.400 --> 03:04:42.940
could make a butterfly's
wing flap once or carry

03:04:42.940 --> 03:04:45.190
an automobile all the way
across the United States

03:04:45.190 --> 03:04:47.770
or is equivalent to a
thermonuclear device.

03:04:47.770 --> 03:04:49.990
A billion is a big
number, and we're

03:04:49.990 --> 03:04:54.220
giving these folks incredibly
powerful tools to work with.

03:04:54.220 --> 03:04:57.520
And in order for them to do
the ethically proper thing,

03:04:57.520 --> 03:05:00.880
we have to make sure
that they understand.

03:05:00.880 --> 03:05:03.970
Here's another kind
of spooky book.

03:05:03.970 --> 03:05:07.450
I left the image of the tattered
book because it is kind of old.

03:05:07.450 --> 03:05:10.030
It's about 1960 or
something like that.

03:05:10.030 --> 03:05:13.960
This guy named Perry was with
a working group at Harvard.

03:05:13.960 --> 03:05:16.380
Probably a lot of you
know about the work.

03:05:16.380 --> 03:05:19.750
He did some studies about
intellectual and ethical

03:05:19.750 --> 03:05:22.180
development in the
college years, a scheme.

03:05:22.180 --> 03:05:27.630
And my terrible
condensation of what he said

03:05:27.630 --> 03:05:29.700
is that when you
arrive at college,

03:05:29.700 --> 03:05:31.800
you live in a nice
black-and-white dual world,

03:05:31.800 --> 03:05:34.330
and the professor always
knows what's right,

03:05:34.330 --> 03:05:38.000
and homework problems are
either right or wrong.

03:05:38.000 --> 03:05:40.740
And you go through some
real angst and struggle

03:05:40.740 --> 03:05:42.660
because you find out the
boundaries are fuzzy,

03:05:42.660 --> 03:05:44.280
and sometimes you don't
know what's important,

03:05:44.280 --> 03:05:45.488
and it gets a little complex.

03:05:45.488 --> 03:05:48.210
And then you come out
thinking, wow, I'm

03:05:48.210 --> 03:05:51.450
really committed to
complex intellectual ideas,

03:05:51.450 --> 03:05:55.910
and I can handle
complex thoughts.

03:05:55.910 --> 03:05:58.520
Some folks may have
studied graduates

03:05:58.520 --> 03:06:03.710
of technical institutes like
MIT and liberal arts colleges

03:06:03.710 --> 03:06:07.160
some years ago and claimed that
the technical graduates come

03:06:07.160 --> 03:06:10.050
out kind of in the
bottom of the bag,

03:06:10.050 --> 03:06:15.140
and the liberal arts people come
out about halfway up the arrow.

03:06:15.140 --> 03:06:16.220
I think that's--

03:06:16.220 --> 03:06:18.080
I don't buy that.

03:06:18.080 --> 03:06:20.360
I think that that
particular scheme doesn't

03:06:20.360 --> 03:06:23.030
take into account some of the
very fundamental issues that it

03:06:23.030 --> 03:06:26.810
should, but I think there
is a signal in that noise.

03:06:26.810 --> 03:06:28.940
And I think we need to
pay attention to it.

03:06:28.940 --> 03:06:32.480
In fact, I think we're
obligated to work really

03:06:32.480 --> 03:06:37.340
hard to get better at using
hard fun and other things to get

03:06:37.340 --> 03:06:41.010
across the complex nature
of the whole thing.

03:06:41.010 --> 03:06:44.880
Now, another thing that
I think is very important

03:06:44.880 --> 03:06:47.040
is pointed out by our
colleague Mike Dertouzos

03:06:47.040 --> 03:06:49.770
in his new book What Will Be.

03:06:49.770 --> 03:06:51.630
He talks about information
work, and there

03:06:51.630 --> 03:06:56.070
are lots of authors
like Eli Noam

03:06:56.070 --> 03:07:02.890
and the head of Xerox
Park in here in the paper

03:07:02.890 --> 03:07:07.430
"Universities in the Digital
World," John Seely Brown.

03:07:07.430 --> 03:07:09.497
A lot of smart people
are pointing out

03:07:09.497 --> 03:07:11.080
that things are
changing in the world,

03:07:11.080 --> 03:07:13.660
and they have a potential
big impact on the university,

03:07:13.660 --> 03:07:15.390
and I think that's really true.

03:07:15.390 --> 03:07:16.990
And I think we have
to be very careful

03:07:16.990 --> 03:07:19.270
and make sure that we
make that an asset rather

03:07:19.270 --> 03:07:20.740
than a liability.

03:07:20.740 --> 03:07:24.209
But that's the subject
of another talk.

03:07:24.209 --> 03:07:25.750
One of the things
that is very clear,

03:07:25.750 --> 03:07:28.330
though, is that we
have to understand

03:07:28.330 --> 03:07:32.810
what the core benefit
of a place like MIT is.

03:07:32.810 --> 03:07:34.730
And I happen to
think that it has

03:07:34.730 --> 03:07:37.580
to do with making
sure that we address

03:07:37.580 --> 03:07:41.040
the really high-margin things.

03:07:41.040 --> 03:07:45.240
Informed creative thinking
is, I think, where it is,

03:07:45.240 --> 03:07:48.570
and I think lots of
people agree with that.

03:07:48.570 --> 03:07:54.780
I think we get at that by giving
them the right mix of thinking,

03:07:54.780 --> 03:07:59.070
doing, sharing, and our incoming
chairman of the faculty,

03:07:59.070 --> 03:08:01.680
Lotte Bailyn, just heard
me say this the other day

03:08:01.680 --> 03:08:04.230
and pointed out that I was
leaving out reflecting,

03:08:04.230 --> 03:08:06.180
and I think she's
absolutely right

03:08:06.180 --> 03:08:10.010
that that's a very important
part of the formula.

03:08:10.010 --> 03:08:17.030
Now, what that means to me is
that being here for four years

03:08:17.030 --> 03:08:23.390
or longer primarily derives
value from the community.

03:08:23.390 --> 03:08:27.050
You're with a whole group of
people who are raising the bar,

03:08:27.050 --> 03:08:30.290
helping you develop a new
self-image of yourself

03:08:30.290 --> 03:08:33.380
so that you have
greater self-expectation

03:08:33.380 --> 03:08:35.600
and the assumption that
you're going to do something

03:08:35.600 --> 03:08:37.640
when you leave here.

03:08:37.640 --> 03:08:42.170
Now, what that
means, I think, is

03:08:42.170 --> 03:08:47.060
that Nerd Pride needs
to continue to blossom

03:08:47.060 --> 03:08:53.020
and have some other
obvious dimensions.

03:08:53.020 --> 03:08:54.690
Life is not a
homework assignment.

03:08:54.690 --> 03:08:56.650
There's a lot more
to it than that.

03:08:56.650 --> 03:09:00.400
And if you start looking, and
if we were really successful,

03:09:00.400 --> 03:09:03.580
we would get students to think
about what my colleague Don

03:09:03.580 --> 03:09:06.670
Schon argues in The
Reflective Practitioner--

03:09:06.670 --> 03:09:09.580
that there isn't even a
right way to do things.

03:09:09.580 --> 03:09:13.850
If you look at the reflective
practitioners in architecture

03:09:13.850 --> 03:09:16.130
or medicine or
engineering, they all

03:09:16.130 --> 03:09:19.230
do things in different ways, and
there's no right or wrong way.

03:09:19.230 --> 03:09:21.800
And you need to understand and
develop your own philosophy

03:09:21.800 --> 03:09:23.550
about how you're
going to do things.

03:09:23.550 --> 03:09:26.840
In fact, if you go
even further out

03:09:26.840 --> 03:09:30.140
and listen to
people like Capra--

03:09:30.140 --> 03:09:33.910
the way of physics,
The Tao of Physics--

03:09:33.910 --> 03:09:37.010
there is a great oneness.

03:09:37.010 --> 03:09:42.880
Maybe it is that modern
physics and Eastern mysticism

03:09:42.880 --> 03:09:44.620
are all the same.

03:09:44.620 --> 03:09:46.780
I don't quite go
that far, but I think

03:09:46.780 --> 03:09:51.400
this book makes some very
powerful points about how maybe

03:09:51.400 --> 03:09:54.670
we shouldn't draw lines like
I did earlier in this talk

03:09:54.670 --> 03:09:58.030
about learning, that separate
learning about yourself

03:09:58.030 --> 03:10:02.870
from learning about others, from
learning about Mother Nature.

03:10:02.870 --> 03:10:05.810
It's clear, though, that
these sorts of things

03:10:05.810 --> 03:10:09.290
are really becoming much
more important and much more

03:10:09.290 --> 03:10:11.300
integral to the
whole thing, and I

03:10:11.300 --> 03:10:14.120
think it is absolutely clear,
also, that the new Cold

03:10:14.120 --> 03:10:17.140
War is education.

03:10:17.140 --> 03:10:20.060
There is nothing
more closely coupled

03:10:20.060 --> 03:10:24.750
to either our standard of
living or our quality of life

03:10:24.750 --> 03:10:27.720
than the success or failure
of our education system.

03:10:27.720 --> 03:10:31.310
And I think we need a
Cold War on the same scale

03:10:31.310 --> 03:10:33.410
as the last one.

03:10:33.410 --> 03:10:35.270
Now, one of the
problems that we face

03:10:35.270 --> 03:10:37.850
is this one, that
societies get the best

03:10:37.850 --> 03:10:41.730
of what they celebrate.

03:10:41.730 --> 03:10:46.370
That's really frustrating
to me in some sense.

03:10:46.370 --> 03:10:52.030
A multi-billion dollar
engineering feat,

03:10:52.030 --> 03:10:56.960
and it was named after a guy
who can hit a rock with a stick.

03:10:56.960 --> 03:10:58.130
I like sports.

03:10:58.130 --> 03:10:58.940
I do sports.

03:10:58.940 --> 03:11:01.110
I participate in lots of sports.

03:11:01.110 --> 03:11:03.930
But what were we doing?

03:11:03.930 --> 03:11:08.190
Where were we celebrating
the evolutionary scale?

03:11:08.190 --> 03:11:09.800
I understand that
Ted Williams was not

03:11:09.800 --> 03:11:12.800
even nice to the
baseball fans in Boston.

03:11:12.800 --> 03:11:15.050
There were literally thousands
of people in the Boston

03:11:15.050 --> 03:11:16.670
area in the last
few decades who have

03:11:16.670 --> 03:11:20.600
made really meaningful
contributions to society,

03:11:20.600 --> 03:11:22.910
but we took this big
thing and did that.

03:11:22.910 --> 03:11:25.160
So with that slide up,
I'd like to show you

03:11:25.160 --> 03:11:28.190
a clip from coverage of last
year's FIRST, where you see

03:11:28.190 --> 03:11:33.237
a young woman named Sarina Kay
making 19 seconds of comments,

03:11:33.237 --> 03:11:34.070
and then I'll close.

03:11:36.920 --> 03:11:39.360
KAY: Three years ago, if I
would've called my dad at 9:00

03:11:39.360 --> 03:11:40.910
at night and said, Dad,
I'm at East Systems

03:11:40.910 --> 03:11:43.368
working on the robot, he would
have been like, yeah, right.

03:11:43.368 --> 03:11:44.180
Where are you?

03:11:44.180 --> 03:11:46.430
He wouldn't believed me, and
at the beginning of this,

03:11:46.430 --> 03:11:47.750
they didn't believe me.

03:11:47.750 --> 03:11:50.900
I started realizing how
important these years are

03:11:50.900 --> 03:11:52.400
for my later years--

03:11:52.400 --> 03:11:53.990
that I need to work
hard now, and I

03:11:53.990 --> 03:11:57.990
need to get my life on track
if I want to do anything.

03:11:57.990 --> 03:12:01.130
- And that's the true
spirit of US FIRST.

03:12:01.130 --> 03:12:04.379
KAY: I changed a lot more
than it appeared I did,

03:12:04.379 --> 03:12:05.420
but I grew up a lot more.

03:12:05.420 --> 03:12:07.340
And I think my parents
have realized that.

03:12:07.340 --> 03:12:09.740
They trust me a lot more.

03:12:09.740 --> 03:12:12.650
- Sarina Kay-- just one of
the many US FIRST stories

03:12:12.650 --> 03:12:14.441
we've heard here today.

03:12:14.441 --> 03:12:15.440
Now for the results of--

03:12:15.440 --> 03:12:23.890
FLOWERS: OK, my closing slide
comes from my favorite poet,

03:12:23.890 --> 03:12:26.990
the Sufi mystic poet Rumi.

03:12:26.990 --> 03:12:30.690
And I think we at MIT and all
of you guys have to do this.

03:12:30.690 --> 03:12:34.550
We have to make sure that we've
got the right big picture,

03:12:34.550 --> 03:12:37.890
but we've got to get all
of our ducks in a row

03:12:37.890 --> 03:12:39.470
while we're working that out.

03:12:39.470 --> 03:12:41.430
And we've got some
immediate things.

03:12:41.430 --> 03:12:43.830
We've got to have many
layers of horizons,

03:12:43.830 --> 03:12:45.670
and we've got to get
them all in order.

03:12:45.670 --> 03:12:49.470
Now, thank you very much.

03:12:51.980 --> 03:12:58.050
I would like now to turn the
program over to Craig Ramsell,

03:12:58.050 --> 03:13:01.440
and Boomwhacker's Rule
will become obvious.

03:13:01.440 --> 03:13:04.905
[APPLAUSE]

03:13:16.785 --> 03:13:18.270
RAMSELL: Hello, hello, hello.

03:13:22.750 --> 03:13:27.190
I think most of you have no
clue what Boomwhackers are,

03:13:27.190 --> 03:13:30.475
and I'm going to start by
telling you that neither do I,

03:13:30.475 --> 03:13:32.710
bu I'm going to do
my best with them.

03:13:32.710 --> 03:13:34.845
And I actually understand
that I'm out of time,

03:13:34.845 --> 03:13:36.220
so I think what's
going to happen

03:13:36.220 --> 03:13:38.050
is you're going to take
a Boomwhacker home.

03:13:38.050 --> 03:13:38.900
Just kidding.

03:13:38.900 --> 03:13:41.720
We're going to actually do
something with them here.

03:13:41.720 --> 03:13:44.290
It sounds like some of you
are already doing something

03:13:44.290 --> 03:13:47.680
with them here, but before
I get too far into that,

03:13:47.680 --> 03:13:51.100
let me thank Beth Garvin
and Woodie Flowers

03:13:51.100 --> 03:13:57.610
for having the vision to get
you all participating today.

03:13:57.610 --> 03:14:00.130
And we're done
listening for a while,

03:14:00.130 --> 03:14:03.460
and we're going to do
some participating.

03:14:03.460 --> 03:14:05.020
I would also like
to say that when

03:14:05.020 --> 03:14:07.780
we get everybody going in here,
it could get a little loud.

03:14:07.780 --> 03:14:10.870
So maybe, if those
of you that have

03:14:10.870 --> 03:14:13.360
any kind of hearing-assistive
devices going on

03:14:13.360 --> 03:14:17.350
may need to take
them down a notch.

03:14:17.350 --> 03:14:17.950
What else?

03:14:17.950 --> 03:14:20.320
I would also like
to acknowledge a man

03:14:20.320 --> 03:14:24.100
by the name of Arthur Hull,
who has been my biggest

03:14:24.100 --> 03:14:26.950
customer, biggest fan, who
does these kinds of events

03:14:26.950 --> 03:14:29.410
all the time for
corporate clients

03:14:29.410 --> 03:14:31.780
as a team-building exercise.

03:14:34.330 --> 03:14:34.990
What a gig.

03:14:34.990 --> 03:14:35.781
I mean, what a gig.

03:14:35.781 --> 03:14:37.840
He just flies around and
does this for people,

03:14:37.840 --> 03:14:39.131
and they're having a good time.

03:14:39.131 --> 03:14:42.340
Now, we have an
excellent assembly

03:14:42.340 --> 03:14:45.850
of people who have rehearsed
all of about two minutes

03:14:45.850 --> 03:14:47.290
with these.

03:14:47.290 --> 03:14:50.680
And I promised Woodie that I
usually just completely wing

03:14:50.680 --> 03:14:51.310
these things.

03:14:51.310 --> 03:14:52.754
But this time we're
actually doing

03:14:52.754 --> 03:14:53.920
a couple of rehearsed songs.

03:14:53.920 --> 03:14:56.470
So we'll get into that.

03:14:56.470 --> 03:14:59.717
The first thing I want you to do
when you get your tube in hand,

03:14:59.717 --> 03:15:02.050
and it looks like they're
getting out there pretty fast.

03:15:02.050 --> 03:15:03.850
Make sure you all have one--

03:15:03.850 --> 03:15:05.860
preferably just one.

03:15:05.860 --> 03:15:07.810
You'll be much
less confused-- not

03:15:07.810 --> 03:15:11.330
that MIT grads can't handle it.

03:15:11.330 --> 03:15:14.162
But we'll just
find out, won't we?

03:15:14.162 --> 03:15:17.620
I do want to tell a
quick couple of anecdotes

03:15:17.620 --> 03:15:20.230
while you're putting these out.

03:15:20.230 --> 03:15:22.000
I stayed in the dorm
for the first time.

03:15:22.000 --> 03:15:24.010
I was in Theta Delta Chi
fraternity when I was here,

03:15:24.010 --> 03:15:25.760
and I stayed in a dorm
for the first time,

03:15:25.760 --> 03:15:28.450
and there was this wonderful
communal bathroom experience.

03:15:28.450 --> 03:15:30.760
And you go in, and you sit
on the toilet on one side,

03:15:30.760 --> 03:15:33.324
and it's got this kind
of a seesaw effect.

03:15:33.324 --> 03:15:34.990
And somebody sits
down on the other one,

03:15:34.990 --> 03:15:37.840
and you think you're going to
launch out James Bond-style out

03:15:37.840 --> 03:15:39.880
of your Aston Martin.

03:15:39.880 --> 03:15:44.920
But so far, we're surviving.

03:15:44.920 --> 03:15:50.350
Boomwhackers-- I've been asked
where did these come from,

03:15:50.350 --> 03:15:52.960
and I usually respond
with well, where

03:15:52.960 --> 03:15:55.550
do ideas come from, anyway?

03:15:55.550 --> 03:15:59.020
And whatever you want to call
that source of intelligence

03:15:59.020 --> 03:16:03.040
that we are all fortunate
enough to tap into now and then.

03:16:03.040 --> 03:16:05.590
But there was a
literal inspiration,

03:16:05.590 --> 03:16:09.190
and it was a cardboard tube
from a roll of gift wrap paper,

03:16:09.190 --> 03:16:12.580
and I was living somewhere where
they were recycling cardboard,

03:16:12.580 --> 03:16:16.490
but that had to be a
two by two foot square,

03:16:16.490 --> 03:16:18.680
and this thing was three
feet long, and oh jeez,

03:16:18.680 --> 03:16:19.970
it's going to be sticking
out, and these guys

03:16:19.970 --> 03:16:21.200
are going to leave it
sitting on the curb

03:16:21.200 --> 03:16:23.090
because I'm not
following directions.

03:16:23.090 --> 03:16:26.570
I cut it in two, and
then the magic came.

03:16:26.570 --> 03:16:30.110
I was not a drummer, still am
not a drummer, but I'm trying.

03:16:30.110 --> 03:16:33.960
And I went do do do
do do, like this.

03:16:33.960 --> 03:16:36.105
And I go, wow, that
sounds pretty good.

03:16:36.105 --> 03:16:38.990
It's cardboard tubes, and
the tones are different.

03:16:38.990 --> 03:16:41.207
I go, hmm.

03:16:41.207 --> 03:16:42.540
One's longer than the other one.

03:16:42.540 --> 03:16:43.860
I didn't cut it exactly in half.

03:16:43.860 --> 03:16:45.690
I think this is like a
musical principle that's

03:16:45.690 --> 03:16:46.760
been around for a while.

03:16:46.760 --> 03:16:49.380
[LAUGHTER]

03:16:49.380 --> 03:16:53.580
So I immediately beat
the tubes up to the point

03:16:53.580 --> 03:16:56.430
where you can hardly hear them
do anything anymore and ran

03:16:56.430 --> 03:16:58.650
across the street to
the neighbor and said,

03:16:58.650 --> 03:17:00.840
I've got a great idea for
a new musical instrument.

03:17:00.840 --> 03:17:01.800
Watch!

03:17:01.800 --> 03:17:02.730
Do do do do do do.

03:17:02.730 --> 03:17:04.650
And they go, oh, Craig.

03:17:04.650 --> 03:17:06.990
I mean, you've been having
some health problems,

03:17:06.990 --> 03:17:07.800
and we're really--

03:17:07.800 --> 03:17:09.900
but we're really
worried about you.

03:17:09.900 --> 03:17:13.290
Please don't just jump at the
first thing that comes to mind.

03:17:13.290 --> 03:17:14.230
I ignored them.

03:17:14.230 --> 03:17:16.680
I did, and now we
have Boomwhackers.

03:17:16.680 --> 03:17:20.730
So you've been extremely,
extremely quiet with these.

03:17:20.730 --> 03:17:22.770
The last time this
many were handed out,

03:17:22.770 --> 03:17:25.210
you wouldn't have
been able to hear me.

03:17:25.210 --> 03:17:27.210
So thank you for that.

03:17:27.210 --> 03:17:31.537
The first thing I want you to
do is to tune your instrument.

03:17:31.537 --> 03:17:32.370
Now you're laughing.

03:17:32.370 --> 03:17:34.840
I know-- it only
produces one note.

03:17:34.840 --> 03:17:37.440
But if they get a
little bit out of shape,

03:17:37.440 --> 03:17:39.810
if they look at the
end, if it's oval,

03:17:39.810 --> 03:17:43.437
you want to kind of smoosh it
down and make it round again.

03:17:43.437 --> 03:17:45.270
And you're going to
find out that it becomes

03:17:45.270 --> 03:17:47.010
much more sonorous that way.

03:17:47.010 --> 03:17:50.100
[LOW ROAR OF BOOMWHACKERS]

03:17:50.100 --> 03:17:51.820
And I will tell you
two more things.

03:17:51.820 --> 03:17:55.440
One is I looked at
putting LEDs on here,

03:17:55.440 --> 03:18:00.040
but it didn't pass the
cost-benefit analysis.

03:18:00.040 --> 03:18:03.770
And they were
designed with Pod-POM.

03:18:03.770 --> 03:18:04.710
Thank you, Steven.

03:18:08.470 --> 03:18:09.910
All right.

03:18:09.910 --> 03:18:11.140
OK, let it rip.

03:18:11.140 --> 03:18:15.310
This is rumble,
or cutting loose.

03:18:15.310 --> 03:18:16.500
I'll give you some signals.

03:18:16.500 --> 03:18:18.970
This is taking the volume up.

03:18:18.970 --> 03:18:21.300
This is bringing
the volume down.

03:18:21.300 --> 03:18:22.680
Quieter, quieter.

03:18:22.680 --> 03:18:23.440
Take it down.

03:18:23.440 --> 03:18:25.180
Back up again, back up again.

03:18:25.180 --> 03:18:27.180
We're going to go up again.

03:18:27.180 --> 03:18:29.200
All right.

03:18:29.200 --> 03:18:33.400
I'm going to give you a count
of four, and on one, keep going.

03:18:33.400 --> 03:18:34.340
Keep going.

03:18:34.340 --> 03:18:35.923
I'm going to give
you a count of four.

03:18:35.923 --> 03:18:38.350
On one, everybody
is going to stop.

03:18:38.350 --> 03:18:40.930
I'm going to go like that, OK?

03:18:40.930 --> 03:18:42.300
You too.

03:18:42.300 --> 03:18:44.270
Welcome.

03:18:44.270 --> 03:18:44.960
OK.

03:18:44.960 --> 03:18:48.640
One, two, three, four, boom!

03:18:48.640 --> 03:18:49.904
Ah.

03:18:49.904 --> 03:18:51.372
[LAUGHTER]

03:18:51.372 --> 03:18:51.872
All right.

03:18:51.872 --> 03:18:52.848
Let's try that again.

03:18:52.848 --> 03:18:53.348
Rumble.

03:18:53.348 --> 03:19:01.730
[LOW ROAR OF BOOMWHACKERS]

03:19:01.730 --> 03:19:05.030
One, two, three, four, boom!

03:19:05.030 --> 03:19:05.943
Better.

03:19:05.943 --> 03:19:08.181
[LAUGHTER]

03:19:08.181 --> 03:19:08.680
All right.

03:19:08.680 --> 03:19:10.330
Let's do a song.

03:19:10.330 --> 03:19:13.300
This-- and again, this is
where I would normally wing it.

03:19:13.300 --> 03:19:19.580
But to accommodate Woodie here,
I've actually mapped this out.

03:19:19.580 --> 03:19:22.680
And it might help with
the rehearsal, too.

03:19:22.680 --> 03:19:25.924
OK, we're going to go.

03:19:25.924 --> 03:19:26.590
I need my tubes.

03:19:26.590 --> 03:19:27.520
Where are my tubes?

03:19:27.520 --> 03:19:28.519
You guys got your tubes.

03:19:30.739 --> 03:19:31.530
Let's get them out.

03:19:34.370 --> 03:19:35.300
OK.

03:19:35.300 --> 03:19:36.920
Thank you.

03:19:36.920 --> 03:19:38.930
You want to come
up here and play?

03:19:38.930 --> 03:19:41.030
I can use some help.

03:19:41.030 --> 03:19:41.650
All right.

03:19:41.650 --> 03:19:42.391
Now, here we go.

03:19:42.391 --> 03:19:42.890
All right.

03:19:42.890 --> 03:19:47.260
This is called Tech
Tool, and it goes like--

03:19:47.260 --> 03:19:48.540
[TUBES STRIKING HEAD]

03:19:48.540 --> 03:19:49.730
Now, I'm sorry.

03:19:49.730 --> 03:19:50.540
Hang on.

03:19:50.540 --> 03:19:52.820
Everybody has got your tone.

03:19:52.820 --> 03:19:54.499
You do what I do.

03:19:54.499 --> 03:19:56.540
If everything goes well,
these guys do what I do.

03:19:56.540 --> 03:19:58.100
And then you can do what
they do because I'm going

03:19:58.100 --> 03:19:59.550
to move on to something else.

03:19:59.550 --> 03:20:02.540
So you follow your person
onstage, all right?

03:20:02.540 --> 03:20:04.147
So here we go.

03:20:04.147 --> 03:20:06.230
And you can hit the palm
of your hand if you want.

03:20:06.230 --> 03:20:08.570
The head starts to
hurt after a while.

03:20:08.570 --> 03:20:10.980
Thighs, back of the
chair in front of you,

03:20:10.980 --> 03:20:13.310
as long as somebody
else isn't going to get

03:20:13.310 --> 03:20:15.300
whacked when you do it.

03:20:15.300 --> 03:20:15.800
All right.

03:20:15.800 --> 03:20:18.590
Let's keep that going.

03:20:18.590 --> 03:20:19.430
What am I doing?

03:20:19.430 --> 03:20:24.110
I don't know what I'm doing, How
can we have an extra-short rim?

03:20:24.110 --> 03:20:27.870
Have I got a purple?

03:20:27.870 --> 03:20:30.230
OK.

03:20:30.230 --> 03:20:32.100
The colors are musical tones.

03:20:32.100 --> 03:20:33.640
They think you need
an explanation.

03:20:33.640 --> 03:20:35.730
I'm giving you an explanation.

03:20:35.730 --> 03:20:41.140
Long red is a C. Orange is a
D. Yellow is an E. Green, teal,

03:20:41.140 --> 03:20:42.720
whatever--

03:20:42.720 --> 03:20:45.810
G. Purple-- A. Little red--

03:20:45.810 --> 03:20:51.270
octave on the C, high
C. Am I missing a tone?

03:20:51.270 --> 03:20:54.380
I don't have my purple.

03:20:54.380 --> 03:20:54.980
There's green.

03:20:54.980 --> 03:20:57.330
I got two greens.

03:20:57.330 --> 03:20:58.350
How bizarre.

03:20:58.350 --> 03:21:00.450
No purple.

03:21:00.450 --> 03:21:02.760
This is very strange.

03:21:02.760 --> 03:21:03.260
Oh no, no.

03:21:03.260 --> 03:21:03.560
I'm sorry.

03:21:03.560 --> 03:21:04.060
Here it is.

03:21:04.060 --> 03:21:04.830
OK, now we got it.

03:21:04.830 --> 03:21:06.650
Thank you, Woodie.

03:21:06.650 --> 03:21:08.070
You guys are doing great.

03:21:08.070 --> 03:21:09.580
I think you got it down.

03:21:09.580 --> 03:21:10.450
Let's leave you do.

03:21:10.450 --> 03:21:14.576
All right, we're going
to do purple and orange.

03:21:14.576 --> 03:21:16.950
This is so much easier when
you're not trying to plan it.

03:21:16.950 --> 03:21:18.118
OK.

03:21:18.118 --> 03:21:22.760
[MELODIC STRIKING OF TUBES]

03:21:22.760 --> 03:21:27.325
Purple, orange, purple, orange.

03:21:27.325 --> 03:21:33.000
Short red, yellow,
and short red.

03:21:33.000 --> 03:21:34.675
Yellow, short red.

03:21:34.675 --> 03:21:38.555
[MELODIC STRIKING OF TUBES]

03:21:46.330 --> 03:21:47.230
Sounds good to me.

03:21:51.190 --> 03:21:54.243
OK, this Is my secret weapon.

03:21:54.243 --> 03:21:58.171
[MELODIC STRIKING OF TUBES]

03:22:09.970 --> 03:22:12.100
OK, one more signal here.

03:22:12.100 --> 03:22:14.800
This is going to be
the speed it up signal.

03:22:14.800 --> 03:22:16.201
So we're going to do like this.

03:22:16.201 --> 03:22:19.638
[MELODIC STRIKING OF TUBES]

03:22:35.806 --> 03:22:36.430
OK, take it up.

03:22:36.430 --> 03:22:38.710
Take it up.

03:22:38.710 --> 03:22:41.460
If you haven't destroyed
your hands yet.

03:22:41.460 --> 03:22:42.620
All right, take them down.

03:22:42.620 --> 03:22:43.120
All right.

03:22:43.120 --> 03:22:47.180
Bring them down.

03:22:47.180 --> 03:22:49.580
Back up.

03:22:49.580 --> 03:22:54.860
[MELODIC STRIKING OF TUBES]

03:22:54.860 --> 03:22:57.931
One, two, three, four, boom!

03:22:57.931 --> 03:22:59.422
Hey!

03:22:59.422 --> 03:23:03.398
[CHEERING]

03:23:03.398 --> 03:23:04.392
Congratulations.

03:23:08.880 --> 03:23:10.350
We'll do one more
quick thing here.

03:23:10.350 --> 03:23:13.380
I want to just get a sound
of just the individual tones.

03:23:13.380 --> 03:23:17.130
So let's start from the
long one here, the red,

03:23:17.130 --> 03:23:18.300
and work our way up.

03:23:18.300 --> 03:23:19.050
Red.

03:23:19.050 --> 03:23:20.850
Let's hear a long red.

03:23:20.850 --> 03:23:23.000
OK.

03:23:23.000 --> 03:23:24.400
Orange.

03:23:24.400 --> 03:23:26.360
Orange up, red down, red out.

03:23:26.360 --> 03:23:27.980
Orange.

03:23:27.980 --> 03:23:29.450
Orange down, yellow up.

03:23:32.160 --> 03:23:33.390
Yellow down, green up.

03:23:36.040 --> 03:23:37.570
Green down, purple up.

03:23:40.580 --> 03:23:44.110
Purple down, short red up.

03:23:44.110 --> 03:23:45.070
There you have it.

03:23:45.070 --> 03:23:49.228
Pentatonic scale, major
pentatonic scale, key of C.

03:23:49.228 --> 03:23:52.574
[APPLAUSE]

03:23:54.486 --> 03:23:55.701
FLOWERS: Thank you.

03:23:55.701 --> 03:23:56.200
All right.

03:23:56.200 --> 03:24:01.100
RAMSELL: Let me-- there's
a couple closing things.

03:24:01.100 --> 03:24:02.920
There was just a
couple of things.

03:24:02.920 --> 03:24:04.390
I'm not sure what you
were instructed to say,

03:24:04.390 --> 03:24:05.098
as opposed to me.

03:24:05.098 --> 03:24:09.430
So what I'd like to do
is say, my understanding

03:24:09.430 --> 03:24:12.250
is you're welcome
take your tubes home.

03:24:12.250 --> 03:24:13.920
And if you decide
you'd rather not,

03:24:13.920 --> 03:24:15.130
there is going to be
a place where they're

03:24:15.130 --> 03:24:16.390
collected, correct, Beth?

03:24:16.390 --> 03:24:18.370
Out here somewhere,
and they'll get off

03:24:18.370 --> 03:24:23.990
to someone deserving, which
